      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Royal Gaieties.—June will be a month of Royal festivities, and the Queen's return, after her long absence abroad, will synchronize with a very busy time at Buckingham Palace, and also at Windsor Castle. The two remaining Courts to be held will be the most important of the season, and the visit of the King of Spain will be the occasion of several State and gala entertainments. flis visit, during which be will stay at Birkingham Palace, will extend overflve days, and after the King's departure, the Court will remove on June 10th to Windsor Castle. The great event there, will be the Rcyal wedding, which is to take place in St. George's Chapel on Thursday, June I.sth. Among other Royal visitors wbo are to be entertained at Windsor, will be Prince and Princess Arisugawa, who are representing the Japanese Imperial Family at the wedding of the German Crown Prince. Their Majesties will entertain a succ es- Lion of guests during the stay of the Court at Windsor, and will have a large house party from Monday the 19t1 until Saturday, the 24th, for Ascot Races. Princess Margarets' Wedding.—The Duke and Duchess of Connaught were married in St. George's Windsor Castle, and that is principally the reason why Princess Margaret preferree to have her wedding there instead of in London. She will be attended by four bridesmaids, her sister Princess Patricia of Connaught, and her cousins Princess Beatrice of Saxe Coburg, Princess Ena of Battenberg, and little Princess Mary of Wales who will act as train bearer. The bride's wedding gown will be entirely of lace, chiefly from Ireland end Buckinghamshire, and the trousseau—which tween the 'tabs, and drawn in at the waist is being largely supplied by British and Irish firms, will beneath the smart belt of soft pink ribbon, shot be shown to a favoured few at Clarence House. The with pale green. Sleeves play an important bridesmaids are to appear in simple gowns of soft blue part in the construction of the blouse of tho satin, and the shamrock and marguerite will be con- moment, and those shewn in the sketch give a spicuous in the ornamentation of frocks and hats., distinctive note to this blouse. Theyare arranged Princess Margaret and her Task.—Like most members in two puffs, divided by a band of insertion, of the Royal Family, Princess Margaret has the gift of with an upward-turned tab, into which the ful- tongues, and she has made great progress in acquiring 'less of the top puff is gathered. Long lace the language of her future country. Unfortunately for cuffs finish the sleeve from elbow to wrist. her, Swedish is net usually included in the repertory' GOSSIP. of foreign languages, which constitute an important part in the education of princes and princesses, but Princess Margaret of Connaught has a taste for Princess Margaret is clever and industrious, and is geology. A lady who knows the Princess well rapidly overcoming the task she has set herself. Some says that her large collection of minerals has been people seem to be born linguists, end to them the . yli arranged by liereelf "Anyone who has .y . mastery of a foreign language is as easy as to others it caret watched tne Princess in an eager hunt for specimens, is difficult. There are many well-known women in or wielding her hammer with zeal and precision, Society who write and speak perfectly four, six, eight, or even ten foreign languages whilst there are others, could see at once that it was a much-loved pursuit." apparently equally cleveranddistinguished,but to whom As a matter of fact, declares the Standard, the the gift of tongues is denied, clever and distinguished women who meet men on The Crown Prince's Wedding.—The wedding festivities equal terms in -the World of Letters, Art, and of the Crown Prince of Germany and the Duchess Science are, as a rule, either indifferent to the Cecilia commence on Saturday. .June 3rd, and will take franchise or contemptuous of it. ' sevelel days fo get through. On such occasions as the „ you s. wish to see women thoroughly insulted, marriage of a Crown Prince, many of the old medieval go to t e papers written for women by women. else, asks the Daily Telegraph. would anyone customs are followed still, and altogether the proces- e sloes, banquets, balls, receptions, and various ceremonials dare to imply that woman was sufficiently idiotic must be something of an ordeal for the young bride. On the Saturday the Duchess Cecilie will make her 'to believe that marriage "of itself alone" state entry into the capital, proceeding first to the guaranteed anything? Schloss Bellevue, in the Thiergartere and from thence The heroine of the average -male novelist, says a to the Imperial Palace. During the latter part of the lady writer in the Globe, is intensely irritating to journey, which is about three miles long, the betrothed the ordinary female reader—she is generally a cat, pair will drive in a very gorgeous State coach, and to often underbred, and even when her manners and them Berlin will appear as a city of roses end triumphal methods and morals are nominally satisfactory you arches. The first halt will be made in the Pariser Plats, are left with the firm conviction that, if she when the city officials will present addresses, and young happened to be on your visit lug list, you would girls will sing a song of welcome, and strew roses in the find her either dull or disagreeeable, or both path of the bride. At this point the procession is to be joined by an escort of butchers, a curious privilege A BROWN LINEN DRESS. enjoyed by their Guild, who, according to ancient The dress shown in the sketch suggests to our tradition, once rendered signal service to a Princess of minds, river proclivities, and for these frocks the Royal House, in distress. On the Sunday, all the linen in white or colours 'is particularly suitable. Royal and Imperial personages will attend Divine The dress under our present consideration is Service, and there will be a grand family dinner nasty, carried out in brown linen crash. The blouse is The following day deputations bearing the wedding arranged in box-pleats all round, and pouches presents will be received, and on Tuesday the civil and slightly over the deep waist-belt, both at the religious rites will he solemnized, the latter taking I back and the front. It fastens under the centre place in the Palace Chapel. Even that is not the end pleat, which is wider than the others, and is of all the pageantry for at night an Imperial ball will be given at which the Crown Prince and his bride will be present, and join in the ceremonial dances. The Duke of Saxe Coburg.—The marriage of the -young Duke of Saxe Coburg-Gotha, will not take place until some time after he comes of age on the 19th of July next, when be will take the reins of government into his own hands. He has lately been with his fiancee on a visit to his mother the Duchess of Albany at Clare- mont, where Prince and Princess Alexander of Teck were also staying, and the gathering. was a very happy one. The widowed Duchess of Albany naturally likes to have her children with her, and it was a great grief to her when her son assumed the title and succession to the Duchy of Saxe Coburg Gotha. She wished him to be an Englishman, "and now,, she said when that event happened." he is obliged to be a German." The Hatless Craze.—There are already signs that the fashion of going hatless, which has crept in from Arnerica,isto be revived this summer. It is often made a reproach against women, that the feminine hat serves no other purpose than that of ornamentation, and that it is solely the creation of vanity. Yet the fashion of discarding it became very popular at the seaside places last summer, and this was so much the case at one resort, that the Vicar issued a notice requesting that women would not come to his church uncovered. Tho men also have taken up the hatless craze. and on the warm days many may be seen going about their daily occupations bareheaded. There is nothing like hot weather for introducing eccentricities in dress. It has given us sandals, the pneumonia blouse, and the hatless craze and, if we should have a topical summer, we shall no doubt see some remarkable developments Of new ideas and peceliarities of attire. Undesirable Extremes.—The long Louis XVI. coats, with their flowing basques, wide revers, gauntlet cuffs, and hip pockets, are in great favour among tall and short, generously proportioned and slender alike, betas a matter of fact, only the tall, slight woman, with long straight back, end slim waist, should select this particu- lar style. Another mode only suitable for the tall and slender, is the large sleeve, which takes from the height and adds to the breadth. There are always people who endeavour to make themselves conspicuous by running to extremes in Fashion, and these individuals are wear- ing huge sleeve and skirts well stiffened with whalebone: hut the best dressed women prefer moderation in the matter of sleeves, and are relying upon a much flounced and ruched foundation and underskirt for the approved flowing fulness about the feet. Dainty Linen.—ln this luxuriant age, it is scarcely terrorising to be told by a firm which makes bed, table, and house linen its speciality, that silken pillow cases trimmed with costly lace, are greatly used, and that not few wealthy women sleep between silken sheets. The majority of US, however, have to be content with linen, and the good housewife will trust such treasure to no estre but herself. Linen pillowcases with fine lawn frills are the hest, and should always be made at home, as thereby n great saving in post can be effected. Linen sheets, if required for hard every day n-ear, should be simply hemmed, and not hemstitched, as the threads in the drawn work are apt to break, but a monogram should appear in one corner, as this affords a very desirable and dainty little personal touch. The initial or monogram should also appear in one corner of each pillowslip; care being taken to keep them as near the edge as possible, or the embreidery would be liable to hurt or mark the cheek. By the way, Irish sheeting linen is much to be preferred to the Flemish or French kind, which has a curious greyish tinge. all the more apparent when placed side by side with our home product. MADGE.</p>
        <p>adorned with three large pearl buttons. The riquare-cut collar is composed of white broderie Ans/aiße. that material also forming the shaped belt. The V-shaped opening at the neck is filled in with a vest and collar of fine tucked lawn. The sleeves are full to the elbow, and gathered into long cuffs of the embroidery. The pleat on the skirt are arranged to follow those of the blouse, and are stitched down to below the hips, a band of the embroidery being •inserted above the hem.</p>
