      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>RIOT,</head>
        <p>RIOT,</p>
        <p>HALL CAINE (Author of" The Manxman," " 27n, Christian," Etc.)</p>
        <p>' It is six o'clock in the morning in this angle ;cf the bay of Biscay which is called Biarritz. The air is thin and clear, the early sun slimes with a gentle warmth through an atmosphere which has still some memories of the salt of last night's spray, and the sea, often so . wrathful on the rocks of this coast and full of unnumbered wrecks, is rolling on the long reaches of the sandy shore with a subdued and measured roar. In the market-place of the little town the day's work is beginning. The big doors are open and the country people are carrying their produce to the stalls within. They have come in their donkey carts, mule carts, and oxen drays from the Basque province rouud about. Here is a mule care full of green vegetables from some farm at the foot of the Pyrenees. At the hearts of the lettnces the dew is still hiding in beads, and you know they were plucked from the fields this morning, while the sky 'was grey and the air was leaden by a line of women find girls who stooped cloak over the ridges and then rose to look at the crimsoning clouds that came up over the eastward hills. Here is a donkey cart full of fresh eggs, of butter in moist cabbage leaves, and of milk in bright pails. As they are lifted out you can fancy you hear the cackling and lowing in the farmyard, and smell the odour of the cowhouse, and almost taste the sweet breath of the calf that called to its mother from a neighbouring stall. Here, too, are crates of oranges from . Seville, and of lemons from Sielly, and as their hoops are hammered off, and they come blubbering out, fringed with stalks and green leaves, you think you can see the sunny groves where they grew on the breasts of hills surmounted by little white cities, and hear the workers singing from unseen places as they gathered the yellow- ing fruit. And the 'owners of the produce to be sold this morning—stripped to their clean white shirts, with their waist-scarfs of red cotton, and their caps of black velvet—are sweating from every pore of their tawny faces as the, spread their baskets on the stalls, and call to each other and laugh. As seven strikes from the market clock the buyers begin to appear—thrifty housewives in black gowns with alpaca aprons and market baskets on their arms. They traverse the lines of fruit and vegetables, bargain, haggle and buy, While the sellers cry their goods, the mules munch their corn, and the slow-eyed oxen, under their sheepskin coverlet, chew the cud and drop into a doze. At eight o'clock the little city has , begun to smoke for breakfast, and to open its eyes, which are the windows of its shops. Hack- ney carriages are tinkling along the streets with harness studded with bells, motor cars are toot- ing around the corners, the sun is becoming strong, and the sea is beginning to shine. When the clock strikes, .the rush of business in the market is over, the sellers are shutting up their stalls, pulling on their coats, bracing up their donkeys and mules, and preparing to make their own purchases before setting off for home. One of them draws up at a clothier's shop, and buys a thin roll of cloth. It is Irish freize. and you can imagine you hear the shuffling of the wooden loom in the far-off factory in which it was spun. Another draws up at a shoe-shop and buys a cheap pair of Polish-made boots, and you can see in your mind's eye the •little popu- lous towns of the Russian Pale where Jewish shoemakers sit stitching early and late by the open doors of their narrow tenements. A third draws up at a draper's shop and buys a print handkerchief of English manufacture, and you can fancy you hear the roar of the rolling machinery of the great mills of Manchester, and see the long line of girl hands who come laughing and skipping arm-in-arm out of the wide gates as the big bell rings at midday. A fourth stops to give two coppers to a blind woman led by a little girl, and you feel the spell of that sweetest part of charity by which the giver makes himself one in welfare and in- terest with her to whom he gives. All are obey- ing in their different ways the homely but irre- sistible God who governs all men ; whose rule began with creation and can'only end with chaos ; who binds together the farthest parts of the earth, and unites the peoples of the world into one family. They are his willing and cheer- ful subjects, happy to serve him and the better for his service. And they go about with his little counters in their hands—gold counters, silver congers, and bronze counters—symbols of green fields, of forests, of farms, of vineyards, of orange groves, of lemon orchards, of houses, of factories, of forges, of mills, of ships, of everything whereby they eat and drink, and live. That god is the. god of Industry, and his name is Rule.</p>
        <p>_ ..e...ss,tusty wtni the litti red counters which represent five francs, and now she 4is playing boldly with the large white octagonal pieces which stand for five hundred. Her, eves are gleaming. a lanah not yet bead in the air is playing about her parted lips, :4 intervals she looks into the faces around and smiles at strangers. Presently she rises, gathers into a little basket the pile of shining ivory which lies on her section of the table, and car- ries it to the treasury to be changed into coin. She has won, but she has also lost—lost some- thing of that most precious essence of the soul, the essence of 'unselfishness, which tells us it is not good to gain if somebody else must lose. The middle-aged man with a pale face and eyes that stare a little when he is spoken to has been losing all along. His section iihows no counters. He is dipping into his waistcoat poc- kets first for notes, then for gold, finally for silver. When all is gone' he is a ruined man. He has gambled away the home of his wife and the bread of his children, and lie is an offender more guilty than many we put in our gaols. The elderly Mall with the ruddy cheeks, smok- ing a long ofgar from a meerschaum holder, thinks lie at least is no offender. His income is £40,000 sterling, and if he cares to waste some of it in the Casino who shall say that he may not do so? If he fr.tive suppers they would cost him something; if lie kept horses or yachts or women nobody would question his right to do what be liked with his own—why not waste his wealth in this way if he will? • So he loses with a laugh, and wins with an equal countenance, and rises from the table with the easy conscienco of one who has amused himself and done hitrra . to no one. But that. man hits, nevertheless, com- mitted the gravest crime against social moral- ity—he has robbed 'money of its meaning, and has set uj nothing in its stead. He has staked the symbols of all we eat and drink and clothe ourselves with, the symbols of the poor man's toil and sweat from generation to generation, from World's end to world's end, against the turning of a card, the fluttering of a feather, against nothing he can control, nothing he can calculate, nothing he can understand, nothing that exists. Therefore he is a criminal against the oldest and firmest of the laws of life—the law which says : , "In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread." They are all criminals, though they sit here in silk and broadcloth, in the Casino that was built by the municipality and is licensed by the State. For the god they serve is the god of Idleness and his name is Riot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>NO COMPANY WOULD INSURE HER! Indigestion and Debility had Brought Her so near Death.</head>
        <p>NO COMPANY WOULD INSURE HER! Indigestion and Debility had Brought Her so near Death.</p>
        <p>BILE BEANS HAVE CURED HER.</p>
        <p>"A year ago no Insurance Company would grant me a policy, I was so near death; yet you see my present healthy condition! " The speaker was Mrs. E. Scott,of Army Terrace, Newport, Salop, and she was speaking to a "Newport Advertiser" reporter. "I looked and felt like a woman whose days were numbered," she con- tinued. "My illness began with feelings of fulness after meals, followed by belching. Then I began to have pain after food and got so bad that eating caused actual agony. I went to doctor but could not keep on with him, as I could not afford the expense of a long run of doctor's bills. Finding myself getting worse I next tried a number of medicines recommended by friends, but none did me any good. "The indigestion and flatulence were succeeded by fluttering feelings round the heart, which rendered me feeble, and prevented me from sleeping I was often obliged to get out of bed in the night-time and walk about the room till the fluttering subsided. "I was at my worst in this respect, and so paid at the heart, I was obliged to cease my work, when a married sister told Me that Bile Beans had done her husband so much good tney wouldn't be without them the house., She persuaded rue to try them. " In it very little time Bile Beans made a wonderful change in me. I continued taking them, and in the end they cured me completely. lam now well and strong again, and an utter stranger to indigestion or flatul- ence." Bile Beans are a certain cure for all forms of indiges- tion, biliousness, liver, and kidney ailments, constipa- tion, piles, rheumatism. and anzemia. Of all chemists at Is. 11. d. or 2s. 9(1. per box, or post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., Red Cross Street, London E.C., uipo receipt of prices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>IRONWORKERS' WAGES. _ Devising a Sliding Seale.</head>
        <p>IRONWORKERS' WAGES. _ Devising a Sliding Seale.</p>
        <p>The representatives of the employers and the opera- tives are applying thernselyef to 014 difficult problem. of devising a sliding scale for the regulation of wages in the Midland iron trade. The question is one which affects a large number of works and many thousands of operatives not only in the Midlands but in a wide district outside (including Warrington) whore the rate of wages'is determined by the scale of the Midland Iron and Steel Trade Wages Board. About a year ago the operatives put an end to the sliding scale which had existed through a long series of years 11. Its working had confessedly become very unsatisfactory It is twelve o'clock midnight in the 7tlnni- and in practice wages bad latterly been remilated in cipal Casino; the performance of "Boccaccio " correspondence with the mtes paid in the 'North of has just finished in the little blue . and white England, though efforts to co-ordinate the two scales theatre ; the curtain has risen again and fallen and work in union had proved unsuccessful. All parties for the last time on the painted and powdered in the trade aro agreed as to the value of the sliding mimes in grotesque costumes who have made scale principle, and it was understood when the old merry for three hours, and the audience of men arrangement was abandoned that the object in view was in frilled shirts and black cravats, and women to establish a more equitable basis. The operatives' in silvery silks and white feathers, are pouring Executive has now submitted to the various lodges throughout the area a scheme which proposes to give a out of the double doors which open into the more representative character as to the ascertaininent of gaming rooms, n• sellin prices by trebling the number of works from There are two gaming rooms. One of them, which. the prices are taken. It is recommended that the outer one, is soon filled with people who— only the invoiced selling price of actual sales be taken as actors now, ,no longer as audience—are into account. Thirty-three works are enumerated for crowding about a table on which little numbered the ascertainment. Special meetings of the ironworkers' around a sectioned circle. The room rings with later than June sth. be communicated to the Executive not toy horses of various colours are made to spin . organisation are to be convened forthwith, and their , many voices—the voice of the man who cries for replies are to . ._ ; .-,.. , _ • , stakes, the voice of the man who turns the crank, which moves the machinery, the voice of ..</p>
