      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>1905.</head>
        <p>1905.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY EMBEZZLEMENT CASE.</p>
        <p>TWO C.L.C. PORTERS CHARGED WITH STEALING TICKET Mum Committed For Trial.</p>
        <p>Warrington magistrates were on Tuesday engaged for some seven hours hearing charges of embezzlement which I were preferred against two, old and trusted servants of the Cheshire Lines Committee. The two men were Edward Hughes, 45, Winwick-street, who waA charged with embezzling 35. on the 17th April, 4s. Gd. on the Ist May, and 4s. 6d. on the 15th May, and John Hudson, 8, Hamilton-street, who was charged with embezzling 4s. 6d. on the 21th April, and ss. on the Bth May. The charges against Hudson were taken first. , Mr. A. Browne appeared to prosecute, defendants being represented by Mr. Madden (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel). Mr. Browne said that in that case the Cheshire Lines Committee were very reluctant to prosecute on account of the length of service of the defendant, Hudson hay- ing been employed by the Company for 24 years. The confidence which had been placed in him had been such Ithat some years ago he was appointed foreman porter being every other week on night ditty When on night, duty he was given a number of tickets to sell to passengers going to Liverpool on trains which left before the booking office opened. He had also to collect the tickets it being his duty to cancel immediately he had done So. Some few months ago it began to be suspected that there was something wrong as it was found that ' ,1 the number of tickets sold did not comply with the I number of passengers.. It became so apparent that the company was being robbed that the management in- stracted Superintendent Ramage to engage outside detectives in order to see who were the,gttilty persons. The principal object of the prosecution was to clear the whole of the staff of the imputation of dishonesty which had been cast upon it. On the 24th April the private detective attended at the Central Station at 5.30 a.m. and purchased from Hudson three single tickets for Liverpool for which he paid 4s. Gd. He booked the numbers of the tickets and each ticket was marked by means of a pin prick in the P in the word April. Those tickets were actually collected by Hudson. Upon arrival at Liverpool Mr. Williams, the detective handed the cumbers of the tickets to Inspector Hogg at 8.40 the same morning Mr. Williams son. went to the booking office at the Central Station and purchased three single tickets to Liverpool. By an extraordinary coincidence he got the three tickets which had earlier in the morning been issued to his son and which had not been cancelled, it being thus clearly shown that Hudson had pocketed the money. At 5.50 the same morning Hndson handed over the tickets he had not sold to the booking clerk, together with some cash. The clerk could not make the cash and tickets balance, he having received too much, and upon speak- ing to the defendant Hudson said, " I know, I took it out of my pocket for change." On May Bth Mr. Williams, jun., again went to the Central Station at 5.30 and purohnsed three single tickets from the defend- ant. Mr. Williams again took the numbers which were 9237-6-5. These tickets were collected by a station constable. When in the train and while passing over Bewsey Bridge he threw a slip of paper containing the numbers to his father who was standing below in the street. Mr. Williams, sari., went to tha Central Station and watched Hudson. When the de fendant,went into the hooking office he sent for Mr. Davies, the chief booking , clerk,wholon arrival at the station found the tickets bear- ing the numbers thrown to Mr. Williams lying on the thsk waiting to be checked as unsold. At 7.50 the same 'morning Mr. Williams sent a boy named Harry Hogg to ' purchase three tickets for Liverpool and amongst those which were given to him was one which' had been sold to Mr. Williams jun. earlier in the morning. Those two cases were samples of five charges which could be preferred against Hudson. The prosecution was not, however' vindictive, the principal object being to clear innocent persons and to prove who was guilty. Mr. Madden said the question was as to whether the money for the tickets was handed over or not, which left the dittpute between the defendant and other people in the establishment. His client indignantly repudiated the charge and under the circumstances he withdrew from his former position and elected to go for trial to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions. Charles Williams, jun.,Bs, Rice-lane, Egremont, mem- ber of the film of Charles Williams and Son, private detectives,Liverpool,said he was instructed by Inspector Ramage of the C.L.C. to make certain inquiries at War- rington Central Station, on the morning of April 24th he attended at the Central Station, Warrington, accom- panied by James McKenna and Harry Hogg. He pur- chased three third-class single tickets for Liverpool from the defendant Hudson, for which he paid 4s. 6d. he took the numbers down on the slip of paper provided. They were 8781, 8782 and 8783. He retained 8783, gave Hogg 8782, and 8781 to McKenna. He pin-pricked the letter P. in the word April in his ticket. On arrival at Liverpool witness gave the peper containing the num- bers to inspector J.Elogg. On May Bth he again attended at Warrington 'Central Station at 5.30 attended by Miss L. Massey and his sister, Miss L. Williams. Ho again purchased three tickets and booked the numbers. He did not mark the tickets. He entered the 5.35-train handing the tickets up to thri station constable. On passing over the bridge near the station he threw a paper containing the number of tickets to Harry Hogg. On arrival at Liverpool he gave the numbers of the tickets to inspector J. Hogg. On April 10th he was withlhis father at 5.30 and saw him purchase three single tickets for Liverpool from a porter whom he could not identify. Whilst looking at his ticket he saw the porter go np,to Hudson. In answer to Mr. Madden witness said he could not recognise the porter as he only saw hint once. He could identify Hudson because ho saw him many times.</p>
        <p>Jam as McKenua, Liverpool, Harry Ho, Liverpool and other witnesses gave corroborative evidence. Mr. Derbyshire, station master at the Central Station, Warrington, spoke 113 to Hudson's duties. Hudson was given the money for tickets not sold by him for early morning trains and the collected tickets were also handed over to him to be cancelled.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Beddard, Knutsford-road, Warrington, booking clerk in the empty of the C.L.C., at Warrington, stated that on April 24th he commenced duty at 5.30. Defen- dant handed to him six single and eleven return tickets to Liverpool and handed him money which represented thirteen double tickets as basing been sold, and no singles sold. He could not make the tickets and cash agree as Hudson counted out £1 17s. while witness only required 21 12s. Gd. Hudson took 4s. 6d. back. On May Bth defendant gave him four single tickets 12 return tickets arid £1 13s. in cash, which accounted for two single and twelve return tickets as having been sold. Other witnesses gave evidence in support of Mr. Browne's statement. Detective Inspector Hogg, of the C.L.C., said lie saw the defendant with regard to three charges of irregularity. For the first the defendant replied "I cannot account for it." To the charge relating to the 21th April he replied "r know it was not done by me." To the last charge he said "The tickets I sold were paid for and I cannot account for them being re-sold." Defendant, who reserved his defence, was remanded for trial to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions bail being allowed in two sureties of £lO each and himself in £2O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHARGES AGAINST HUGHES.</head>
        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST HUGHES.</p>
        <p>The case against Hughes as then proceedel with. Hughes elected to go for trial to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions. The prosecution in the evidonce alleged that the modes operandi was the same as in the case against Hudson.</p>
        <p>Charles Williams, son., sairLhe attended the Central Station"at 30 A.M. on April 17th, and purchased three single third-class tickets for Liverpool from Mr. Hughes. The tickets were numbered 8,534, 8,535, and 8,538, and Hughes collected the tickets from him. On : May Ist witness brought three third class tickets for I Liverpool, amongst which was one numbered 9,023. He put a pin prick on the "y" of the word May. Chas. Williams. jun.. said on May 15th, he purchased three tickets and took the numbers of them, and dropped a piece or paper containing, the numbers over Bewsey Bridge where someone was in waiting. Harry Hogg said that acting on instructions, he waited outside Bewsey-street until Charles Williams, jun.,threw an envelope to him containing the numbers of the tickets. He took the envelope at once to the house of Mr. Davies. chief booking clerk. Fred Liddle and James iqc Kenna gave evidence to the effect that the former purchased two tickets for Liverpool, and paid 3s. for them. A pin prick was put in each one in the letter" y " in May. Inspector John Radford said that he received instruc- tions from Inspector Hogg to go to Warrington. He found that thl tickets numbered 8,531 and 8,533 weie entered as not having been sold. .</p>
        <p>J. W. Davies sail he proceeded to the station after receiving information from Harry Hogg and SAW tbe tickets with a marked pin prick. They had not been cancelled.</p>
        <p>tv.vi ence as also given by Elijah Cocking. Henry Platt, Charles Owen. George Henry Parker, A. Johnson, P. Darbyshire, and Inspector Hogg. The Liverpool Sessions are to be opened at the Court House, Islington on Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>A powerful sermon preached recently by the vicar of Tunbridee Wells church impressed several members of his congregation so deeply that they have sent him ;jewellery and other valuables, and requested that the proceeds of their sale shall he devoted to Christian work. A family relic of cnnsiclerable value was placed in the offertory bag. From the official "Rules for Sub-Post-masters ":--- "Free medical attendance is allowed in respect of injuries sustained on duty at the 'hands of the depart- ment's medical officer."</p>
        <p>• He : "I was an intimate friend of your late husband. Can't ,you give me something to remember him by ?" She (shy;y) : " flow would I do ? "--" Punch."</p>
