      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. Lactura to tha Warrington " V" Branch</head>
        <p>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. Lactura to tha Warrington " V" Branch</p>
        <p>Formed quite recently, the Warrington" V " branch 1 of the British Women's Tezuperance Association is already doing an active work and promises to become a I very useful organization. There Was a large gathering lof members in Cairo street school on Taesdav evening to hear a lecture on " Chemistry and alcohol" by Mr. C. Spiegelhalter, B.Sc. of the Yorkshire C Wage, weds Miss 13Eswfcm presided, and in introducing the lec- turersaid she hoped the leoture would proves profitable 1 and helpful one, and that they might carry away some- thing useful. She was very glad the branch had been formed, although it was not yet in thorough working order. She hoped the work and meetings would be bright, attractive and helpful to all,aud that they would all try to make the branch larger and do their best for its success. (Applause). The object of the branch was to help their fellow creatures, and to protect others from 1 falling lower. (Applause). The LECTURER commenced with a few observations on the history of chemical .processes, of which the manufacture of alcohol was one. lie pointed out that the first object of chemistry was to ascertain the composition of the various things we saw around us, and also to find out whether such things could be artificially ipide, and if so Iby what method. Proceeding, be applied this test to various articles in common use. A chemical process of ' questionable importance he said, was the manufacture of beer. Huxley said that ansong the earliest records of men we invariably found a time when they got drunk, and so it was that the precess of brewing seemed to have been handed from age to age ftona the vary earliest times. The process was a purely chemical one an which the formation of alcohol played a chief part, Having dealt with the properties of alcohol, the lecturer said its effects upon the human system were well known. It intoxicated when taken in dilute form, while in large doses it was poisonous. It lowered the temperature of the body when taken internally, although the sensation of warmth was experi- enced. Distilling was practiSoi in England by monks as early as the 11th century for medical purposes and it commenced as a trade in the Tudor period. As to the medicinal Mies of alco'.ol, the lecturer pointed out that alcohol itself was a narcotic poison, therefore all alcoholic stimulants were more or lass poisonous in their nature : It was a matter of vital consequence to decide whether stimulants were injurious or beneficial. If they were taken in modera- tion there could be no doubt that they were beneficial, but i f,on the other hand,they were taken to excess,it was not difficult to impress upon any intelligent person that they were highly pernicious. If a person was unable to keep within proper bounds, he should at once make up his mind to become a total abstainer. Indeed he would go further and say that if a person had once exceeded. he should either make a point of conning himself to a limited quantity of drink, or if he was unable to du that, he should resist the temptation of taking it alto- gether. There could be no doubt that stimulants were a great aid in the treatment of certain diseases, especi- ally those which produced great exhaustion, and it MIS beyond doubt that many valuable lives had been saved by-judicious administration of those bear mges. The difficulty in the drink qtiostion Was to draw ;the line, and probably,from amoral point of flew, the teetotaller had the advantage, simply; however, from the fact that over indulgence in alcoholic stimulants did such harm to society at large, producing evils which were to be deprecated by every rig ht-thinking man. There could be no doubt that insanity and crime were largely duo to excessive use of alcoholic liquors. With temperance (not necessarily with total abstinence) a better order of things would undoubtedly prevail. Incidentally Mr. Spiegelhalter pointed out that the abuse of alcohol had a great deal to enswer for in the scientific sense, as the duty imposed on it made it ex- ceedingly expensive to scientists who had to use it in , large quantities for experimenting. purposes. If the drink evil could be removed and the duty done away with, it would be much better for science. The lecture was made additionally interesting by several experi- ments. _</p>
        <p>Mrs. SIMPSON in moving a vote of thanlcs to Mr Spiegelhalter, said the lecture had been quite an educa- tion to them and she hoped the lecturer would speak on the nses and abuses of alcohol at a future meeting of the branch. Scientists did not advocate alcohol as a beverage or a food. The members of that branch, as professing teetotalers, ought to do all they could to try and convince others that they were doing them- selves harm by taking alcohol in any form. Miss FIELDIIOUSE seconded and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>James Webborn, a member of Mumbles lifeboat crew. was swimming under water at Swansea Baths on Monday afternoon, when his heart failed and he was found dead in the bath head downwards.</p>
