      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>P SA. MOVEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE P SA. MOVEMENT.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Wycliffe Society. The annual meeting of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society wlts hold. in the Church on Sunday afternoon. In the ahsenco of the President ((ev. John Yongn), Mr. • It. Atkinson was voted to .the chair, there was a fair attendance of members.</p>
        <p>In presenting the twelfth annual report the SECR I.ll' Ara' (Mr.B. Farrarisald the workers had done theirjwork with ,) a willing spirit. The officers of the sick club had given great satisfaction to its members and at the close of the , year- they were able to pay a dividend of ss. to each member who came under the rule and had net, received more than a certain amount of sick pay during the year. The savings club had been very well conducted and already upwards of £l4O was deposited, after a heavY withdrawal at Christmas time. The benevolent fund had been of great service to some of their needy brethren and the wish of the Society was that it could be increasingly useful. After having acknowledged the faithful services of various officers, the secretar,v ' reviewed the meetings of the year, which, he said, had&amp; been Marked sith religious earnestness. Hearty thanka were offered to all who had se willingly helped to make the gatherings bright and uplifting. 'lt was pleasing to ' report that the number of members enrolled last year '• was about 30 more than the previous year, the total rnemberahip being ,530 with an,:iteerage attendance Of : about 300. The number of prizes obtained was 707 e making the total number awarded since the commence- meut of the Soniety 10,603. The Society was rendering a splendid service by introducing good book' into the homes of the town. Continuing, Mr. Farrar- said their P.S.A. was no loner an experiment. It wa°- not even on ite trial. but was now a recognized institu- ;-• tion of the town. The prophets who foretold its speed disiolution had long since been discredited. Some sti4 clung to the idea that the movement was for amusement only, because of its name "pleasant," but, said Mr' Farrar,ithe movement was animated with an earnest Par" 'pose, and existed for the highest spiritual ends. Thel were thankful the • mbar of critics was diminish- ing. Christ's own test could be put upon their work "Bv their fruits shall ye know them." Great numbers. of lives once careless, drunken and unclean, were sober, honest and pure, through the influence of the P.S.A. For that, they gave thanks to God and took ,0 encouragement for their future work. Let them as brotherhood do their duty under al strong sense of their obligation to God for nIl he had done for them !' individually. A spirit of revival was in the air. What. had begun in Wales they hoped would so spread all over :y this country, and that its inflaenee would be felt la Warrington. The membership of the Society for the - present year was 450, and the number was still increas- ing.</p>
        <p>FIN'ANC IAJ STITEM ENTS. Mr. R. Arn INSON (the treasurer) submitted the annual' financial statement. The total income of the societi had been £l2l 2. Old., leaving a balance of Is. o,ld. hand. Mr. R. l‘ftr.r.s presented his absentees report of visi— tations, stating that over 500 visits had been made. Mr. J. 0 wEs (Benevolent Fund secretary) said hobs&amp; made a large number of visits to needy brethren. which' were greatly appreciated. The SEC 214 CARY of the Sick Club submitted a pleasing report. After meeting all liabilities they paid out dividend of ss. to its members, and had a good balance' carried forward. - -</p>
        <p>All the reports were adopred.—The Rev. John Yonge was re-elected president ; Mr B. Farrar. general secret tary ; Mr. 1. Atkinson. treasurer ; 11r. A. Cotterili,.. rmiistration secretary; Ir. R. Mills, abseutee secretal Mr..f. Owen, Benevolent Fund secretary and treasurer , Me srs. W. and El. Farrar, markers ; kessrs. J. Tank' ford, T. Wood, and J. Dutton. stewards ; geneml COP,: mittee, Messrs 1 Evans. G. Donaldson, W. Beswic,"? G. Wallington, J. Bailey, W. Crosskill, A, Healey, G. Smiley. E. Smith, J. Sirucock, J. Garner, G. Colley, Cotton, J. Dobbins. J. R. Palmer, J. Grestv, J. West, 1" Riley, G. Ford, W. Sparkes, IT. Taker, .W. Miles,„,"' Hewitt, T. Ellison, H. Mcliachern. %W.Greeuleaves, Donald</p>
        <p>Determined Suicide at Sankey. „ On Friday evening last week James Jay (11), playing near the St. -Helens and Sankey Canal iv1.,„.10. he saw a nnn deliberately jump into the canal. raisel the alarm and the body was recovered shortly, H afterwards, and artificial respiration tried but without avail. The body was identified as that of John Moo OH under-hand puddler, who lived at Booth-stre Warrington.—At the inquest which was held on Tao" day William Box, 3, Booth-etreet, stated that 01? deceased had noi heen well for sonutime, and haa complained of " something being the matter with 117 chest." He bad been drinking heavily, and he hi' heard him threaten to take poison, but not to drol himself. A verdict of " Suicide whilst :of unsoup" mind " was returned.</p>
      </div>
