        <head>BLACK • MOTOR CAR. . B. HARRIS BURLAND.</head>
        <p>THE BLACK • MOTOR CAR. . B. HARRIS BURLAND.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIII. NEWS FROM VALPARAISO.</p>
        <p>Tim next morning the whWe of Essex 'rang</p>
        <p>with the news of _the, burglary at Heather: , stone , Hall, . and the murder of _young Lord Overclille,. heir to the title and• the great, Heatherstone estates. By noon the story had '.-reached the LAndon_ evening papers, and the</p>
        <p>_ . lialf-penuy press woi-lted up three colurnris,out •of the Scanty material at their disposal. Ittiousarie3dot23 of the family from the</p>
        <p>71me of Henry VIII. supplied winch of the 7 t er. The news of the murder was brief, and Was ingeniouslysndWieted be'‘Ween the mastr7es of irrelevant information. But that .day saYT a score of keen reporters on the spot, .an7l half a dozen detectives, equally keen, 'but 17777 s obtrusive in their methods, and the falloWing morning all •England knew of the Fleatherstone murder. The news came ta the Red • House through the postman.. Lipp blanight it with the letters to Willittei Jordi'son as the latter wat l'reakfast. He conveyed the neWs, in tWa 'words. , -"" Lord OVercliffe,"• he. Nvhi!,pered quietly in lhis master's oar. Tordison did not look up from his substan-, titil breakfast oh eggs And bacon, but taking up. the letters Which had been placed by his side, glanced carelesly at the enyelnpes. Lipp still stood behind his chair, as though expect- ing some remark. , :fordisOn went on with his breakfast, after, •seleqing one letter from the others, and Ilueingit by the side of his plate. , " Lord dvercliffe," 'Lipp repeated hoarsely.- Jordison .turned found sharply and looked at. The, man's, face was white and unwhole- some in appcaraace. There was a milieu look</p>
        <p>of fear in his eyes. ' " It doesn't matter, Lipp," Jordison said, • " the fact remains the same, it is an . ugly .busines." • " We'll both Swing for it," continued the .servant in a half friglitdned tone. " Possibly," .:said Jordistin, " but all men's lives are equal. . Lord Overclifle is of no more importance in the eyes of. the law, than one of the .footnien." The inan bent 'down till his ugly month was within 'an inch of Jordisou's ear. , _</p>
        <p>Yer a bloomin' fool," he said familiarly. • The death of _a servant would be forgotten</p>
        <p>in a month. But 'ha, that young lord—lor bless yer, they'll 'move heaven and earth an' 'ell to find out who killed 'im. And they've •the brass to keep things rolling, and we 'ave'nt a dog's chance." . " I will take such chance as there is, Lipp," Jordison replied quVoy, " and stay here. Von can go if you like. But your departure will be known and you will be followed and arrested on suspicion. Your past career will probably cone to light, and then---" ." And then wet ? " , " Why the fat will be in the fire; and you will frizzle in it."</p>
        <p>The man swore a horrible oath and gripped the back of jordison's chair with his fingers The latter did not look round at him, but his Tight hand stole to his pocket as though 'searching for his handkerchief. " Take my word for it, Lipp," , he said •quictiv. " You are safe. enough here." " Wot. cf Jeri-ay and Susauson ? " 'said 'the man. " oe a Li,00:) reward faz this job, may be L5.0D0, may be L.tooio. 'Ole °Etherstone von t spare expense. D'yer think 'Liman natur"ll stand it ? ' jordison made no reply. But the thought ran through his mind that he himself , was equally dangerous to Lipp.. He kept his band in his,pocket. " D'yer think I'm gain' to put My neck in their May 'ands, or yours either," continued the ruffian, echoing jordison's own thoughts. " I ain't such a babe and suckling." jordis:Ai turned sharply niund and. caught the ilook in his servant's eyes—a murderous, evil look. He also saw a pair. of thick 'muscular hands unpleasantly near the back of his neck. The man drew back, as he turned, and showed his .yellow teeth. . " That'll do, Lipp,'' jordison said ; " see that jenny and Susanson keep their mouths shut. As tor me, I'm in the same boat as yourself. Now will you kindly go. as•l want to read my letters and finish my' break- fast, and mark yOu, Lipp, no tricks. you'll Aind me an unpleasant person to deal with, if you cut up rough. • But if you stick to me, • i'll stick to you. Do you understand ? " " Yes, guynor," the man replied ; me your solemn, oath L---" jordis3n rose to his feet and held out his hand. - " Here's my hand.' on it, Lipp, and ray word of honour."</p>
        <p>"but gi'</p>
        <p>The man took' the proffered hand and peered into jordison's face. Then he grinned .and, the master knew whit was passing through the servant's mind, and his face flushed. " Word of honour ! 'che , phrase had come to his lips from the past wilen it had had a real meaning. He felt that it required sotne re-adjustment to suit the prbscmt cir- •cutnstances.</p>
        <p>" There's honour among .thieVes," he said bitterly ; " you can trust me, Lipp." Swelp me bub,. I believes yet.. guvoor. I'll stick to y-er." • - " Neither Jenny nor Susanson must leave the house until I've decided how to keep their mouths shut. I can rely on you to see to that." . " Right, guvnor," the man answered, as he Islouched out ol the room.</p>
        <p>When the door closed, Jordison hastily •swallowed a few more_ mouthfuls of breakfast, but his eyes never I:.sft the letter which lay on the right of his plate, and every now and then he fingered it expectantly. The en- velope was thick to the touch, and ,bore a monogram of 13. • and W., intertwined so -,gracefully as to be barely intelligible. When he had finished his • breakfast he tore open the envelope and pulling cut several 6neets of paper studied them carefully. The letter from Messrs. Briggs aad Warlock' tram as, follows : " DEAR S!,— " In accordance with your instructions we sent out our special agent .to Val- paraiso to investigate the supposed death of your sob. We enclose you his report, from which we regret to learn that Mr. Richard Behag is undoubtedly dead, and was in all probability mur- dered by his friend, Mr. Arthur Sterious. Perhaps you will wish to let the matter end at this , point. If you desire to trace the murderer of your son, our services are at your disposal. We have so far not gone beyohd your instructions. " We are, dear sir, •• Your re.Tectfully, " DRIGG.S_ AND WARLOCK."</p>
        <p>Enclosed was the following report, written nn foreign paper in a neat bat commonplace -band :</p>
        <p>"DEAR MR. -WARLOCK,- -" I sailed for Buenos Ayres on August 9th in the .` Caballo,' and crossing overland to Valparaiso put up at -• the. ' Viajero ' Hotel. Within a few hours of my arrival I called on the Chief of the Police, and asked for his co- -operation in the matter which had b2en .placed lin my hands. He 'treated ins with cool- ness, but when I carelessly pulled out my wallet on pretence Of searching for a letter, and thereby disclosed a. considerable bundle of English bank notes, his manner underwent a marked change, and at the end of half an hour 'we became the best of friznds.- This was a' considerable but a necessary expense, and I did it ts cheaply as. possible. Senor Donoso a very proud man. He promised to search UP the records at his office. ,I• gathered this .would take time; as life is held cheap in these South American States, and the records oi *SUdden death are somewhat voluminous. A 'week afterwards I received a note from him asking me to,call at his private, house, and there, with the help of some. .excellent wine and cigars, he told me all that he knew about</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 27, 1905. BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 27, 1905. BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</p>
        <p>Richard Behag. and breadth of shoulder, with fair - hair "It appears that when Behag left his ship and grey eyes. As you may imagine, this at Valparaiso he obtained work as a clerk in .description will fit a large number of men. the trading house of Hicks, Sterious, and "I am, dear sir; your obedient servant, Company. who were one of the leading English ." EDWARD LAMPBT." firms in the city. He seem ts to have been a Jordison laid the letter down, and stared young man of considerable intelligence ana into the fire. It warmed his face with a red more than average education, for by the end glow, yet the tinge of colour did not soften the of the year I find that he had been promoted bard cold misery in his eyes. His son was PI the responsible position of head cashier. It dead ; little Diclde, whom he only rem eni- also seems that he had formed a somewhat uh- bered as an innocent child, was dead—a thief desirable friendship with young Arthur I murdered by 'his accomplice. Sterious. The latter was the eldest son of the "it is well," he murmured to himself, "it is principal partner in the firm, but he had, well that he is dead." little or no aptitude for business, and his , Yet that day he wired to Brigge and War- name was mixed up with more than one disre- lock, and before evening the message had been putable scandal in Valparaiso. From all T- flashed across the broad Atlantic to Edward could gather he- appears to have been a young Lampet. The cable wag thort but expressed man of athletic build and pursuits, but of a all that was uppermost hi the man's mind. peculiarly wild and vicious character. I " Sterious must be found. Spare no expense." strongly suspect that this somewhat undesir- And all that night Jordison dreamed of a able friendship sprang from interested sources tall, broad ihouldered yhung fellow with fair cal both sides. , Behag saw a possible rise in ehair and. grey eyes—a bronzed young fellow his position from his intimacy with the son versed in seacraft ; and, in one of the dreams of a meniber of the firm, and Sterious, as I I the stalwart frame hung limp from a rope, should guess ftom what subseqbently trans.; with strapped arms and a broken neck.. pired, had even more sordid reasons foe - friendship with the cashier of his father's office. I may do either or both of them an injustice, but, except on one point, their tastes appear to have been very dissimilar, and it' is at any rate curiOus that a steady, hard work- ing young fellow like Behag should ' have chummed up with a man whose character was not even, up to the somewhat lax standard of South American cities. They had, how- ever, one pursuit in common,. and it may possibly liave been this which linked them tegether. , They were both fond of the sea. Arthur Sterious owned, two yachts, one the Pajarito;' a small five4onner, and the other the ' Pajaro,' a fifty-tor:tier. Behag had been a sailor by prcifession, and was probably glad to be a paseenger on the bigger boat, or else potter about in the smaller one for his own eniiisement. At any rate he seems to have spent much of his spare time on the water. " Then one Sunday morning Behag and Sterious went out in the Pajarito,' and since that day no one has ever set eyes on them again. "It was a fine warm day with a slight southerly breeze. Both were experienced sailors, and it is extremely tmlikely that they could have come to grief in fair weather. But for all that the ' Pajarito ' was never picked up. A week afterwards her dinghey came ashore twenty miles north of Valparaiso, bottom upwards. , '.The general opinion at .the time was, tnati tor some reason or other the yacht had foundered, and that the two men had put off in the (Hughey and met with an accident in the open sea.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV.</p>
        <p>WHAT JERAIY FOUND IN THE CREEK.</p>
        <p>LIPP was right in his conjectures. Twt. weeks after the murder of .young. Lord Over. 'cliffe a bill was posted throughout the length' and breadth 'of the county proclaiming that Lord Heatherstone would give 45,000 reward to anyone who would give such information as would lead to the deteetton of the murderer. The keenest professional inquiry agents in England gathered to that part of the world, as vultures to a carcase ; the police moved on in their, own silent methocal way ; and the number of men who left their legitimate business and . assumed the ride of amateur detective was so consider- able that it amounted to a serious dis- organisation of labotni in the 'Eastern counties. It. seemed, however, as though the murderers had left no trace behind them. Lipp and Jordison feared only one' danger, and that was within ,their own house. It came home to them 'at last. One morn- ing I.ipp, half-dressed, saw a figure fait down the road that led to Gorehaven. At first he thought it .was the postman, then, noticing. that. it was Moving away from the house, 'he rushed downstairs, and found that Susanson was missing. Half-an-hour later, Jordison was driving his motor furiously along the road between the creeks,. and Lipp was sitting in, the tonneau 'behind with a pair of field glasses in his horny hands. Jermy, bound hand and foot was lying on his back in a locked room; and contemplating a patch of dull grey sky through a barred window. He felt it to be an tndignity, but he realised the necessity of the precaution. Hour, after hour he spluttered horrible oaths through the gag that had been fastened in his mouth, cursing Jordison, cursing Lipp, and cursing most of all Susan- son, the dirty little jew, who had forestalled him in his treachery. He pictured ,to himself Susanson squandering his is,ooo on diamond rings and fat little Jewesses with black greasy locks. •</p>
        <p>'.'But three months after Wards it was rumoured that the great firm of Hicks sterious, and Company were in difficulties. A month later they failed, and in the subsequent . enquiries into the matter it came out that they had been robbed of Cash and negotiable securities to the morn-ions amount of 200,000, and • that the fraud had been discovered twenty-four hours after the disappearance of .the head cashier." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Jordison rose to his feet with darkened face half crumpling the letter' in his hand. He scarcely dared to read another line. His mind flew back to the miserable termination of his own honesty: Was it possible that history had repeate I itself, that the son was tainted with the • father's blood, that the Criminal instinct had become hereditary. If it Were so, he thanked God that his son was dead, and that the race had been stamped out. He sat down again in the chair and buried his face in his hands, and several moments clapsed before the had the courage to resume the perusal of the letter. It continued as follows ':—</p>
        <p>But the sense of fury and disappointment in Jenny's mind soon gave way to an over- whelming terror. He knew • that he had not many • hours to live. • .He realised that his death would be a most desirable event in the eyes of Lipp and jordison, and that neither of them were likely to have any scruples about taking his life. If Susanson had reached the Police station at Gorehaven, Lipp and Jordison would have all their work cut out to save themselves, and probably they would not even return to the house. If on the other hand, they overtook Susanson, it was quite certain that the poor little Jew would never have another chance of betraying them. In which case Jenny would be the sole possessor of the secret and it would be almost necessary for them to ensure his silence.</p>
        <p>"The firm had kept the secret till they were forced to disclose it, knowing well ' that any rumour of their loss :would involve -them in ruin. But directly the truth I was known, it was clear to everyone that the robbery had been committed by Bebag and Sterious, and that the two men had endeavoured to make off with all the negotiable securities on the Pajarito.' " At first it seemed probable, that the two men had rowed ashore in the dinghey and made good their escape, but a month after the failure of Hicks, Sterious, and Company, the sea gave up its dead. The body of a man was washed ashore forty miles north of Valparaiso, and though scarcely a shred of flesh was left on the bones, and the clothes were so worn as to scarcely afford the means of identifica- tion, yet there was no doubt that it was the body of Richard , Behag, for a gold ring bear- ing his monogram was still hanging on one of his fingers. The skull was broken in, and the doctor who examined the body gave it as his opinion that this was the result of fou play. The mark was long and clearly defined as though it had been inflicted by some heavy instrumen,t, and it was, clearly not caused by rocks or an accidental collision of the body with anything in the sea. " This discovery pot an entirely new com- plexion on affairs,. and the police ,came to the reasonable conclusion thi_t Sterious had mur- dered Behag and made good his escape with the lost securities.</p>
        <p>In either case Jermy realised that he must free himself from his bonds. He would eithet be left to starve to death, or else he would be murdered; or else he would himself fall into the hands of the police, an event, which, though preferable .to the other two alterna- tives, would be by no means a thing to be desired.</p>
        <p>All through the clay his mind had been trying to evolve some method of loosening his bonds, but he was as helpless as a man with a broken spine. Not only was he bound . hand and foot, but several separate ropes had been passed round his body, till he was swathed like art Egyptian mummy. As an additional security, a rope had been passed round his neck and fastened to the leg of a heavy iron bedstead. Lipp had dime his work well. The prisoner could not even roll over on the floor. Jermy knew that he must free his hands, but it seemed almost an impossibility. The wrists were lashed tightly together, and his arms were bound close to his body. ' Even if he .could have raised his frrist to his mouth he would have been no nearer to freedom, for the gag effectually prevented him from doing anything with his teeth. All through the day he lay like a log, hun- gry, thirsty, 'half choked, and aching with the pressure of his bonds. And yet all the time forgetting physical discomfort and pain in the agony of terrible suspense, with ears strained to catch the sound of voices outside thewin- dow and the tramp of footsteps on the stairs. • It was not until the sunlight had died from the sky that a humble friend came to Jermy's assistance. Shortly after the clock in the hall • had chimed five, he heard the patter of tiny feet on the floor of his bedroom. He knew at once that it was a rat. The . house was literally over-run with them.. His mind at once travelled back to stories he had read— thrilling stories, where a kindly rodent had nibbled through the bonds, of the hero. He, glanced at the ropes. They looked un- appetising, and he remembered that the hero had generally managed to rub them over with oil or fat. However,. he lay still and hoped, for it is wonderful what straws a drowning man will clutch at. , The rat moved round and round in search of something to eat. Once it came within a yard of Jcrmy's head, and looked at him with beady eyes. Perhaps it recognised a brother, for it was the animal of which Jermy was tile human type the unclean ravenous thing that man stamps under foot and exterminates. Jermy held his breath and wondered what it would. do next. lie recalled unpleasant tales of men being eaten alive by rats, and the sound of others in the wainscotting by no means reassured him. The rat, however, did not seem to care for a closer acquaintance with the lump of silent flesh on the floor, and, turning round, it ran up the bed clothes, and made its way up a bell rope to a long deal Shelf, some eight feet above the ground. The shelf was covered with tins, jars and • bot- tles, and Jermy heard the clink of glass as' the animal crept along on its tour of ,inspection. Then the rat did a thing which, if it had occurred in the middle ages, would have earned it a place of honour on the Jermy coat of arms. It discovered a large jar half full of axle grease, and in its efforts to taste this rich dainty, it sent the whole thing crashing to the ground. The bottom half, with its jagged edge of thick earthenware rolled to Jermy's _side.</p>
        <p>"This theory was subsequently confirmed by a most unexpected occurrence. One morning about six months after the day Sterious and Bebag had left Valparaiso, Mr. Sterious senior received a bulky 'package from ;England. It was registered and addressed in a handwriting that be did not recognise. On opening it, :he found that it contained the whole of the missing securities, and that only the sum .of 2,0C.,0 was missing. The surprise was as welcc;ine as it was inexplicable. But Hicks, Steritms and Co. have regained nearly all the prestige they had lost, and they are to-day one of the most respected firms in Valparaiso. I may say that the package was posted in the south. west district of London. "For obvious reasons the, firn. made no endeavours to trace the whereabouts of Arthur Stcrious, and the police here have their hands too full of murder cases :to take any trouble in the case of a young .man who had no 'friends to stimulate their . efforts with hard cash. The matter has been • entirely dropped. Richard Behag is .dead and Arthur Sterious has disappeared, and there the. whole story seems to *-nd. A careful perusal of the evidence that I send you has led me to the foliowinr,' simple conclusion. " Behag and Sterious arranged to rob the bank, and escape oh. the ' Pajarito.' They subsequently quarrelled, as thieves will do, over the division. of the spoil. Or, perhaps Sterious, who seems to have been a thorough scoundrel, thought it would be best to put his accomplice out of the way. At any rate, Arthur Sterious killed Behag, threw him over- board, and tried to call the N-acht himself. He probably found this too much kir him, got into difficulties, scuttled the boat, and made off for shore in the ilinghey, which he subse- quently left to the mercy of the waves and winds. Eventually he managed to make . his way to Europe. Up to this point the theory seems to present no difficulties, but the return of the securities ig absolutely inexplicable, and I have, as yet, been unable to conceive why a man who had risked so much to gain his end, and who had even killed his accomplice in the course of his crime, should give back the very thing for which he had committed both rob- ber 3„, and murder. " I may say in conclusion that I have made strenuous efiorts to secure a photograph of Arthur Sterious, and have even committed a mild burglary with this ,object, in view. The young' man has apparently , only once been photographed dtiring the past seven years, and I have been -unable to lay "my hands on a single copy. As, however, my instructions do not extend beyond the 'confirmation of Mr. Behag' death, I duresay your client will be satiated.with what I have accomplished. " Unless I receive a cable from !eon within two months from dr.tc„ I shall return home. Ikt the meantnng I will endeavour to obtain sulth information about Mr. Sterious as may lead to his, ultimate capture The case inter- CSi.5 me, and if anyone else is sufficiently interested in the ^detection of the ;nurderer, at;cl Would pay , my expenses and a trifle for Myself, I iwill guarantee to track the fellow down. At prcsent my only clue is the tact that the S(eurities were posted from London, aud that the fugitive is a man of great height</p>
        <p>In less than a quarjer of an hour Jenny had sawed through the rope that boand his wrists and arms. The broken edge of the jar was as lieeri as a razor. Rolling his body from side to side, he worked carelully and with "enormous difficulty. His hands streamed with blood, but the loss of a fiugor would not have sto,ppv‘l him.</p>
        <p>iTo be cuLtiriued.) Worst Half</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptha saves half of wash- day—the worst half. ' No hot fire, no hot water, very little ,rubbing. The soaking does most of the work.</p>
        <p>Fels-Naptlia 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
        <p>high. speed of the stroke allows little command to be had over the red. A very much better stroke for fairly good players to practise is a. thin screw with running id e (right hand side to the diagram). A few attempts will quickly determine the best kind of contact with the red. The great superiority of this thin screw in-off over the forcing in-off lies firstly in ,the greatly reduced speed at which the cue-ball travels to the pocket; and, secondly, in the much better command which the player has over the red ball. It is often quite possible to get these thin-screw ut-ofTs without even sending the red over the line, but except when- the cue-ball happens to be very. near the red it is better to play them with suffi- cient strength to drive the red in and out of baulk-, and thus avoid any possibility of leaving the ball in baulk. Another great point in favour of this thin-screw is that by reason of the red ball being caught very thin, it is kept well away from the side cushion as it travels down the table, Whereas the forcing stroke, with its fuller contact, always throws the red on to the side cushion-, and complications often ensue from its contact with the angles of the centre pocket</p>
        <p>When playing serew-strckes. whether cannons or in-offs, with the cue-ball not very far from the object-hall, the degree of contact with the object-ball can as a rule be varied between very wide limits by graduating the amount of screw inapartNl to the ball; conseqrtently the object ball can -be driven in quite different direction according to the after-position desired.</p>
        <p>Giving good direction to the object ball by taking It fuller or finer, according to the requirements of the after-position.—Measurements at A, red ball 10 irfches from the side cushion and 64,1 in. Mes from the top cushion. Cue ball 61 inches from the side cushion and 481 inches from the top cushion. At B—red ball on a line between the centre pockets and 61- inches from the fall of the slate. Object white 9i inches from the side cushion and 4) inches from the baulk cushion. Cue ball 13 inches from the side cushion and 39 inches from the top cushion. '</p>
        <p>Diagram 104 shows two other screw cannons made by hitting the cushion in front of the object ball. The one at A requires a good deal of judgment as the cue ball has to travel right across the table to the second„object ball. Yet although such a stroke is never anything like a certainty with the very best players, it is one that as a stroke is not beyond any fairly good player, and the knowledge that such a stroke is on; may often get a player out of a difficulty. The cannon at B does not require the same accuracy as the one at A, for the cue-ball may cannon direct on to the red, or it may strike the top cushion just in front of the red, or some distance away from it previous to making the cannon. The placing of the balls on the dia- gram shows the easiest position for this particu- lar cannon, as there is no room for the cue ball to pass behind the red. The cushion must be hit a considerable distance in front of the object white, and the stroke played with plenty 0: strength and Screw.</p>
        <p>A fhin•serew in-off.—Fled ort the spor. cue ball n inches from the top cushion and .30A iuchea from the side cushion.</p>
        <p>Diagram 105 shows an in-off from the red on the spot. Most players use a forcing stroke for this in-off, and as a rule the stroke is over forced, and in any case Owing to the high speed at which the stroke must be played; the ball must go very tine to the pocket, otherwise it will be thrown away by the opposing angle. Then, again. the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>17-' Cllw xxrv-x.</head>
        <p>17-' Cllw xxrv-x.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>xxxix. Screw and Reverse Rotation</p>
        <p>(CONTINUED.)</p>
        <p>SCREW OFF A CUSHION.</p>
        <p>Most ordinary players are under the impres- sion that all screw strokes require a direct stroke on to the object ball, but this is not 60, for reverse rotation on the cue ball will cause it to screw on coming into contact with an object ball, though a cushion be hit before con- tact between the balls takes place. By hitting a cushion first, many screw cannons can be made by a comparatively slow stroke, which would otherwise" require a strong scattering stroke if played as ball-to-ball screws. In other cases a cannon may be made, by means of a strong screw off a cushion which would be quite impossible by means of a direct screw.</p>
        <p>DIAGRAN 103.</p>
        <p>A screw cannon off a cushion.—Red ball on the Spot. object white, one inch from the top cushion and 29i inches from the side cushion. Cue ball five inches from the top cushion and 29 inches from the side cushion.</p>
        <p>Diagram 103 shows cannon made by means of a screw off the cushion. This pi.si- tion, or something very similar, frequently occurs, and though there are several diffe- rent ways of making the cannon with, the balls placed. to the measurements given under the diagram, a screw cannon made by striking the cushion just in front of the white is the best stroke to play. A cannon could be made by means of screw direct from the white on ta the red, or direct from the red on to the white, but in either case plenty of strength would have to be Used, and there would be a large element of uncertainty as to the after position. A cannon could also be made off the white and the cushion, but such a stroke would require well judging. The cannon made by screwing off the cushion also requires judging, as too much strength and screw will cause it to be missed. 'With a little practice, however, this cannon by screw off the cushion will be found to present very little difficulty to any moderate player.</p>
        <p>Two screw cannons off a cushion.—Position R. object whits of an inch from the side cushion and 12 inches from the ton cushion. Red ball 11 in-lics from the top cushion and 26 sieJles from the side cushion; and cue ball on the baulk line, 13 inches from the side cushioti."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE.</p>
        <p>Successful Show.</p>
        <p>Success continues to attend the efforts of the promoters of Warrington's annual horse parade, the procession on Saturday in many respects being superior to those of former years. One factor which contributed largely to the attainment of this was the brilliant weather with which the parade was favoured. The competitors assembled on Arpley meadows and at 12 o'clock the judges commenced their difficult task of making the swards—a task which was not completed until two hours later. in total the entres were about the same as last year, but there was a marked improvement in the condition of the animals which competed. Messrs. P. Walker and Son were again the largest exhibitors, though Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. were also to the fore in this respect, their animals, however, not being entered for competition. The nightsoil department: of the Cor- poration was also well represented, and there was a splendid turnout of animals belonging to the majority of the firms in the town. The judging over, Lady Greenall presented the prizes to the successful drivers and after she bad been thanked for her services the procession moved off, headed by the corporation Fire Brigade with the two steam I tire engines, both of which had been tastefully decora- ted. Messrs. Walker's Band preceded the animals ; belonging to that, firm and like the other two bands in the procession—Crosfield's and the Borough—discoursed excellmt music throughout the proceedings. The whole of the route was thickly lined with spectators, who were loud in their praise of the splendid condition of the j animals in the procession. Many of the drivers had been j to the trouble of gaily decorating the turnouts in their charge, but as the prizes were awarded for the condition of the horses, the majority of the I drivers were content at having slightly decked the I harness with highly coloured ribbons. It would per- ' haps add considerably to the attractiveness of the Parade if a prize were offered for the best decorated horse, on some inducement given for drivers to make , their animals and vehicles look as pretty as is possible. ' It cannot be denied that there was a sameness about the exhibits which, to the average onlooker, made the watching of the parade a little tedious. Some small variety was afforded by the inclusion of three fire bripades—the Corpora tion,Crosflelds'And Messrs.Greenall Whitley and Company's. As usual collections were made en route on behalf of the local charitable institu- tions and prominent amongst the collectors were the footballers, Hallam and Isherwood, who carried with them ins landau the Northern Union Cup won by the Warrington football team. Praise must be given to the gentlemen who worked hard to make the event so successful. They were : Chief marshal, Mr. T. H. Pretty; referee, Mr. Peter Davies : Reception Committee, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Councillois Roberts and Fultoe Smith, and Mr. James 1 Monre.; general committee, Messrs. W. Bainbridge, H. Ferguson, jun., W. Fletcher, Robert Garton, S. Picton, H. G. Roberts, W. P. Rylands, R. Spann and G. Venn. The following gentlemen acted as judges: —Messrs. John Abbott. Thomas Baxter, P. Davies Wm. Fairclougls, Peter Knowles,' John Longshaw, George Marsh, H. ' hlawdesley, E. Millar, Thomas Percival, INI.PiIIing,EAV. Sankey, Robert Turner, J. Turton, W. Gilbert, and 1 James Gould.</p>
