        <p>Diagram 106 shows two screw in-offs, both simple enough shots, but strokes over which the ordinary player tufo often comes to grief. In the position shown at A, the player who simply plays for, the in-off without any thought as to where the object-ball may go, frequently sends this ball into the baulk pocket or causes it to catch the angles of the pocket, as shown by the thin inter- sected line on the diagram, with the result that it • remains in baulk. . When this happens the el ver thinks he has had very hard lines. whereas he has really played an exceedingly bad stroke. The thoughtful player takes care to avoid alto- gether the baulk pocket and its angles, and this . is best done By taking the object ball full enough to cause it to strike the baulk cushion a font or more away from the pocket, as shown by the thick intersected line on the diagram. In the position shown at B a careless stroke may easily cause the red ball to kiss the white, and when this happens both balls will most likely go in baulk and remain there. Instead, by taking the red hall thinner, it can easily be driven past the white and in and out of Laulk as shown by the intersected line on the diagram. (To be Continued.)</p>
