        <p>he " Cpuniner " *mit;</p>
        <p>Mr. BALFOUR indulged TRIFLING WITH A in his favourite juggling PLEDGE. on Monday night. He had elected to regard Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN'S legitimate questions about the scope ot the Colonial Confer- ence which will meet next year as implying that the Opposition was objecting to discussion of the the fiscal issue outside the House of Commons. But the objection relates to action, not discussion, and to the very action from which we should be debarred by Mr. 13.&amp;LFoun's speech at Edinburgh. He seems to think that it is no business of his to prevent the Conference from making proposals. But he said at Edinburgh that he would not enter into nogotiations with the Colonies on this subject with. out an express mandate from the constituencies. There is really no way of dodging this pledge, and yet Mr. BALFOUR tries to dodge it. The Opposi- tion was justified in suspecting that this change of fro&amp; is part of the new " deal" wh:ch Mr. BALFOUR is hegotiating with Mr. CHAMBERLAIN. Mr. CframnEautix's friends objected strongly to the plan of securing two General E actions before our fiscal system could be altered. They could not see why one national mandate should be rev ired for the discussion of preferential trade with the Colonial delegates, and another national mandate for any proposals the aonference might adopt The point has no material importance for us,except as illustrating the necessity of an early appeal to the country on the whole question. It shows that Mr. BALFOUR'S resolve to run no risk of losing office until next year, or even until the expiration of the term proscribed by the Sspteunial Act, involves him in constant breach of the plainest obligations. To keep the promise at Edinburgh in letter and spirit, he ought to dissolve before the meeting of the Conference, or else bind himself to allow no discussion of the present issues by the delegates. When he says they are as much entitled to discuss it as anybody in or out of Parliament, he is trifling with his pledged word.</p>
        <p>L )rd HUGH CECIL has constructed a series of questions which seam designed to give M. BALFOUR a rare opportunity of farther exercising his peculiar gift of equivocation. L3rd LinGn is evidently of opinion that the automatic Carder- (ince, as it is called, ought to meet next year, according to arrangement; but he wants guarantees from Mr. BALFOUR that the meeting will not be used to further the cause of tariff reform. Mr. 'BALFOUR Will give any number of guarantees with alacrity. He will swear that the Conference has nothing to do with the fiscal question, and that its opinions will not be binding on any. body. If Lord HUGH should find this explanation satisfactory, he must be very simple-minded. In ordinary circumstances, the opinion of the Con- ference would be harmless enough. At the Con- ference of 1902 many things were said with which the people of this country do not agree ; but they had no bearing on practical politics, and so they passed without demur. Next year, the whole situation will be different. The fiscal ques- tion will be in its third year of violent agitation. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN and his friends will use every endeavour to get strong declare- tons in favour of preference from the Colonial delegates. We have never pretended that sonne,Colonies were not in favour of Prefer- ence—on their own terms. Bat we have not per- ceived any disposition on their part to give a fair equivalent. They would like to do a capital stroke of business for themselves, and we do not blame them. They are fair-minded enough not to blame the Liberal Party for pointing out that the stroke of business is not good enough for this country. It is still more unsatisfactory when it is used in Mr. BALFOUR'S calculations in blank opposition to his pledge, that no Conference should discuss the fiscal issue before the General Election. How is this argument met? We are told that it shows a spirit of " dictation, suspicion, and antagonism to the Colonies." The Colonies, we are convinced, will not take that view. They will not relish such an obvious desire to make elec- tioneering use of them for the benefit of the Government. But it is necessary to point out that if next year's Conference is held before a Dissolution, this is precisely the use that will be made of it. Every effort will be made to represent its academic opinion as the only means of unifying the E mpire. We have not the smallest doubt that the constituencies will reject the idea of preferential trade, all the same ; but it is likely nough that some votes will be affected by this perfectly illicit appeal, which the Conference of 1906 was not not intended to make when it was projected. Mr. BAIXOUR'S friends assure OE that</p>
        <p>It is all right. Their great chieftain, is successful at the polls, xvil.l summon another Conference specially charged with the duty of framing a scheme of preference, and this will be submitted to the ordeal of anothet General Election. Does anybody really believe it? Suppose Mr. BALFOUR was to get a majority when he dissolves. Is he the man to hazard his luck again when preferential trade had taken definito shape with an agreement with the Colonies ? We thin.&lt; not. Mr. BALFOUR Is just the man with those cards in his hand to hold them for another six years or so under the Septennial Act. We do not believe he will have the opportunity, but his present manceuvring sufficiently illustrates the devices to which he is capable of resorting.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner, Bart., M.P., will formally open the Widnes and Runcorn Transporter Bridge on Monday next. No more fitting person could have been found to perform this important office, for Sir John was one of the prime movers in the scheme which is now completed for increasing the facilities for inter-communication by road between the adjacent counties of Lancashire and Cheshire. Previously it was a case of "so near and</p>
        <p>yet so far" and it is remarkable that for so many hundred years the inhabitants of the two comities have put up with the very limited means of crossing the Mersey sit their disposal. The Transporter Bridge should do much to assist in the commercial development of the , districts which it will feed. Up to nowlhey have been practically " wide as the poles asunder" and had a barely nodding acquaintance with one another. The ceremonies on Monday commence with a reception at Widnes Town Hall at 12-30 and conclude with a luncheon at itancorn Town Hall, where Sir John Brunner will be the host.</p>
        <p>Whenever the election comes, it will find the , Liberals of Warrington well prepared for the con- flict. The enthusiasm with which the work of organizing the forces is being entered into is indeed refreshing to those who have the furtherance of the progressive cause at heart. Mr. A. 11. Crosfield is doing his utmost to educate the constituency to a full realization of the great causes which are at stake, and his thoughtful address at the joint ward meeting on Monday will appeal to a far wider circle than that to which it was delivered. Mr. Crosfield has a happy way of illustrating his points with racy anecdotes, and some of the most telling parts of his speech on Monday were brought home lin this way. Ode of the most important subjeits dealt with was home defence, and we venture to think that the majority of the electorate will agree with Mr. Crosfield that the interests of the nation would be much better safeguarded ny, affJrUing every encouragement to the volunteer inuv,metir which proved its value during the War, and wtlicri has been continuously aLd shamefully neglected by the Government.</p>
        <p>The acceptance of millionaires' money for religious and charitable purposes has, a.) far, nit resulted in the question of ethics Wilt" very care- fully considered in th.s country. Riceutly it came to the front in Warrington, when the To- pont to ask Mr. Carnegie for a library donation came before the Council and, rather than argue the point, the CJUlleil shelved.the matter ou to a back shelf from whence we do not suppose it will be brought to light for some time to come. Tile vast fortunes of most millionaires are looked upon by many people as ill-gotten gains— and in many case's it is an indisputable fact that they are. The history of the amass- ing of the Carnegie millions does not make pleasant reading, and the Carnegie money is apparently no more welcome in Hull than in Warrington. Speaking at the Hull Free Cnurch Council the Rev. A. T. Guttery said the churches paid too much homage to money, and went on to chide them for bowing down at the feet of the American millionaire in order that they might secure organs cheaply. "They did not remember," he said "that the millions were amassed out of ie blood and sweat and sinew of the ill-paid toilers." Mr. Carnegie is the second richest man its America with a fortune estimated at £50,000,000.</p>
        <p>The wealthiest an of the lot is Mr. John D. Rockefeller, the much-execrated head of the Standard Oil Trust, who is reputed to possess such a pretty little penny as £100,000,000. Like Mr. Carnegie, the oil tnagnate is very anxious to dis- tribute some of his wealth among the American churches and educational institutions. Across "the pond" however the feeling against the trusts and their organisers is grJwing hourly more bitter. Rockefellet's money is not merely rejected—it is spurned with contumely. Cartoons depict him busy oiling his pathway to Heaven. The R 317. Dr. Dille of Sao Francisco ham just said publicly:</p>
        <p>. _ Until Mr. Rockefeller repents and makes restitution so far as is possible, the Christian Church had better ask him to keep his ill-gotten money and allow the conversion and enlighten- ment of the heathen to wait till we can send the message of light and love with money untainted by corruption."</p>
        <p>In another of his striking articles on "Frenzied Finance which we publish elsewhere, Mr. Henry Whitham refers at some length to this interesting question in ethics and quotes the views of pantie men on the Reel&amp;ller benefactions. We cannot resist the temptation to make extre.34 here—they are so illuminating of the moral quickening which seems to be spreading over the American people. Dr. Washington Gladden the general moderator of the Cangregational Gladden, speaking of the Standard Oil Magnate's aspirations, says : "It is undesirable that among the powers which have led the nation into the peril of a con- flict between corporate greed and industrial free- dom none has been more potent or more ruthless than that which it is now proposed to take into partnershit in missionary work." Another minister says fervently: " For myself I would rather go before God the Father to-night in the person of the manslayer who had deliberately killed his one man than in the person of the head of the Company."</p>
        <p>"'These words bite deep," adds our correspon- dent. Tney need to do. The extortion and ruth- less oppression,and the contempt for the individual rights and privileges of the people,which have beea potent factors in the building dp of these vest fortunes will someday be atoned for. But not by the founding of colleges and the furthering of missionary enterprise but by the gold-gluttons themselves. The new economic conditions which have been created by this vast wealth being in the hands of individuals are at last being realised, and the day of retribution is bound to come. It Will be here when the people awake, and even now they are slowly stretching their arms to and a way out of the deep, slumber which has bound them helpless so long!</p>
        <p>The bill which has been introduced to link up a number of our waterways has little chance of pass- ing into law this year. Fur one thing, the railway directors are too powerful in the House of Com- mons for a smooth passage to be given to what would create formidable competition. Railway companies hoid nearly onethird of the existing canals, and the little they have done with them would be a discredit to their enterprise if it did not tend to their 'profit. They cannot be blamed, therefore, though they have no reason to complain if this part of their stewardship is taken from them. Compulsory purchase is, of course, part of the scheme. The total mileage of canals already existing is about four thousand miles, but the banks of many of them are grass. grown. Beyond there is scope for much develop- ment, as the country hardly needs to be reminded. What an opportunity this would give for work for the unemployed! Mr. Keir Hardie's afforestation schemes are fantastic by comparison. It is useless to worry over the tact that foreign growers can send their agricultural and horticultural produce to London cheaper than home growers at one- fourth the distance. These things are known. The canals, with the aid of electricity, for motor power for the boats, would help us. Surely we shall wake up to the fact spon.</p>
        <p>The reduction in the consumption of beer which was noted in the presentat;on of the Budget state- ment, to the satisfaction of Temperance advocates, is having a very distinct effect up,m the brewing industry. Figures which are published in the "Brewers' Gazette 't shew that the production of beer is steadily diminishing. In the rat quarter of 1904 the production in Great Britain and Ireland was 8,076,865 barrels ; in the same quarter this year it had fallen to 7,669,293 barrels and there are indications that this falling-off 'is continuing. These figures, and others which are produced, certainly indicate a change in the habits of the masses of the population towards beer. The altered conditions of life, the spread of the popula- tion into new and healthier surroundings, and the increase of pureltiits such as cycling, which demand a steady hand and nerve, have much to do with tile tendency; nor must the efitkrts of the Temperance advocates tie forgotten. The influences which are at work are many. For instance, in rural districts institutes of various kinds help to ' keep men out of the public houses, and the re- duction of licensed houses in various districts cannot have been without effect. However much or, little each of these causes may have contributed there can be no question of the actuality of the result. The land is becoming gradually more sober.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Lady Crewe has been elected President oi the Women's Social Guild.</p>
        <p>* * Football Club secretaries and treasurers of the quality of Messrs. Ashton and Pemberton are cheap at £7O the pair! . .</p>
        <p>* * Radium is valued at £tioo,ooo an ounce, but as there is not so much in the whole world the quotation is of little value.</p>
        <p>* * * "Mr. Joseph Chamberlain is the cleverest man we have had since the death of Charles Peace" —sa we heard a ventriloquist say in Manchester the other evening. * * * The "Daily Telegraph" came out in a somewhat ill-tempered mood on Tuesday morning, and remarked "Unless some full, prompt, and ample apology is made by the Opposition through its official spokesman, we do not envy the position of the possible Leader of a Radical House of Commons." Most people will agree that an apology really is du, —but from Mr. Balfour and not from the Opposition. * * *</p>
        <p>" Twenty-three years old and working every day " is the record proudly borne by "Old Charlie," an equine servant of the Warrington Corporation which figured in the horse parade on Saturday. Surely this is a testimonial to the care which the Corporation takes of its horses; and what is more, Charlie is in first-class condition and looks equal to many more years of useful service. * * * Spiritualism and spiritualists came in for vigorous denunciation at Southport on Thursday, The" cult" was shown up, and " mediums " and other professors of the*" unseen "were stigthatised as frauds and hum- bugs. The occasion was the annual conference of the Catholic Truth Society, and amongst those present at the " exposure " of spiritualism were the Catholic Bishops of Liverpool, Shrewsbury, Nottingham, and Salford.—" Daily Dispatch." * * *</p>
        <p>Anyone who has paddled up the Dee to the Iron Bridge and back a few times, will be familiar with the swans. That those ornamental looking creatures can be fierce, however, may come as a surprise. This week one of the birds flew at a couple of young men who were boating and caused the capsizing of the craft and the ducking of the occupants. If they had been teasing the biz d,which is not an uncommon amuse- ment with some of the trippers, the mishap serves ' them right. * * *</p>
        <p>When is the trial ti ip over Light Railway no. 1— Stafford road to Victoria square, Stockton Heath—to be completed? It was commenced a few weeks ago, when several worthy councillors assembled in the hope of negotiating the swing bridge per car' for the first time. An unfeeling steamer unfortunately upset the mechanical arrangements and the trip perforce stood adjourned. People generally, and the Heathen; in particular, are more or less patiently awaiting the day when the cars will actually be seen running in the * *</p>
        <p>Our present Member cannot imagine " anything more disastrous " than that foreign affairs should be entrusted "to unknown hands from the other side cf the House." He might be surprised if he knew how many people are thinking that the " unknown hands" may be trusted not to plunge the country into a war which might be avoided, which might cost £250,000,000, and which might saddle us with a bankrupt colony. There is the not-to-be-overlooked chance, too, that the same hands may secure some of the promised but elusive £30,000,009 from the Randlords.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>* * At a time when our local legislators are being urged to take steps to have betting news "blacked out" of "the newspapers provided at the Warrington Free Library, it is interesting to note how the experiment has worked in Liverpool. A. writer in the "Liverpool Daily Post and Mercury" says :—The " blacking out" of the sporting news in the various daily newspapers hung on the frames in the Free Library, William Brown-street, for public perneal, has had the, antici- pated and desired result of keeping away from the Institution a large number of individuals who seized the papers at the morning hour, and for a considerable length of time excluded the legitimate users of that Part of the library premises. No complaints appear to have been made against the censorship which has removed the cause of ann)yance and inconvenience to .regular readers. * 5 *</p>
        <p>Addressing the members of the conservative Association, Mr. R. Pierpoint; M.P., related with gusto an Irish member's " bull" and then proceeded ' ) contradict himself. He was' essaying the difficult Task of supporting the Aliens Bill mid at the seine time upholding the privilege of oppress.d foreigners O Consider England a refuge. Naturally his steps faltered and be finally fell between two stools. Allud- ing to the Russian and Polish Jews who suffer' SO much persecution he said "although we Were sorry for them, we should not impoverish lour own people for , the sake of their Poverty." If this 'means anything it means that the Oppressed alien is welcome to our sympathy, but be lutist be content with that and not expect shelter and Protection also. But before leaving the subject our Member added. that," nothing cau.be more abominable, 'than the idea that anything i* any Bill should prevent England being. . . . the refuge for the politically Oppressed." We•have no doubt that Mr. Pierpoint's. 'anus are estimable, but he' lamentably tilled to make the Aliens Bill appear anything but a farce.</p>
        <p>FOUND THAT voR QUALITY ANTI VALIIR TN WINBi AND SPIRITS FULTONE KOS. CANNOT RE BEATEN. FULTON BROS. 20, RENSHAW STREET, LIVBRPOOL.</p>
        <p>ALSO AT FULTON'S BUILDINGS, ROCHDALE. Telephone 2261 Royal. Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>* * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Labouchere is a -funny, little, insignificant looking, pre-historic sort of individual. His idea respecting the Woman's Suffrage Bill are almost a 9 antiquated as he himself appears to be. Why should , not women be treated with justice? We are eager to have their assistance and welcome their support at election times but we are apt to ignore their demand 9 for fair and equitable representation on certain public bodies. We, personally, believe that they can help in many ways, and we hope that they will press their claims until they are acknowledged and honestly dealt with.</p>
        <p>* * * A well-deser4d compliment was paid the official s and committee of the Warrington Football Club when they were re-elected with practical unanimity. They* have proved themselves good ad faithful servants, and under their capable management the players have won the Northern Union Cup, the South-West Lanca- shire Cup, and the Combination Championship. There still remains one pinnacle of fame to aspire to--the League Championship. When Warrington have achieved this also, they will need, like Alexander, to look round for fresh worlds to conquer, but not before!' * * *</p>
        <p>The President of the Board of Trade has appointed a Committee of Inquiry on Bond Investment Com- panies, The Committee are directed to inquire as to the operations of companies—not being life assurance companies—which collect periodical payments front the industrial classes in retqrn for benefits promised in the future, and whether it is desirable that there shottld be any restrictions imposed on such companies or any Government supervision of their transactions, and to report to the Board of Trade. This inquiry' has doubtless been hastened by the series of articles which have appeared in the "Manchester Guardian," and which have been widely read and talked about. * *</p>
        <p>"Gold must be tried by fire, as the heart is tried by pain," so runs the song. Alderman Bennett is s gentleman for whom we have profound respect. We differ from him politically but we believe that he has the best interests of the town at heart and is honestly striving for "the good time coming" which he has so often sung about. The Conservatives tried the other evening to make him, a mere party man with no ideals save the soul of his own political party. That he resented it, we are not surprised. We hope that he will continue to work for the good of his town and that he will be helped by the kindly assistance of all honest progressives.</p>
        <p>* * * Warrington has long held the reputation of be; for its size, one of the worst paved towns in Lancashire. It used to be the boast of cyclists that they would know the instant when they entered the B3rough of Warrington if they were blindfolded. Now motorist; are saying the same thing. There is no doubt that at present the paving of the town is in a disgraceful state. If there is a really well-paved street in tin borough we should like to be introduced to it. The main thoroughfares are certainly awful to the man on wheels. both wood and sett-paving being, like a riddle, all holes. What with ,the streets and back passage's which are unpaved, and the streets which are badly paved, the Paving and Sewerage Committee has iti hands more than full.</p>
        <p>* * * The Labour men in Manchester are showing remarkable activity but,by striving for too much, they appear likely to lose all.We notice that Mr. J. R. Clynes is announced to contest North-East Manchester against Sir James Fergusson. This is very plucky of Mr. Clynes and his friends, but they are spoiling a "good thing," If the fight were left to the Liberals Councillor Boyle would probably stand, with excellent prospects of succesg, while the Liberals would back Mr. Kelley, the Labour candidate in South-West Manchester. Some under- standing. of this nature would probably enable the Progresswe forces to capture both seats, and the Labour party could claim South-West as their "yen own." With Mr. Clynes in the field, events will pro- bably so shape themselves that the Tories will retain' both seats. Liberal and Labour should each be prepared to make sacrifices to prevent this happening-</p>
        <p>* * * The crop of suicides and murders all over the country has, of late, become horrifying in its dimen- sions, and we cannot but come to the conclusion that many of the tragedies are due to the force of example- In fact it has been disclosed by one recent inques that the suicide's mind had been affected by the tragic accounts he had read in the newspapers, while the succession of three murders very similar in type in one district alone, points to the same conclusion - We are afraid the newspapers have a lot to answer for in this respect. The circumstantial accountF,l. which are printed of ghastly crimes Oonbtless sell papers, but they are capable of producing immense harm, and moreover they are not compatible with the lofty ideals of the British Press. There should he greater care and discrimination shown in reporting such awful events as have lately been sadly tel• common. It is not pleasant to think that a great" " daily," by • pandering to the vulgar curiosity 8;9 morbidness of thousands of its readers, may -t directly responsible for a repetition of the tragedy 1 describes.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WEER ENDING MAY 20th, 1905.</p>
        <p>During the week, the following births and deaths were registered:—Warrington Registration District births, 51, deaths, 21. The excess of births over deaths was 39. _ . _</p>
        <p>The death-rate from all causes was (final to 15.8 per 1,000 againstl9% the average of the previous 4 weeks and against 17•5 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the seven chief zymotio diseases was equal to 3.7 par 1,000, against 4.6 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 60 the corresponding period of 1904. The ages of mortality were :- 0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65—up. Total 4 6 1 2 5_ _ 3 21.</p>
        <p>The deaths included-4 from measles, 1 from enteric fever, 2 from phthisis, 1 other tubercular diseases, 1 from cancer, 1 from bronchitis, 4 from pneumonia, 7 from all other causes. . . _ . .. . .</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the wee::: :—Scarlet fever, 5 eases; erysipelas 1 case. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending May 20th, 1905, 35. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 54 Fah. and therefore 4 degrees higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 711?., was recorded on the 17th inst., and the minimum temperature 371?., on the 20th inst. The rainfall measured nil. The general direction was E.</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GORNAI.L, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
