      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 27, 1905,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 27, 1905,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>HARMONIOUS ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Cap Victory to be Farther Celebrated. Considering the unusually successful season and the admirable way in which the Warrington Football Club has been managed, it was little wonder that the annual meeting of the club which wasleld at the Co-operative Hall, Cairo-street, on Thursday evening was most har- montons. A spirit of satisfaction at the team's achievement in winning the Northern Union Cup per- vaded the meeting, and it was decided that the victory should be further celebrated by the holding of a smok- ing concert. Mr. W. R. Neil presided and was supported on the platform by Mr. H. Ashton (secretary), Mr. T. Pemberton (treasurer), Messrs. T. Bowers, W. Nevins, T. Davies, W. Hewitt, T. Barnes, and T. Locker (committee). REPORTS ADOPTED. On the motion of Mr. F. FREEMAN seconded by Mr. A. H. SANDERS the annual report and statement of accounts which appeared in last week's " Examiner " were adopted. ELECTION OF PRESIDENT. Mr. NEIL said that about two months ago he wrote to Mr. Ashton sending his resignation,thinking he had held the presidency long enoagh. ("No, no.") He had occupied the office for four seasons and he thought it was time he retired. From what he could hear however the committee had decided to leave the matter in the hands of the members. It had been a great pleasure to him to have held the presidency during the past year when they had had the honour of bringing back the , premier cup. (Applause.) Mr. ASHTON said,as Mr. Neil had stated,their president considered he had held that office long enough and would like to be relieved. The matter was before the com- mittee and they felt that they had a remarkably good president—(applause)--and one who had identified him- self closely and seriously with the affairs of the club in many matters. He could assure them Mr. Neil had been of great service to the committee. Hisa.dvice had been of great value and the committee felt that if Mr. Neil would only allow his name to be placed once more I before the members they would be only too pleased to dots°. (Applause.) On the motion a( Mr. A. Starkey, seconded by Mr. E. A. Sanders, Mr. Neil was unanimously re-elected. Mr. NEIL said he thanked the members very heartily indeed for having re-elected him, though hs thought it would have been better had he retired. As be had said he was particularly pleased to have been president during the yeakin which they had managed to bring back the senior'Cup and in which also the "A " team bad secured premier honours. He was sure it had been a most successful season and he knew from the state- ment of accounts that practically speaking, there is no better club in the Northern Union. (Applause). His duties during the year had been very light as they had. a most excellent committee—in fact ' they could not possibly have abetter or more hardworking committee (Applause). Judging from the statement of accounts and the condition of the club and from the suocess which had followed the club during the past year he was sure they could not have a better committee. (applause). Ile would endeavour at all times to do his duty as president and to further the interests of the club to the best of his ability.</p>
        <p>OTHER ELECTIONS. Mr. S. WRIGFIT moved the re-election of Messrs. if. Ashton and Mr. T. Pemberton as secretary and treasurer respectively. He was sure the club could not have two better officials. Their duties during the past year especially had been very heacy, and he did not think another club in the Union received sticks detailed state- ment of accounts as Mr. Pemberton presented. Mr. R. Lurr,EwooD seconded and the motion was carried with enthusiasm. _ . .</p>
        <p>The CHA.IRMAN said he was very pleased the meeting had taken that coarse, as he could speak from expert- ewe of the valuable services rendered by both the Secretary and Treasurer. They, would have to go a long way before they found a better treasurer' and one who was more exact and correct in his report than Mr. Pemberton. The same remark applied to Mr. Ashton in his department. Mr. E. A. SANDERS moved the re-election of Messrs. T. Wallington and W. Webster as auditors, remarking that they could not have two smarter men for the work. Mr. W. HAYES seconded and the motion was unani- mouslyagreed to. For seats on the committee all the retiring members (Messrs. T. Davies, T. Bowers, T. Locker, W. Hewitt, W. Nevins and T. Barnes) were nominated, and in addi- tion the names of Messrs A. Jones and J. W. .Plinston were brought forward. Mr. W. HABBERLEY said that Messrs. Jones and Plinston were both good men who had rendered useful services in the past. In consideration of the events of the past season and the enthusiastic way in which the committee had done their work it wbuld he a very graceful act on the part of the meeting to re-elect the whole committee co bloc. There seemed to be such a good feeling between the committee as had not existed for years. It was decided to let all the names go to the vote,and the ballott tesulted as follows ;—T. Bowers, 167; T. Locker, 165; W. Hewitt, 163 ; W. Nevins, 163; T. Davies. 155;' T. Barnes, 137; A. Jona, 34; J. W. Plinston, 33. The old committee was therefore re-elected. HONORARIUMS TO SECRETARY AND TREASURER.- 4SURER.</p>
        <p>'in the Union which had participated as Warrington had ,rte in four semi-finals and three finals. (Applause.) D iring the past season the form of,the players had been sulciently good to win practically every competition, and with a little bit of balance at one period of the season they would have occupied a most dignified posi- tion'in the League table. (Hew. hear). Throughout Oa Union the name of the • Warrington club was; re;arded with respect mid admiration. The players had bn-taught to play the game and it would be a good thing if all other clubs in the Union would adopt similar tactics. (Rear, hear and applause). Mr. T. PENEBEECTON, who on rising was received with applause, said he was extremely obliged to the members for the way in which they had received the proposal. As one got older his enthusiasm became a little less— (laughter)—and many times during the winter months he had thought he would "chuck" It.fle still had a little bit of love left for tbe club and he) would always endeavour to do his best for it. (Applause.) MEMENTO FOR TIIE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. WRIGHT moved thst the committee be thanked for their past services, and that they be given a little memento of the club having won the cup. Mr. E. ENGLAND seconded, and suggested that the memento shauld take the form of a photograph of the committee and officers of the Club—s photograph to be given to each member of the committee. Sergeant SWEENNY MOvei as an amendment that the members of the committee should be presented with a suitable medal. _</p>
        <p>A proposal to leave the matter of the selection of n memento in the hands of the cornmittee• was defeated. On the motion of Mr. HA YES, the hon. secretary and hon treasurer, and Messrs. E. A. Sanders, S. Wright. , Haves Jeffrey and Dutton, and Sergeant Sweeney were • appointed a committee to consider the question. A proposal to extend the mementq to the auditors did not meet with support, it being pointed out that If this course were taken many others, such as groundsmen ito., would be entitled to a memento. \ TRUSTEES. Messrs. Bowers and. Locker were re-elected trustees. HONORARIUM TO AUDITORS. It was decided to grant the usual honorarium of £5 to each of the auditors. FURTHER CELEBRATIONS OF THE CUP VICTORY. Mr. S. W. JEEFREY said they had had a surfeit of rejoicing over the cup victory but the members of the club had had very little share in it. The team and officials could have their great feasts here and there— (laughter)—but the members of the club would be con- tent with an ordinary "smoker." Let them have it soon, and not prolong the celebrations much longer. Mr. E. A. SANDERS seconded this proposition. ft ,sas true the members of the club had a share in the rejoicings. Champagne itc. had been flying about until they were tired of reading ;bout it. (Laughter.) See- ing they had a share in the work of the club, it was time they had "a little tuck in" at the cup. Laugh- ter.) He suggested that the Committee appointed to consider the mementoes to the committee should take the arrangements for the concert in band. This proposal was agreed to. THE PRESIDENT THANKED.</p>
        <p>- - I Mr. S. W. JEFFREY moved a vote of thanks to the President for presiding and for his services to the club during the year. Mr. Niel was not merely a "figure- head " for be did hard "graft"at tirnes.lle was not only popular with the commi:tee but with the players and members of the club in general. Hr. Niel had seen them win the Cup and he (the speaker) hoped he would retain the presidency until the club won the league championship, (Applause.) Mr. W. LITTON sezonded and the motion was carried with great enthusiasm, the company singing " For he's a lolly good fellow." The CRAM:RAN briefly acknowledged the compliment and paid a tribute tci the services of the players.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHURCH SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHURCH SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>----- Rev. Morley Stevenson and the Working</p>
        <p>of the Education Act.</p>
        <p>A DEMAMD FOR RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTION. The Rev. Morley Stevenson, principal of the Warring- ton Training College, preached a sermon on religious education at St. Peter's Church on Sunday morning. Having briefly reviewed the history of the educational work in England he asked his hearers to consider what had been done for education in Warrington by the Church of England. He had been t,yin,, to make accurate inquiries, but it was very difficult to search into past history, and to be quite accurate, but so far as he could make out, setting aside all the schools built in past days, which had been closed or fallen into decay, and taking the schoals which existed amongst us at this moment, he was not exaggerating when he said that the cost of the sites and baildings of the Churoh of England schools in Warrington at this particular nloment had not been a bit less than £50,000. That was to say, that the sum of £50,000 had been given by the Church people of the town for the religious education of children in the belief they held. • They sometimes heard the Church schools spoken of in rather a dis- paraging way, atid it was said that they did not seem to have very much claim to Church of ngland teaching. They dia not get any great meed of gratitude given for what had been done in the past. He thought they ought to realize what tiMt meant. Let them sappose that the Church people atid'other Christian bodies had not come forward. Let them suppose that all the schools in the town had had to be built in the past as they probably would be in, the future—out of the rates. They would see what a heavy burden rested on the rates. It would also be seen that the education</p>
        <p>A k iii-laidEi,E-AS E. rate of the town was going to rise very much higher Mr. S. WRIGHT thought that in view of the increased than it was now, because in. future the schools would amount of'work which had devolved upon the Secretary be built, not by private benevolence, as in the past, but and treasurer in comparison with what they had to do out of the rates, and when that came about, and they all when the honorariums were fixed at £25 each, the club groaned, as they would groan, under a heavy rate, he ought to consider the desirability of giving them addi- hoped they would understand a little better than some tional recompense for their services. The membership people seemed to do the debt of gratitude which was of the club hal coneiderablyincreased,and that entailed owing to those generous individuals who had clone so a vast amount of work on the secretary and treasurer much in the past for the religious education of the town. in collecting the subscriptions. On the whole, the club It was very astonishing to him to hear some of the was very economically worked, and he did not think things which were said about the Education Act. He there was another club in the Union that paid its saw it gravely reported that by the Act the schools had officiels such a small sum as the Warrington Club.(Hear, been handed over to the control of the clergy. Not hear). He proposed that the honorariums should be only was that not true, but the very contrary was the increased to £3.1 each. (Applause) case. What did the Act do? It had placed the whole Mr. E. V. WALKER seconded. Seeing that the club of the secular instruction in every school under the direct was a progressive one and that for some years they had control of an Education Committee, and partly of other enjoyed prosperity, it believed them to endeavour to people co-opted on the committee. No manager now, pay their officials in some way commensurate with the be he cleric or lay,had any power whatever to teach any work they did. They conld not hope to go on progress- single particular in the working of the school, except in log as they had done unless th^y made some recognition the first three-quarters of an hour. That did not look of the excellent services rendered by the secretary and like nutting the school under the sole control of the treasurer. (Hear, hear). The proposed increase would not clergy. Before the passing of the Act there were many raise the honorariumsto any large extent.but they often places where the clergyman, especially in country places, heard of clnlis paying £5O or more for a player who might was practically the only person who had the control turn out to be worth nothing at all. Theextraremnnera- of the school. That power had now been taken away. tion offered to the secretary and treasurer would be in Every school must have a certain nuraleer of managers, the nature of an investment for which club was assured generally speaking, at least six, and two must be a very sound return. (Applause). - appointed by the educationanthority to represent them. Mr. Asirroe said it was very gratifying to Mr. Here again the old system of what was called one-man Pemborton and himself to feel that the sympathies of control had been for ever set on one side, and every the members weee with them in the manner in which school was bound to have at least six managers. Once they had conducted the affairs of the club. His duties more,under the old system in the Chych of England were not so multifarious as many people seemed to school, the whole control of the religious teaching was think, although a great amount of time had to be devo- in the hands of the clergy of the parish. Now it was ted to the work throughout the season. He had behind in 'the hands of the six managers, of whom' him a committee in whom he had every confidence, and probably, the clergyman was one, but there he owed them a great debt of obligation for the manner were five other people, who, if they wished to