was thrown in the be as well to keep the club's affairs well in hand and teeth of Churchmen. "What right have you to always have a sinking fund to meet any emergency. give religious teaching in these schools when you Mr. JEFFREY regretted that it fell to his lotto bring don't contribute any money at all ?" Perfectly right opposition into what should be a very harmonious if it were true, but it was not true, In the first place meeting. Be however, thought there was not a single the Church had not giver the buildings and the site, no member of the committee who would agree with Mr. small gift, and in the second place, it was the duty of Ashton's generous offer. They thought it was sufficient the managers, out of voluntary contributions to keep honour to hold a seat on thecommittee and w erewilling the buildings in repair, and to make such alterations in to work to keep the club in the forefront withont any them as the education authority might reasonably renumeration. He thought they would be wise not to require. People very often forgot that, but if they ' adopt Mr. aViiton's suggestion and if the meeting I were the managers and had to find the money, they adopted the suggestion he took it there would not/be a I would see that it was a thing to be faced. Was it unfair single member of the committee who would accept the if the Church of England provided the sites and build- money. (Hear, hear.) He knew how willingly the ings, and spent a reasonable sum every year in repairs, committee did their work and they certainly would be that they should be ;Bowed to control the religious wise in not accepting the suggastion,generous though it teaching in the schools they had built? Well, now as was. - to the future. There were two demands made by Mr. T. DAVIES said that so far as he was concerned those who were opposed to the Church schools. and as concerned the rest of the committee, he did not First of all, they demanded that in future tne religious think it would be fair to adept the suggestion as the /teaching sheuld not he in accordance with theprinciples work of the, club had been done for nothing in years of the Church of England, but should be either what past. He did not think the work would be done so well if the committee were paid. There was not a single club in the Northern Union in which the com- mittee men were paid, and so far as he was concerned he would not accept ,payment under any consideration. (Hear, hear). Mr. WRIGHT said he steongly objectedto Mr Ashton's suggestion, and quite agreed with Mr. Jeffrey that it would be unwise to pay the committee. Mr. NEIL remarked that the committee preferred that the matter should remain as it was. Upon a vote being taken Mr. Wright's motion was unanimously carried. Mr. ASHTON said he was deeply obliged to the members, and hoped that he would be able to perform his duties with the same interest and enthusiasm as be had done in the past. (Applause.) He would use his best endeavours to promote the interests of the club and would not rest antil they had secured the League Championship. (Applause.) He considered the greatest honour was to win the Northern Union Cup and he was pleased to be able to congratulate them upon being the proud possessors of that trophy. There were few clubs</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</p>
        <p>BISCUITS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>was called "undenominationalt" or should be removed altogether. But they. as Church people, should make a firm stand against that, and he thought they could make a stand on the question of religions liberty. Why should not the children of members of the Church of England receive the teaching which their fathers and mothers believed to be true, and in which their children wished to be brought up? Why should they be sub- jected to another form of religious teaching which, although it might have its excellences, was not that, which they considered to be the best? Did they say it I did not matter? Did they say as people sometimes said," We don't want to bother little children's heads about such things as these ? " That was a very shallow objection. Did they think they should not like to teach the children about holy baptism' the great days of the church year, about Christmas, Easter, Ascension Day, Whit- sunday, and other things? Other people did uot acknow- ledge these days. That was what undenominational teaching meant, and so he could go on, but he wanted I to show that, without perplexing the heads of little children with deep and difficult doctrines. which no sensible person wished to do, there were many subjects they would like to talk to them about. If they had undenominational instruction they would not be allowed to do that. In the name of religious liberty that ought not to be so, and every person in this country, whatever his belief might be, should have the right to have his children taught in that belief. He would give the same liberty to all that he claimed for his own Church bathe did protest against any system being introduoed ,into the schools and forced upon all, whether it coiumended itself to them or not. The other point claimed by those who were opposed to their schools was that there ought to be no religions tests for teachers. That meant to say that when they ,ppointed a head teacher to a Church:of England school they must not say to him, "Are you a member of the Church of England ?" but take him whatever he might be. That seemed to ,him (the preacher) to be in opposition not only to justice, but to common-sense. Did they think the Church of England people in that parish would like to see a Roman I Catholic head teacher at the head of the school ? Was that just, right or fair to the people who' had built the school? Would they like to see at the head of the school one of those Noncon- formists of !whom he hoped there were very few, who would prejudice the minds of the children against the Church of England? Would they like to see at the head of the school—he had known instances—a man who did not believe in the Bible at all? Did it seem not only common-sense but justice that they should see put at the head Of the school a person who was in sympathy with the religious teaching to promote which the school was founded? He was persuaded that the Church of England people of this country, if they would only hold together and be reasonable, conciliatory, charitable and just, would get their fair demands. They had a right, in recognition of what they were prepared to do in the future, in the name of religious liberty, to demand that their children should be taught what they believed to be true. Oa that he hoped they would all stand firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington sadly disappointei their followers on Saturday, for in the third League match of the season they had to bow the knee to St. Helens. The encounter at the glass and pill centre attracted a large crowd and it H apparent that the newly lorm3d Leagu.3 is making rapid strides in public popularity. The home eleven were in great form and triumph 1 by a majority of 82. It is evident that League honmrs are to be keenly competed for, and Warrington will hava to be on their mettle if they intend to cover themselves with glory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WORKS COMPETITION'.</head>
        <p>WORKS COMPETITION'.</p>
        <p>The Works Competition continues to attract a large number of spectators to the King and Queen Hotel green., and on Saturday a large crowd watched farther progress which was made in the first round of the com- petition for the Finlayson cup. The most interesting game lay betwem teams r.presenting Thewlis and Griffiths and Walkers Brewery. The teams were evenly matched and each in turn claimed the lead, the first named comhination in the end qualifying for the</p>
        <p>---- I second round. . The file-smiths stated w-elcfoarteen Winning the toss, Warrington batted fit and put points being recorded in their- favour as the together 131. The highest scorer WAS W. H. Pod-1 result of the first game. J. Dagnall, jun., how- more whose 38 Was the result of viggl•ous hitting. ever allowed that lead to be reduced. Against E. A. Connor, the Warrington captain, also did good J. Bolton he never looked like winniug and 12 points service and was not out with 26 to his credit. It was separated the players after the last end. Matters did a singular feature that no fewer than bar wa'rring- not improve from the point of view of Thewlis's men ton batsmen found themselves out for infringing the as A. Fidler by defeating J. Dagnal, s3n., gavei I.b.w. rule. In one or two instances th 3 accuri.cy of I Walkers' the lead by 8 points. The nertgame proved the umpire's decision was questioned but, without equally disheartening and as a result Walker's claimed going into these particular cases, the f set tem 'ins lan advantige of 19 points. IL Holleroft however came that the attractiveness of cricket is lessened when 'to the rescue of his team thouzlt at first it appeared batsmen play the bill with their pads, and the practice' as though he was to suffer the same fate as his should be discouraged. I colleagues. ,</p>
        <p>The St. Helens club had the assistance of F. H. Sugg, who some years ago gained. distinction by his achievements with the Lancashire eleven. Thera ha 9 been considerable comment on the fact that this well- known cricketer hid not played for qt Helens for several years and the point is one which the exectitive wonld do well to decide for the guidance of other clubs. The home innings realized 213, the finest bitting display being that of W. H. Ward wlao ran up 46. There were several other \ notable scores, S Urmson and -L. March each making 31, Horne 32, and ISugg 29 (not out).</p>
        <p>Atoka sixth end his onnonent, J. Richarlion, lead 7-2. Up to this point Holcroft htd been ill at ease not he then got into the run of the green and went to his points before Richardson could add another chalk to his score. The totals stool at 42-53 in favour of Walker's when W. Hodcroft and J. Titchard took the green. Hadcroft opened with two doubles to which Titchard replied with a single. Two doubles gave Walker's men the lead when likderoft went t 3 8-5. three ends later the lead was reserved and stood at 9-8: Th 9 next end want against Titchard who how- ever role to the occasion and by SMI9 splendid work ran out winner by 15-11.</p>
        <p>Warrington second sustained a-iothor reverie, St Helens defeating them on their own around by 48. Loose fielding by Warrington resulted in many Irma being given away, while the bowling of P. P. Neville and E. Dennett prevented mmy of the local batsmen from making headway, E. Pierpiint,however, gqire a highly creditable displev, his total being 4L G. Neivbourne also batted well for his 22.</p>
        <p>• The file-smiths hopes were s nnewhere in the region of zero but their tail enl I”gtn t. waz and to them the game began t-) wear a mere rosy aspect.W.ll4yneux was the ctuse of this change of feeling as by defeating J. Millman 15-1, he reduced Walkers' lead to 1. The nest game pineazl them in a better plsition still F. Dicilyneut running away ftqm Baxter 15-4, bring- ing the totals to 83-73 in favbur of Thewlis's. Still victory was not assured and L. Aukland placed the issue still further in dmbt. Against 11. Potter he peeled at 4, 7 and,9 and then carried the score to 15-9.</p>
        <p>Warrinzton S3coad3ry School exponenced the hardest of luck on Souirdiy. Th 9 motet with Ligh Grammar School pr vet-1 exciting and in the end the Leighites were victorious by a single run. There wa3 no heavy scoring on either side, H. Houghton (1() making the best effort for the Secondary School.</p>
        <p>At this psriod with three men still to play four paints separated dm teams. J. Lawton fell an easy victim to R:Dagnalt and Walkers' hopes of ulthhate victory were not particularly strong, bat J. Howard improved the situation and left T. Wells the task of winning by 15-5 to give his team the lead. Unfortunately for Walkers Wells was pitted against Ball sen., an old player who generally gives a good account of himself. Oa Saturd ly he was in excellent form aid winning 15-9 Thewlis team claimed the laurels of a hard fought gams by 15 points.</p>
        <p>Monks, Hall and Co's. team had the satisfaction of butting their" P and K" rivah by 22 runs. The extent of the scoring is indicated by the fact that not a single player reached double figures.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills ware fortunate in averting defeat on Saturday. Their opponents from Glazebury proved to be in much superior farm with the, bit and declared their innings after scor- ing 122 for the loss of 9 wickets. Sankey, just saved the situation their score at the close being 48 with 9 wickets down. Mather bowled vary effectively for Glazebury, while Mather and Wood gave a capital display with the bat. .• Cheshire Lines Engineers experienced a rude shock at St'. Helens, the St. Thomas' eleven trouncing them by a majority of 70 runs. The Engineers' batsmen made a surprisingly poor show against the deliveries of Smith and Stanley, no double figures being recorded —in fact the total scare was only 15. _ Messrs. ,Peter Walker and Son's eleven received their annual visit from the firm's Barton team on Saturday, and defeated them in ridiculously easy fashion. For th 3 hoar, team J. Min batted remark- ably well, making a grand score of 62 runs before he was dismissed by an excellent catch at mid-off. W. Norman and J. Dargan both bowled well, the former taking fi'vrickets for 7 runs and the latter taking 4 wickets for 12 runs.</p>
        <p>The other two matches were n3t productive of such excitement as in neither was the issue in doubt after the first faw games. Richmonds were pitted against P. and K. (No. 3) team—a te un composed of young snd promising players who as events proved however Jacked experience. Richrnonds started by obtaining a lead of 7,points. P. and K. next :claimed the advan- tage by a single chalk. From this paint matters went in favour of Richmonds who won every game and the match by 56 points.</p>
        <p>Orford at first ran the C.L.C. team very close. A lead of 13 points which they gained as the result of the first game was reduced to one of 3 and then lost altogether. C.L.C. took up a commanding position and though their lead varied and was once or twice reduced their players held the upper hand throughout ultimately winning by 31)k points. The most interesting game of the match lay between N. Carrington and W. Lingley. Lan,gley opened with two singles', when his opponent carried the score to 4-2. At the tenth end L ingley led by 7-6. Three ends later the pair peeled, drawing level ag tin with the score 13 all. Two singles to Langley, however, placed another victory to the credit of die C.L.C. team.</p>
        <p>To-day three matches are down for decision in which till competing teams will be St. Helens Cable, Rylands' Netting, Whitecross (No. 2) • and Bylands' (No. 2),ancl Whiteeross (No. 1) and Castle Rubber. The second match will be a particularly stiff, one. In the other two matches the probible winners are White- cross and St. Helens Cable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>It is evident that this season will witness a most exciting.strutazle for the championship of the War- rington and District League. It is generally under- stood that the competition will rest miinly between last year's leaders, and by defeating the champions on Saturday, Vie Bull's Head players are already enter- taining visions of wreaths of laurel, and the capture of the enviable position at tin head of the League. It is not as though their hopes in this direction were not justified, for they have done what no other League team has done, and have every reason to feel a little proud of SAturday's display, as it is no easy thing for any combination to keep their .colours flying after a 1, visit to the Cemetery Hotel green. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LENGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LENGUE.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery club have not opened their season particularly well, rather to the contrary, an d their defeat at home on Saturday . came as a surprise to them as well as to everyone comforted with the League. It was generally expected that the Bull's Head would give the champions a close game but it was not expected, except perhaps by themselves, that they would secure the victory. Berhaps their deter- mination to .win influenced the result of the game, at any rate they went down intending to give a good account of themselves and succeeded beyond their ondest expectations.</p>
        <p>Last year they were beaten by exactly 20 points— the margin with which they won on Saturday—and' also fell victims to the dexterity of the Cemetery trundlers wlibn playing on their own green. Their defeat at home lost them the chance of the champion- ship. Their victory on Saturday, while being in the nature of a revenge, places them in an excellent posi- tion and it is some odds already on their occupying the leading position at the close of the season.</p>
        <p>Apart from its bearing upon the championship. Saturday's encounter was most attractive by reason of the fact that what might be described as the pick of Warrington bowlers participated. G. Ball Ben. and J. Allison, two old and capable trundlers were the that to try conclusions and the game going against</p>
        <p>the home man the Bulls' Head team took a lead of 3 points. The next game further improved their posi- tion. The home man. P. Mier, started well and ran to 7-5 and 8-0. R. Fairbrother drew level at 8 and the players again peeled at 12 and 13, when IFairbrother carried off the game with two singles. ' W. Swanton was in arrears throughout his game with A. Bird, and the third victory was recorded for the Church-street men. G. Ball, junr., fell a victim to J. W. Worsley, and with four games won the visitors led by 10 points. T. Bolton raised the hopes of the homesters ,in his game wits E. Shaw. Starting with two doubles and a single he went to 5-1 and to 94 Shaw made a great effort to equalize, but was unable to do so, the final score being 15-10. Tile Bull's Head, however, were not to be despoiled of their lead which Gleave, by defeating G, Holbrook, increased to one of 11.</p>
        <p>T. Ellison caused them a little uneasiness by defeating H. Clare 15-9. R. Rothwell pr wed too strong for J. W. Jolley and beating his man 15-10 the teams were placed more level. J. Harrison at first looked as though he was going to give the Cemeteiy men the lead, but Swanton by some really splendid bowling brought about a contrary result. The pair peeled at 5, from which point Htrrison went to 13-6. Swanton then monopolized the scoring, going to 11-13, and then to 14-14. The last point went to to the Bull's Head man. W. Gibson was never g:ven a chance by F. Berry who gave his team a lesd of 16 points. T. Berry had no more success than his colleagues, losing .by 10-15. He opened well and went to 7-3 bat Shaw drew level four ends later. This placed the result of the match beyond doubt, tbugh E. Taylor reduced the lead of the Bulls Head to one of 20 after a close struggle with J. Worsley.</p>
        <p>Poor Pcmketh • Park! To be defeated twice the same day is hard enough. • but when to this is added the fact that both . reverses were sustained on their own green their misfortune is intensified. They have indeed fallen upon evil times and unless their play improves one wonders what will happen when ther, visit—say the Bulls Head green. I have been privately it formed that 100 points will be the extent of their defeat on the Fearnheacl green to-day: There is. however a doubt, about it. Penketh Park are smarting under Siturday's reverse and mean to give a better account of themselves and to show that Saturday's was not their true form. •</p>
        <p>The position of the clubs up-to-date is : Fearnhead 3 3 0 0 .457 —424 ... 6 Sankey 3 . 2 I . 0 .470 ...404 ... 4 Balls Read 9 1 , 1 0 .303 288 2 Cemetery 3 1 2 0 ...4t8....442 2 Horse &amp;Jockey 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 0 ...289 ...308 ... 2 Penketh 3 0 3 0 .. ..• .394 489 0 e'</p>
        <p>To-day three matches will be played and in each case the home team shonid win.. The matches are Cemetery v. Horse and Jockey; Farmer's Arms v. Penketh Park; Bulls Head v. Chapel House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Cycling</head>
        <p>Cycling</p>
        <p>• —r On Saturday evening if r. Gorge Rigby, head game- keeper to Sir Richard Brooke, of Norton Priory, met witha som3what serious accident. He was riding his bicycle.on the pith leading from Norton to Runcorn, on the right bank of the Ship \CAnal. In erossing the rude bridge Which spans the Halton Brook the machine skidded or caught SOlll3 ob3truction, end Mr. Rigby was thrown violently to the ground. Some 'men who witnessed the accident ran to his aid, and 'found him stunned. He quickly recovered. He sustained a very severe cut under the chin, and Dr. Carruthers, of Halton, put several stitches in the wound.</p>
        <p>The weather was again bnutifully fine on Saturday, but the dustiness of the roads detracted samewliat from the pleasure of cycling. Twenty-two members of the Warrington Club cycled to Chester and on arriving partook of tea at the Hop Pole Hotel. After- wards the places of interest in the quaint old city were visited, and several membsrs took ths opportunity of enjoying a sail up the Dec, which at present looks at its best. The journey to Warrington was accom- plished in good time after a very successful meeting.</p>
        <p>One of the prettiest rides within easy distance of Warringtln is to Budworth v a Arley, and about thirty members of the club took part in the run to these picturesque Cheshire places. on Tuesday evening. short stay -was made at Budworth and Warrington was reached at, eleven o'clock after a pleasant evening,.</p>
        <p>To-day's run is to Cranage and on Tuesday Halton Castle will be visited, -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>Address by Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>MINISTERS AND NATIONAL AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>An event which is always looked forward to with con- siderable interest and pleasure is the Sunday School Anniversary in connection with Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church. This year's celebrations, which took the form of special services last Sunday, will be remem- bered as highly successful. The preacher for the day was Mr. William Smith (J.P., for the County of Derby- shire and the esteemed president of the Local Preachers' Mutual Aid Association). Mr. Smith is the foremost and most widely-known Wesleyan layman in the Midlands, and has ever been a generous supporter of the Methodist institution. His eloquent discourses on Sunday were highly appreciated by the large congrega- tions. The morning service was, as usual, held in the church while the afternoon and evening services took place in the Parr Hall. Special music was rendered by the scholars and choir. In the morning the anthem "Come unto Him " (Gounod) was sung, while at. the afternoon musical service an exceptionally creditable programme was submitted. Mr. A. H. Crest:told pre- sided and delivered an interesting address which is reported below. The hall was crowded in the evening when the service included several hymns by the scholars and the hymn "Come and Rest" by the choir. Mr. Smith in a powerful sermon dwelt upon England's greatness among nations which he attributed to Christianity. He instanced the lives of John Wycliffe, Oliver Cromwell, and John Wesley, as showing the influence of Christisas revivals upon the country. In asking for support on behalf of the Sunday schools, he warmly commended the work which these institutions were doing in instill- ing Christian teaching into the rising generation. The collections for the day realized the handsome sum of MO Is. 43.</p>
        <p>MUSICAL SERVICE IN THE PARR HALL. ADDRESS BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>The musical service held in the Parr Hall in the afternoon was one of the most pleasing features of the anniversary. The hall was well filled, and the school children occupied seats on the platform gallery. Mr. A. EL Crosfleld presided and an address was also delivered by the Rev. G. 11. Carnson, superintendent minister of the Circuit.</p>
        <p>The CHU MI AY, having thaoked his friends for their kindness in asking him to preside on such an interesting occasion, said he was sure the very beautiful sight of the benches immediately behind him would encourage all present to give the best support they could afford to the object they had in view. For his o'wn part, he did not stand there 33 a sectarian. He had been educated as a member of the Church of England while, on the other hand a line of ancestors which, in its modest way in the North of England had been traced back t s the early part of the sixteenth century, w Nonconformist. He for one could msver forget all: IL Nonconformists had done in developing the grand-xi, Ii tions of English liberty and in the formation of British character. It struck him when he was asked to take that honoured position, that some of his friends would say" What are you doing on a platform with ministers of religion? Yon are nothing better than a wretched politician." He heard it moreoften pat the other way, ministers of religion being rebuked for venturing to take part in polities. Years ago he was much impressed by a memorable and splendid speech which Mr. John Morley delivered at I Dundee. t was just about the time when Mr. Bryce introduced his Access to Mountains Bill, and Mr. Mor- ley said the time would come when the people in this beloved island of ours would see and know more of the glories of the mountain and the moor, and when alert , would be happier, and happier in better ways. He added —and he (Mr. Crosffeld) remembered it well—that when the statesman and politician had done all that could be done for men and women in our country, there would still remain the major part of the work to be done by the good men and women who had a patriotic and a noble desire to assist their fellow-townsmen a,md</p>
        <p>their fellow countrymen. That seemed to draw a dis- tinction between the work that was to be done by ministers of religion and that which was to be done by people taking an active part in public affairs. But he thought Mr. Morley would himself be the last man to suggest that any hard and fast line of that kind should be drawnibecause he (Mr. Crosfield) did not see how lany minister of religion, whatever his creed might be, I was going to preach to them of a happy state in the future, and at the same time ignore the condition of people in this present world. How could a man preach of a high spiritual condition to a drunkard? /low was a man going to hold out, with any hope of practical result, a high ethical standard to someone who had been born in one of the worst FIIIIII9 iii London.whe had, perhaps, lived with three or four other families la the same room? He, for his own part, could not but think that, of all people in the land, it must be brought home most to a minister of religion, how mind and matter, and body and soul were inextricably bound up together. There was another question which concerned him as one who took a great interest in politics, and which, surely, must appeal to all members of I,Toncon- formist congregations, and that was the great principle of toleration ; because their Nonconformist ancestors were the people who had won for them the best tolera- tion which they found in England. The question of peace was also one on which it seemed to him they could not draw the dividing line. He had spoken to the best of his poor abilities many a time froln the standpoint of a man who thought that peace was the greatest of British interests. He had, however, never spoken from the point of view of what WAS called " peace at any price" man. There were just wars, and, hideous as war was, it was sometimes necessary. The most fearful war the aaorld ha,d witnessed in modern times,with the possible excep- tion of the terrible struggle now being being waged in the East, was the straggle between the North and the South in America, which cost one million of white lives- He believed that the sober opinion of our people here and of the majority of people across the water was that that war, with all its fearful sacrifice, and its long drawn agony extending over four years, was a great con- test that. had to be fought out. it was a contest. on behalf of liberty as against slavery. Then they had the great struggle of Oliver Crom- well himself in our country, and he believed they all felt that England would have been much the worse if Cromwell had not fought that great battle on behalf of liberty and progress. It was a fact they had to con- sider that, when all was said and done, three out, of four of all the wars that were waged were unnecessary wars. Take, for instance, the awful tragedy in the Far East. Did they not all know that those irresponsible magnates, thoae powerful people in the Russian empire, who were mainly responsible for the war, had brought on a conflict which never ought to have occurred? He was quoting the other day a description of the life of the Russian peasants from whom the taws were squeezed as one would squeeze blood out of a stone to pay for that ruinous conflict, and the very reliable author of that description said distinctly that the life of those poor people among the peasants in Russia was worse than the average life of any brutes in the field. When they considered the accounts they had had from Port Arthur of men in the charnel-house outside that besieged fort, raising their hands two or three days after the battle in order that succour might be brought to them when no succour could be brought ; when they thought of those people suffering That parching thirst which scorches in the breath Of those who die the soldier's fiery death he believed they would realise that unfortun- ately amongst mankind there were agonies and tortures suffered far beyond anything to which they would find a parrallel amongst the brute creation. People said "Oh yes, that is the fortuns of war," but there was something worse than that,and it was another matter which, he thought, concerned every Christian congregation in the land. They saw in countries which were in a state of revolu- tion but not at war, inflicted upon their fellow Christians not in units or tens, but by thehundred and the thousand, even tens of thousands, tortures for which they could only had a parallel if they went back to the history of the Inquisition and the echoing cells of the middle ages. He would like to know if that kind of thing did not directly concern every minister of the Gospel and every Christian congregation. Such events as the horrors on the Congo, and the iniquities perpetrated in Asia Minor, Macedonia and other parts of Europe were, he said, distinctly our concern, because while the well-being of our population in England was our first consideration, it was not our first and last con- sideration. Wo owed a duty to other populations. especially where we had incurred exceptional responsi- bility. When people told him that no clergyman had any right to meddle in any shape or form with states- ittanshiP, it struck him that a minister of the Gospel was not doing the highest work and was not fulfilling his mission in the loftiest manner by shutting himself off from the stream of current human events, by living in a religious sanctity of his own, and failing to face the difficult and in some cases terrible problems that lay before us. A man and a preacher,who was also a great teacher fulfilled his office in the highest manner whenhe did hear full in the ear the soued of the cry of humanity craving ceaseless succour from her children. The Rev. Q. H. Carnson delivered an interesting address, his theme being God's love for humanity. The musical programme was most enjoyable and reflected credit upon all concerned. The scholars joined in singing a number of special hymns, Mr. T. Brough , conductintt. The choir rendered Goamod's anthem . "Come unto me" with taste and feeling. Mr. E. G. Rickers ably presided at the organ. Messrs. Crosfield's Prize Band was in attendance and under the conductor ship of Mr. W. Hallivrell gave several selections in a ' manner which quite maintained its high reputation.</p>
        <p>A Durham telegram etates that after twelve years' search a firm of London solicitors have traced Richard Roberts, a Durham sandwich man, as the next-of-kin and heir to a wealthy tailor, who died at Malvern, leaving an estate said to be worth a million sterling,</p>
