      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. _ -- Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. _ -- Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>- Moxiasv.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Aldermen Francomb and Smethurst, Councillor Wilkinson, Messrs. .T. W. Hayward. G. Pilling, H. Thornton, S. Wright, W. Roberts, and L. W. Reynolds. A. Rocett CHARAcrett.—Peter Skully, 1, Jolley's- court, was charged with having been drunk and dis- orderly at Market-gate on Saturday night. He was further charged with wilfully breaking five panes of glass and an electric globe in a cell at the police station. —P.C. Hayes said the prisoner was drunk and he re- quested him to go away, but he refused and used very bad laeguage.—P.C. Leonard said he heard a breaking of glass in the cells, and found that Skully had broken live panes of glass and an electric globe. He had to put the handcuffs on him to prevent further damage being done.—Skully denied being drunk, and said he had been locked up for nothing. He broke the windows so that he should be locked up for something.—Prisoner who made his 38th appearance was sent to gaol for two months with hard labour. , A PRISONERS'S THANKS.—'rnoraas Maloney, 14, rriars'-green, was charged with having been drunk in Castle-street early on Sunday morning.—P.C. Redding- ton said he found the prisoner lying on the footpath in Castle-street helplessly drunk. He found in his possession £9 and a whisky bottle.—Maloney was fined , 55.. and on leaving the dock thanked the constable for ' arresting him. WARRINGTON'S BLACK-LISTER AGAIN DRUNK.— John Wilfred Delooze, 93, Sharp-street, Warrington's only blacklister, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street on Saturday night.—Detective Clough said he saw the prisoner drunk and he could scarcely walk. He cautioned him and told him to go home, but the prisoner used very bad language.—Superintendent Monaghan said this was the prisoner's 37th appearance.— A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed, or in default fourteen days' imprisonment. CRCELTY TO A HORSE.--John Cross, 47, Horsemarket street, was summoned for illtreating a horse by causing, it to be worked whilst in an unfit state on May 15th.— Insp-ctor Gibson, R.S.P.C.A., said he saw a boy driving a mare in Church-street on the date named. It was ' very lame on the off fore leg, and was suffering from inflammation of the fetlock and spraine tendons. Cross was fined 10s. and costs. TUFSDAY.—Before Mr. H. Roberts (Presiding), Messrs. .T. R. Young, W. Richsrdson, and C. Jackson Holmes. THEFT OF A CYCLISTS'S TOOLS.—Williara Rankin, , Bank-street. was charged on remand with stealing a hicychit outfit, the property of James Pollitt, valued at 3s.—Superintendent Monaghan said that on Thursday morning Mr. Pollitt, who was employed by Messrs. Richmond and Company, left his bicycle in the office lobby. The bicycle bag contained an oil can, a spanner and a repairing outfit. On Thursday morning the prisoner went to a pawnshop in Mersey-street and offered the goods for sale. He said he had been carry- ing a parcel to Battersby-lane, and as the man could not , pay him his wife gave him the articles. He (the super- intendent) arrested the prisoner the same day, and ' charged him with the theft, and he said: "I found them in Buttermarket-street."—Prisoner, who pleaded not guilty, was sent to gaol for one month with hard labour. ASSAULT ON A CONSTABLE.—Thomns Gallop, School- brow, was charged with having been drunk and dis- orderly in School-brow, on Tuesday afternoon. He was further charged with 2.ssmElting P.C. Cooper.—P.C. Cooper said that as he was passing along School-brow he saw prisoner leaning against a shop. Prisoner asked witness what he wanted, and struck him a blow on the chest. Witness then closed with him and Gallop became very violent and butted witness with his head, He also kicked out right and left.—Prisoner, who made his 17th appearance. was fined IN. and costs, or in default 14 days' iraprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SEQUEL TO AN ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO AN ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Claim for Damages at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., William Bite, wiredrawer, brought an action against Charles Ashton, 22, Cartwright-street, to recover £25 damages for injuries sustained, he alleged, through the negligence of the defendant. Plaintiff was represented by Mr. T. S. Steel, Mr. A. Browne appearing for the defendant. Mr. Steel stated that the plaintiff as employed by the Whitecross Company Limited, and on the 13th of March he let his work at about 5.30 and he and some companions went to the Railway Inn and had two glasses of beer. He left the Inn and was walking in the direction of Priestly-street he being on the outside of the footpath. To reach his home it was necessary for the plaintiff to cross the roadway and he was stepp- ing off the footpath when the shaft of the float which the defendant was driving struck him in the chest and knocked him down, the float passing over his legs. Defendant was put on the float and taken home, beinr, subsequently taken to the Infirmary whore it KaS being that his shoulder had been broken. Defendant was driving very carelessly and on corning down Frogball bridge thrashed his horse. He was driving along the right hand side of the road when be .uddenly swerved knocking the plaintiff to the ground. Plaintiff earned £2 per week and as a result of the accident had been unable to follow his work for eight weeks. Under cross-examination plaintiff admitted that he had drawn ,E 1 in sick pay while he was off ill. He had about three weeks ago seen the defendant and asked him for £2 to settle the matter. Mr. Steel: You saw the defendant before your claim was entered? Witness : After .the claim was entered. . .</p>
        <p>His Honour held that in face of Plaintiff's statement he could not succeed. Mr. Browne said that the defence was that the plaintiff had been guilty of negligence. As he had been hurt, however, the plaintiff agreedto give him .£2. Mr. Steel said that the plaintiff's statement came as a surprise to him. The case was withdrawn with the consent of his Honour.</p>
        <p>Chat Moss Ablaze. About midnight on Saturday the Leigh and Glaze- bury Fire Brigades were summoned to a great fire on Chat Moss, near Windy Bank Farm, Glazebury; three miles from Leigh. The flames extended for nearly half a mile and lighted up the heavens for miles around. It was a wonderful sight, and thousands of people gathered to watch the spectacle. The firemen were useless, as no water eould be found anywhere near,,and the fire had to burn itself out. It was burning until yesterday afternoon. The damage to trees, shrubs, market produce, and game preserves War very large. The cause of the fire is unknown.</p>
