      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. F. SLATER. Twenty Years Vicar of St. James', Latchford.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. F. SLATER. Twenty Years Vicar of St. James', Latchford.</p>
        <p>The announcement of the death of the Rev. Francis Slater, who for twenty years was vicar of St. James', Latchford, will be received with regret by many War- ringtonintis. It is six years since the deceased clergy- man, who was much esteemed by his parishioners, left Warrington on exchanging livings with the Rev. F. E. Powell who was then Vicar of St. Chad's, Over. While ' working in this ancient Cheshire parish in [ 1901 he sustained a great bereavement in the death of his wife, and shortly afterwards he resigned the living. Accepting a chaplaincy at Heidel- berg in Germany, he retained that 'appointlnent until April 1904 when he.returned to England and became the chaplain,to the Sisterhood of St. Thomas at Oxford. For some time he had been in failing health and he passed away at Oxford on Monday at the age of fifty- nine. He leaves two sons and two daughters with whom much sympathy is felt. The deceased gentleman was a native of Now Mills, Derbyshire, his father, Mr. Leigh Slater' being a well-known cotton spinner. His earlier days were spent at Mr. Perrin's School, in Man- chester, and afterwards he travelled in Egypt and Palestine. On returning from the East he went to [ Brunswick, Germany, where he studied for Oxford. He matriculated at Queen's College, Oxford, in January 1867, and took his degree in 1870, but while reading for honours in the Law and Modern History School his health broke down. Fortunately, however, he obtained a travelling tutorship, and spent a long time in Italy and Switzerland, greatly benefiting from the change. [On his return he was ordained by the late Bishop [ Jacobson, of Chester, and licensed to the curacy of ' the Parish Church of Wigan, where he remained for three and a half years with the Rey, the Hon. G. T. 0. Bridgman. Soon after his ordination he was married to the youngest daughter of the late Rev. Theophilus Williamson, of the Groves, Chester. Leaving Wigan Mr. Slater went for a short time to Coatham,in the North Riding of Yorkshire, but feeling that his vocation was mission work, he accepted a curacy with the Rev. J. C. Hordern, of Holy Trinity Church, Bury. Mr. Slater was a staunch *Ellett Churchman and it was mainly through the influence of the Rev. Canon Knox-Little and the late Mr. John Tinsley, who were anxious to obtain the services of a clergy- man of his views in Warrington, that. he was appointed vicar of St. • James' Latchford, in 1878, by the late Rev. Canon Greenall, of Grappenhall. The services at St. James' at that time were conducted With the minimum of ritual, the black gown being in urn. Under Mr. Slater's care a great change was gradu- ally effected, and when he resignecl.the living the church had come to be looked upon as the most advanced " in Warrington. The church itself. too, underwent a [ transformation, the galleries being removed and a scheme of renovation carried out at a cost of over £l,OOO. The present commodious schools, which provide accommo- dation for GOO scholars, were also erected. When tho late Mr. George Artingstall's house became vacant the late Sir G. Gree.nall bought it and presented it to the, living as a vicarage, while the old vicarage and land in Knutsford-road were sold on chief for the benefit of the living. Sir Gilbert also increased the endowment. !qr. Slater, throaghout his connection with Latchford, applied himself with great zeal to the discharge of his parochial duties, end even those who did not hold with his views recognized his conscientiousness.</p>
        <p>The vaccinatiou officer for the Oldham Union ha.s been instructed by an inspector of the iLocni Oovernment Board to prosecute parents whose children have not been vaccinated, unless they can show a certificate for exemption. Widnes has christened one of the streets in the vicinity of the docks "Little Mary-street."</p>
        <p>along your letter, and I will try and find room 'or it in "The Children's Corner."</p>
        <p>Now, for something VERY IMPORTANT. Open your eyes and your ears as wide Is pos- sible. At the foot of this letter you will find a coupon by which you can join "The Children' Corner League." My aim is to band together all the children who read this journal into a great brigade of mutual helpfulness. There are at present other branches of the League being formed in various parts of the king- dom, but I want the one in your own district to be as strong and vigorous as any. Will you do your best? Will you join yourself, and get others to joint Any young people can join who are not more than sixteen years of age. I in- tend offering valuable prizes to those who ob- tain the most members during the next four weeks. Begin with yourself. Cut out the coupon below, write your name and address plainly, add your age, and get your father or mother or guardian to add their name; then post it, addressed to "Uncle Ned," at the office of this paper. The names of new members will appear each week, and their letters will be printed from time to time. It costs nothing to join, and you will receive many advantages from so doing. Now, dear children, ,I must finish my letter this week. Let me know that you have read it. With all good wishes from</p>
        <p>Yow affectionate UNCLE NED.</p>
