      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Mr DEAR CHILDREN,</head>
        <p>Mr DEAR CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>You will see by this that it has been decided that in future you should have a corner all to yourselves, so that this it your "very own" part of the paper, and I promise to try and make it as interesting as possible. It is always nice to have something all to ourselves, isn't it? Why, when mother turns out an old drawer, or box, or cupboard, and says that you can have it all• for your toys and books, don't you feel just delighted? You set about and arrange it all at once. You havn't got to please anyone but yourself, so you always suc- ceed in doing it. Now, I want you all to know and to remember that this is "your corner," and you can have it arranged just how you like. If you find in it what pleases you, write me a little letter and tell me so. If there are any other stories or subjects you would like to read about, get out that writing-desk of yours and let me know all about it. If you have got a pet animal that helps to make you happy, send</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PUZZLES FOR PLAYTIME.</head>
        <p>PUZZLES FOR PLAYTIME.</p>
        <p>I will give a PRIZE OF/FIVE SHILLINGS to the reader under 16 who sends the best and zeatest answers to the following:- 0 1. Square Word. _ A Boy's Name. A Period of Time. Another Name for Father. 2. Riddle-Me-Ree.</p>
        <p>My iiiFt is in JAMES, but not in MOLLY; My second is in PRAM, but not in DOLLY; My third is in PAPER, but not in,WOOD;, My fourth is in BAD, but not in GOOD; My fifth is in NOISE, but not in SHOUT; My whole is a land you've all read about.</p>
        <p>3. Jumbled Boy's Names. MITRE&amp; REIC. HELAARF. NIDELA. MSHTAO. 4. Jumbled Girl's Names. NANOETCCS. SAGYLD. ROCLEFEN. • TEMGRARA. VIOLE. INAROM. 5. Diamond. A CONSONANT, AN ANIMAL WEALTH. A FISH. A CONSONANT.</p>
        <p>All solutions must be sent, with full name, address, and age of competitor, to " Uncle Ned," at the office of this paper, within fourteen days.</p>
        <p>SPEAK KINDLY.</p>
        <p>A Iran once saved a very poor boy from drowning. After his restoration he said to him, "AN hat can I do for you, my boys" "Speak a kind word to me sometimes," replied the boy, the- tears gushing from his eyes. "I ain't got a mother, like some of them." A kind word! Think of it. This man bad it in his power to give that boy money, clothes, and playthings, but the little fellow craved for nothing so much as a kind word now and then. If the man had ever so little heart, the boy must certainly have had his wish granted. A kind word! You have many such spoken to you daily, and you don't think much of their value; but that poor boy in the village, at whom everybody laughs, would think he had found a treasure if some one spoke a kind word to him.</p>
        <p>-+-..-.</p>
        <p>LITTLE PUSS. Sleek coat, eyes of fire, Four paws that never tire, That's puss. After ball, reel, or string, Wild as any living thing, That's puss. Round and round after tail, Fast as any postal mail, That's puss. Curled up like a ball, On the door mat in the .hall,</p>
        <p>That's puss. From a Saucer lapping milk, Soft as, soft as any silk, That's puss.</p>
        <p>M=Titk</p>
        <p>E=a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>HUMANE SWALLOW.</head>
        <p>A HUMANE SWALLOW.</p>
        <p>A lady I know was watching some swallows perched on one end of a window-ledge. Pre- sentty she saw a very quarrelsome swallow arrive, which rushed from one end of the ledge to the other, attackiag each bird in turn and. , driving it sway, till there reihained only one poor weak little thing, apparently very help- less and miserable. On came the persecutor, with wings outspread, and was preparing fa peck at the sick bird, when, seeing that some- thing was the matter with its proposed victim, it stopped short and flew away, leaving the in- valid unhurt. • QUEER RAPID TRANSIT. When your mother wishes to carry you 81$e clasps both arms round you and lifts you up. Then she holds you flrmly, and away you fo, whither she will. But some mothers cannot do that. When the little bird falls from the nest the mother has no way of carrying it back. When the cow wants her baby to walk she gives a coax- ing "M -o-o," and the calf wobbles along on its weak little legs. So it is with most animals; there is no way by which the moth,cr can carry the baby ; but some animal mothers Lave devised plans of their own._</p>
        <p>Pussy is one of these. If you peep at ,her too often as she nestles her little ones in her bed in the manger,. you will find that she has moved them. She is not happy unless her kittens are hidden away from prying hands and eyes. She has no arms to hold them in, and so she lifts them up by the back of the neclo with her teeth. Sometimes she will c,trry them in this way for a long distance. Although it does not hurt the kittens, they do not seem to enjoy it; but that makes no difference to the mother cat.</p>
        <p>Another animal that has its own way of carrying its young is the elephant. The mother elephant packs her baby in her trunk. The baby takes .hold of her tusks with its trunk, and seems to be much happier in itS journey than the kitten,. But the queerest of all ways of carrying babies is that of the mother kangaroo. ;she puts her baby in her pocket. Baby kangaroo may be quietly feeding, or playing in the grass, while the mother is watching to see that no harm comes to it. If an enemy appears the mother giveir the warniiig call, baby jumps into her pocket, and away they gallop, six feet at a jump; indeed, when the niother is very much frightened, she has been known to leap twenty feet at one bound. What would you think if your mother could jump clear across your dining-room? When they are in a safe place, baby climbs out again.. After all, baby folks have the very best way of being carried.</p>
        <p>FROM HIS POCKETS. s The other evening, after Johnnie, aged five years old, had gone to sleep, his mother and I made an inventory of his pockets. As she named the articles, I recorded them. Here they are: Baking-powder can, chisel, wooden toy Cole, three keys, iron bolt, eight buttons, five piece' of crumpled paper, a jackstone, marble beg and twelve marbles, paint-dish, shoe-buttoner, pasteboard box. four lead-pencils, piece of tin, elate-pencil, two safety-pins, three matcher, scrap of plush, butter-dish, piece of tile, two balls of string, piece of cardboard, piece of cloth, penny, screw, and a dirty handkerchief. If Johnnie owes no boy anything, he is surely a boy capitaliet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>GYGLitira</head>
        <p>GYGLitira</p>
        <p>Ey THE CLUBMAN.</p>
        <p>The present relations between cyclists and motorists are a most eloquent testimony to the reasonableness and forbearance of cyclists generally. The incon- venience and annoyance of motors on the road iS at least ten times greater to the cyclist than to the pedestrian; yet although one often hears strong verbal comments on the lack of consideration shown ' by many motorists to Cyclists, it is but seldom any of these complaints find their way into print. Close shaves at high speed ate what we principally object to, and the corkscrew-like method of progression adopted by many motorists when narrow roads are crowded with scores of Cyclists travelling in both directions. The present Motor-Car Act will, however, expire next year, and will then have to be renewed. New rules for the</p>
        <p>governance of motors when passing other vehicles , will then be introduced, and cyclists will get their benefit. In the meantime, running-down cases are increasing in number and seriousness.</p>
        <p>lip ....,.. -1 ..,;‘,...1:3-7,1 6.44" tri if A ,trgit i 7/.111,1r1 1 -) 7' ...... 11 - J 4</p>
