      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Reprehensible Practice.</head>
        <p>A Reprehensible Practice.</p>
        <p>The thoughtlessness which prompts some cyclists to allow their dogs to follow them when out cycling cannot be too severely condemned, not only on the ground of its cruelty to the dog, but from the danger it causes to other users of the road—especially to other cyclists. Scarcely a week passes but what one meets a gasping, panting animal of this kind galloping toilsomely after his thoughtless, cynical master, too intent on his own Pleasure to heed the evident distress of the poor brute at his heels. If perchance such an animal should be run over by another cyclist coming in the opposite direction, a mix -up of a most dangerous character is certain to result. Dog. lovers should certainly leave their dogs at home When starting out to take their pleasures awheel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Teaching a Lady to Ride</head>
        <p>Teaching a Lady to Ride</p>
        <p>looks all right, but is really far from being a desir- able kind of pastime. Here is a hint which may make it shorter and easier work. From an oxperience of .teaching many who have become expert and ,enthusiastic riders, I am sure that the most important part of learning to cycle is to acquire the art of mounting and dismounting, and this may be ac- - qtdred readily in an • I - hour if the following plan is adopted: A • smooth, level piece of road, twenty yards long, is chosen, and he learner is made to go through all the usual routine opera- tions of getting into :he saddle. A lady, Dr ins:tance, stands on le left-hand side of tie cycle, holding the andle-bars lightly ; daces the right foot in the right pedal when it has just passed the highest point, and is begin- ning to descend. She hops lightly into the aaddle, adjusts the skirt with the right hand, and. rte the machine begins to move, 'takes up the Pedalling and endeavours to acquire the balance. While she is doing this the teacher in a general Way trots alongside, holding up the pupil, and restraining her from falling to the left or right. After trotting a quarter or a half mile the teacher in an ordinary way will be quite exhausted, and Will gaspingly desire the pupil to dismount while the teacher recovers' lds wind and prepares for the ,laext bout.</p>
        <p>This is a mistake. At the end of twenty yards the pupil should dismount, the cycle should be turned round, and the operation of mounting, 'riding twenty yards, and dismounting should be gone through again, and so on, repeating the Operations for about an hour. At the end of that .tune the learner will have acquired the art of Mounting and dismounting perfectly; the art of balancing will come quite naturally, and in another hour's lesson you will be able to ride by the side of your pupil and observe how nicely the art of riding halt V</p>
