      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Remarkable Year's Cycling.</head>
        <p>A Remarkable Year's Cycling.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. W. Weeding, of Kingston-on-Thames, a road surveyor, whose duties necessitate his travelling daily; has ridden over 100,000 miles in the last ten years. During 1003 be put up the. strikingly tall total of mileage on his bicycle of 14,034 miles. During the weeks in May and August respectively he riode 601 and 743 miles. His highest monthly total was 2,112 in August, and his lowest 639 in January. The only detail vouchsafed concerning his machine is that be used a New Departure hub, which, on examination at. the close of the year, was found to exhibit no signs of wear, and was in the same condition as when first fitted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Record of Hard Work.</head>
        <p>A Record of Hard Work.</p>
        <p>Mr. Patrick O'Sullivan, a telegraph linesman, living at Oakpark; Tralee, purchased at the' Cycle Depot, Pembroke Street, Tralee, on June 3rd, 4.501, a New Hudson bicycle. He has used it in his • work ever since, travelling about 'all over County Kerry, egecting telegraph repairs, and averaging a weekly riding distance of 300 miles. Besides carrying himself he takes 'upon the bicycle about 1 cwt. of repairing tools and materials. Allowing for holidays. Mr. O'Sullivan has ridden his New Hudson under these severe conditions over 40,000 miles since he purchased it, and last week it was still going as well as ever, and considering this weight it had to carry and its heavy wear, it was still in a fairly presentable loondition as regards appearance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LANCASHIRE AGRICULTURAL STUDENTS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AGRICULTURAL STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>Results of Diploma Examinations.</p>
        <p>IThe following-are the results of the examinations for i the national diploma in agriculture tor 1905,he1d by the i• national agricultural examination board, at the Univer- sity, Leeds. In part lof the examination, which com- pris es the subjects of agricultural botany, mensuration and land surveying, general chemistry, geology and agricultural zoology, 56 candidates were examined and of these 38 passed. In part 11. of the examination,com- prising the subjects of practical agriculture,agricultural bookkeeping, agricultural chemistry, agricultural engineering and veterinary science; the number of : candidates examined as 32, all of whom had previously passed part 1. Of these '2O satisfied the examiners and were awarded the National Diploma in agriculture. ! No fewer than eleven students from the Lancashire 1 County Council Agricultural Classes at the Harris !Institute, Preston, passed Part 1. of the above examina- tion, viz :—George R. Bland, Prestwich ; James R. Bond, Banks ; John Fisher, Pilling ; Ernest Harrison, Levenshulme ' • Alfred Kilrue, Fulwood ; Frederick J. Podmore, Sefton; Thomas Hummer, Bickerstaffe ; !Esther L. Robinson, Farnworth, Widnes ; Charles ! Situnders,Brierfleld ; William R. Stolter,Wigan ; William Sugden Nelson. Five Lancashire County Council Students also passed !Part 2. and were awarded the full Diploma, viz ' Alfred Easthant. Penwortham ; George Fisher, Pilling ; John Robinson,Downhara, Clitheroe • Kate M. Nickson, • Prestwich ; Robert Richardson, Singleton. It may be interesting to note that Miss Kato M. Nickson is the first woman to whom an agricultural diploma has been awarded since these examinations , were instituted.'</p>
        <p>The following is a classificatiort of the passes from each of the Teaching Institutions of the United Kingdom that has prepared students for these examina- tions : Lancashire County Council Agricultural Students Harris Institute, Preston West of Scotland Agricultural College, Glasgow The University, Leeds South-Eastern Agricultural College, Wye, Kent 5 The University, Aberdeen 3 Harper-Adams Agricultural College, New- port, Salon 3 University College of Wales, Aberystwyth 3 Royal College of Science, Dublin 0 Holmes Chapel College of Agriculture 3 Armstrong College, Newcastle-on-Tyne Marischal College, Aberdeen</p>
      </div>
