      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Board of Education Interviewed.</head>
        <p>Board of Education Interviewed.</p>
        <p>THE PITCHBLENDE MINES OF HUNGARY.</p>
        <p>The radium, or rather pitchblende, mines of Joachimsthal, in the Erzgebirge, Hungary, are situated in a volcanic country, where dykes qf basalt, nepheliue, and leucite are mingled with metalliferous, especially silver and bismuth, veins. The vertical seams of pitchblende are in Tertiary Mica-schist, beds, with quartz veins. PitcJiblende is an oxide of uranium; but includes a varlet of rare and cominon metals. At Joachimsthal it is •</p>
        <p>associated with phosphorus under the form of uranite or autunite, and also with vanadium in carnotite. Apparently hot springs have caused the crystallisation of very complex minerals in the beds, for the pitchblende is also accompanied by pyrites, chalcopyrites, and other sulphates.</p>
        <p>ANALYSIS BY CENTRIFUGATOR. Bordas and Touplain have applied centrifugal force to the analysis of milk. Drop by drop ten cubic centimetres of milk are put into a glass tube, holding a solution composed of alcohol at 65deg., acidified by acetic acid. After' letting it rest some moments it is "centrifugated," or submitted to centrifugal force, and decanted. The precipitate is washed in alcohol. The milk is again centrifugated and decanted. Fellling's test for lactose is then applied. Butter is extracted from the precipitate of the preceding operation in two lestsi one with alcohol, the other with ether, centrifugating each time. Caseine in fine powder remains in the ceutri- fugator. The method suppresses the ordinary filtrations, and the long drying of the caseine in the ordinary analyses. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>RADIUM RAYS' EFFECT ON METALS.</head>
        <p>RADIUM RAYS' EFFECT ON METALS.</p>
        <p>M. Bronislas Sabat made a series of experiments in M. Curie's laboratory as to the action of radium rays on the electric resistance of different metals. The rays were obtained with the strongest prepara- tion of radium in the laboratory, namely, a bulb containing 0.2 gramme of pure radium bromide. Thin wires of the different metals were rolled around paper tubes in order to form a resistance coil, and the radium bulb was 'placed in the centre of the tube. The rays of the radium thus act upon the wire. As an example, an iron wire which started with a resistance of 4.64 ohms at once rose to 4•66 under the first action of the rays, and after five minutes' action rose to 4.68, which is the maximum effect. After removing the radium, the wire comes back to the original resistance in a few minutes. For iron, this gives a difference of 0.776 per cent. In the case of a platinum wire of the same diameter (0.1 millimetre) he finds 0.257 per cent. For German silver, it is but 0.092 per cent. Bismuth shows 0.284 per cent. The variations depend upon the size of the wire and the absorbing power for the rays, Itc. A certain increase of resistance is thus found at once, and before the heat is com- municated from the radium. The greatest variation of the resistance sometimes goes beyond the point which would be reached by the heat sent from the radium alone, and it is probable that the metals absorb the rays (principally the a-rays) and these are transformed to heat, raising the temperature of the metals and then changing their electric resist- ance. This action is analogous to the absorption of the cathodic rays by metals. The 3-rays give a smaller heat effect, however, as they are absorbed by the metals in a less degree than the cathcde rays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>FIXING ATMOSPHERIC NITROGEN.</head>
        <p>FIXING ATMOSPHERIC NITROGEN.</p>
        <p>Professor W. Eschweiler, of the Technical Hoch Schule at Hanover, and. Dr. IL C. Woltereck, of London and New York, have devoted years of research to seeking for a commercially practicable method of fixing, in a form suitable for assimilation by the roots of plants, the nitrogen in the atmo- sphere. They have now succeeded in manufacturing sulphate of ammonia on a commercial scale at a cost of less than half its previous market price. This substance is the most important M artificial fertilisers, and practically all that is obtained is employed for this purpose. Hitherto, nearly' all the sulphate of ammonia on the market has been obtained as a by-product of gas-making; but the supply from this source, though increasing hi amount, is quite inadequate to meet the demand, with the result that, in spite of an annually increasing output, there has been a continuous increase in the market price of sulphate of ammonia of late years. The process, says the Globe, consists essentially in passing a current of air mixed with steam over a mass of peat maintained in a state of slow combustion. As a result ammonia is formed • by the chemical combination of hydrogen obtained from the steam with part of the nitrogen originally present in the peat and part of the nitrogen con- tained in the air passing over it. The current of mixed gases is thou passed through sulphuric acid to form sulphate of ammonia. Over five tons of ammonia sulphate are obtained for every 100 tons of peat treated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>WONDERS OF PHYSICAL CHEMISTRY.</head>
        <p>WONDERS OF PHYSICAL CHEMISTRY.</p>
        <p>Sir William Ramsay gave an account, at the Royal Society, of the quantities of neon and helium, gases discovered by him, which is inter6t- ing as shewing the extremely small amounts with which modern physical chemistry can deal. First, argon, it will be remembered, was found by Lord Rayleigh and himself to lurk in atmospheric air; then helium, a substance which had been detected by the spectroscope in the sun, was identified in the earth's atmosphere. Next, three other gases were revealed—krypton, xenon, and neon—hiding themselves also in very minute quantities. Some time ago Sir William Ramsay communicated to the society estimates of the amounts of krypton and of xenon in atmospheric air, and since then he has been doing the same for neon and helium. After a series of delicate investigations, which he described, he arrived at the conclusion that there are in gaseous air 86 parts by weight of neon in 1,060,000,000, and 123 parts in the same by volume; N'‘ bile of Whim there are 56 parts by weight in 10.000,060,600 and 400 by volume in the same. Such minute amounts Seem almost incalculably small, but cc:rroborative tests had been applied, which indicated that the estimates could not be far from accurate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ORIGIN ~OF RIGIITHANDEDNESS.</head>
        <p>ORIGIN ~OF RIGIITHANDEDNESS.</p>
        <p>, ' The question .why the , majority of persons use the right hand with greater skill than the left has never been solved definitely. Dr. George M. Gould, of Philadelphia, has been specially studying the problem, and, writing in the Popular kg fence Afollthly. he says he believes that righthandedness is so thoroughly ingratiated in human nature that it Must have been partially developed in the savage ancestors of the race. Primitive man, in his opinion, must have felt definite need for the exercise of his right hand in preference to his left: and that necessity rriust have been ree'eg,nised and. obeyed .• for a long period continuously. The impulse could not well havo dictated his habits in (suing, for: knives and larks are of recent origin, the modern gun bows, and been invented, and writing ' i WAS practically unknoWM Moreover, in such opera- tions as chip 'Wielding club, ng arrows, weaving baskets, and ' ...ows, and arrows, it could not have mattered much which hand was employed. When warfare haul been carried to such a, state of advancement' as to involve Ake use of a shield,, however, that object was I:robably held on, the left' side in order to protect the heart. Then, as a matter ot convenience, the right arm wa- left tree, for the mere active function of fighting with spear and sword, and with habitual exercise' came iipecial</p>
      </div>
