      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL ACTIVITY.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. MR. A. H. CROSFIELD ADDRESSES Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases sent their names and addresses to the Editor THE FAIRFIELD AND HOWLEY as a guarantee of good faith. WARD COMMITTEES.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks and the Religious Difficulty in the Schools. . Sir.—l think there is a danger of our Education Com- mittee drifting into a religious controversy that by mutual consideration ought to be avoided, and without the sacrifice of any principle on either side. For the Secularist to assume (because he has the power) to deny the right of the churchman to perform what he regards The enthusiasm with which the work of organizing as a religious duty, is just as unreasonanle and unfair the Liberal forces is being proceeded with was further as when the churchman assumes the right by direct and evidenced. on Monday evening when the annual social indirect means to force all children into the performance gathering in connection with the Fairfield and H3wley of his own religious observances. Unfortunately we , have to deal with extreme men on both sides of this Committees s held in the Brick-street Mission. Want was e question, if we take the speeches and its correspondence Gratifying success attended the function, and it is as representing their honest convictions. Let me say at evident that the workers in these important once that there cannot be any dual management. The wards intend to leave no stone unturned to authority of the Education dommitiee must be supreme secure a Liberal triumph at the next election. and they cannot recognize any other authority than A new departure was made in inviting the wives of Parliamentary. If the ecclesastic choose to do so that committee members to attend, and judging from the is hi own private affair, and is quite outside the large response, the innovation appeared to be highly question at issue, but for public body to do so, and appreciated. An excellent tea was served by Coun- submit to it will raise the far deeper question of principle, which every honest Nonconformist will resist cillor James Evans and afterward a meeting was to the bitter end at any cost of personal sacrifice. If held. Mr. A. H. Crosfield presided,'and the room was any ecclesiastic bases his refusal to comply with the well filled. Amongst those present on the platform requirements of the Education Committee on what he were Councillor Clegg, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, calls the higher authority of the church, then the corn- G. Walker, J. Howes, S. Wright, J.P., and J. Howes, mittee are bound to resist his assumption on public • grounds, and to deal with the school he represents as a inn. (registration agent). A capital musical pro- public defaulter. gramme had been arranged and the items, which But what harm can come if the committee recognize reflected great credit upon the artistes, were very well that men differ in religion as they do in politics- and received. Songs were rendered with pleasing effect other matters, and make their arrangementsaceordingly. by Miss Howes, Mr. P. J. George, and Mr. R. W. Cook, If a man chooses to fast on a Friday and g? to Church and Mr. Shaw gave a very amusing recitation. The on a W.dnesii4y why should we refuse to give him the selections by the Apollo Male Voice Quartette were a opportunity if it can be done without injury to anyone special feature, and the beautiful blending of the else. The Education Committee fit and regulate all voices and artistic treatment of the various items, gave holidays, of which in my opinion there are plenty. Why not shorten the summer or winter holidays by a few manifest pleasure to the audience. During the even- days and make such days as Ash Wednesday or Ascension ing several speeches on the political outlook were Day or any other day which a Churchman or Dissenter delivered. recognizes as a day of public prayer, as part of the MR. GEORGE WALKER AND LABOUR holidays to which his children are entitled. fa recog- I REPRESENTATION. nizing your neighbour's right to his opinion you don't Mr. trEORGE WALKER was the first speaker. Alluding make any sacrifice of your oWn. You simply do that l to the excellent attendance of ladies he said it was a which you claim for yourself. .at argument in favour of female sufferage. Mr. The introduction of religious services into our schools gLraPeouchere had expressed himself hitter opponent of is one thing, and very different to the recognition, that that reform l di many men believe and wish their children to attend (Laughter.) becauseHNlv la es were invariably opponTories. e r. a ker)should think Mr.Labonchere their own religions services in the place appointed. I had never been to Warrington. (Laughter.) The large I hope our Education Committee will be able to see their . ance of ladies that night augured well for the I way out of the difficulty, in attendance spirit of true toleration, rising generation of Warringtonians that they at any which is the only safe guide in all religious con- rate should be brought up in the true'faith. (Applause.) troversy. Yours faithfully,' They were on the eve of a political contest, but he was afraid it was a case of "hope deferred maketh the heart sick." They would like to have a tussle with the Con- servatives but somehow their opponents fought shy of it. Whether ladies took a prominent part in politics was a question, but he was slue the Government of the country was at present carried on by aa old ‘voman. (Laughter.) Mr. Howes ventured to assert that that was a libel on the old women—(langhter)—and he (Mr. Walker)almost agreed. Ladies could at times make up their minds but in the case of the Prime Minister it seemed impossible for him to do that. There never was a time in the history of the country when there was a greater necessity for workingmen to interest themselves in politics—not to have their politics made for them but to think for themselves. He held it was a duty they oved to their fellow-men that they, should arrive at a decision on great questions and vote accordingly. He could not understand the position taken up by some of their Labour friends in the town who refused to vote becanse the Liberal candidate was not, perhaps, to their line of thinking. In the neighbouring division they fled a candidate who was not to his (Mr. Walker's) line of thinking, but he (Mr. Walker) intended to go as far as that candidate went in his directioa. When be could not go any further with him he would stop, but he would render all the assistance he could as a Labour man, and would not hold aloof because the candidate's views were more extreme than his. He wished the working- men of Warrington to take a generous view of the matter. He was quite sure that a worse Government than the present one we never had in the history of the country. The army had been badly managed and it was a question whether the navy would prove to be much better if it were put to a true test. Yet the Govern- ment seemed to have no object in view but to hold on to office. They as Liberals were fighting for principles and not men. In Mr. Crosfield he believed they had a man of principle, who made a study of social questions and was prepared to treat them from a Liberal point of view. He trusted that on the day of the election they would not be found wanting in Fairfield Ward. (Applause).</p>
        <p>F. MONKS.</p>
        <p>Walton Old Hall, May 24th, 1905.</p>
        <p>MR. CROSPIELD'S SPEECH. Mr. CROSFIF.LD had a very hearty reception on rising to address the meeting. He said he thanked the meet- ing with all his heart for such a cordial greeting. He felt very much in need of a little encouragement of that kind because he was told that his dear friend Councillor Evans had done his duty nobly and that the tea had been a great success. He knew that the artistes had done their duty, and they had had such a pleasant treat and such a delightful musical entertainment that he , was afraid the ladies would not thank him for talking dry politics. (Laughter.) They were all very pleased to see so many ladies present. He pirticillarly enjoyed seeing the political meetings graced with their presence. They felt that in the great issue that was ahead of them it was very largely to the women and ch;ldren that they had to make an honest appeal. (Applause). It would be amusing if they could have a little telephonic arrangement laid on with the Conservative Club. where , he understood, they were having a political meeting. It would certainly interest him very much to hear what his friend Mr. Pierpoint had got to say. (Laughter.) He wondered at this juncture whether the member was going to stick to his guns or whether, like very many others of his party, he was going to FLEE FROM THE WRATH TO COME. (Laughter.) Would he give them what he (Mr. Cros- field) had noticed in so many of Mr.Pierpoint's speeches —the pure milk of the Protectionist word—and preach the gospel according to 'Joseph of Birmingham—(laugh- ter)—or would he find refuge in some metaphysical , Balfourian disquisition which nobody could make head or tail of. (Renewed Laughter.) The fiscal faith, according to Mr. Balfour, was a plant of very delicate 11 growth. It would not stand the open air; it had to be developed in the hot-house—(laughter)—and they even told him that it would not stand the light of day. Whether its habitat was Downing-street, or whether it grew in that dark chamber, that detached room they heard of in the House of Commons, the result would be particularly interesting to them some day when the Premier was forced to show his hand. It was quite evident that Mr. Chamberlain knew that those who were what were called " whole hoggers " a little time since, were fight- ing shy of the programme he had laid out for them, and that the army was in full retreat. But they had it straight from Mr. Chamberlain himself that although he was prepared to get a beating at the next election, he was determined to bring this question of Protection for- ward afterwards, and in due time he (dr. Crosfield) had not a doubt that they would have. PROMISES FROM MR. CHAMBERLAIN. in fact, he did not know what Mr. Chamberlin would not premise. (Laughter, and a voice : " Three acres and a cow.") Yes.perhaps he wonld promise three acres and a cow—those shadowy pastures cropped by phantom cows. (Laughter.) Or perhaps he would promise old-age pensions, which he had promised over and over again. It struck him (Mr. Crosheld) that if they wanted to find those old age pensions—and although he was speaking in a jocular vein at the moment, the provision of a well considered scheme of old-age pensions was one of the greatest and wisest ram:tires • that could be passed for this country—if they wanted to know where they had lost them, he was afraid they would have to go and search for them on what Mr. Chamberlain himself had described as the illimitable veldt. (Hear, hear.) They would find a great many other things if they went and looked for them there. There were influential people in Warring- ton—powerful employers of labour—who made a strong hid for the support of their fellow-townsmen for this policy of Protection, and there were the same people in every large commercial town in England—people who were</p>
        <p>SEEKING THEIR OWN eitortT at the expense of the whole community. (Hear, hear.) There had always been that section in the Conservative party, and it was upon that sntion that Mr. Chamber- lain relied for success. He (Afr. Crosfleld) therefore wanted the people of Warrington to think twice when some more promises from Birmingham were dangled before thorn. Mr. Crosfield said the situation reminded him of the story of the canny Scotsman who went to consult his dentist and complained of bad toothache. When Saundy cot into the chair, Macpherson, the dentist, said, " Saundy, its a verra bead tooth. Ye mon hae it oot. jist gie ye the gas." And so the dentist was about to apply the apparatus when &amp;randy pulled some coins out of his pocket. " Tut tilt," said Macpherson, "ye'll nae be payin me the noo." " Hoot, mann," replied Saundy " a'm no ga'en to pay ye (laugh- ter) hut before ye gie me yen gas, a'm ga'en ta coont ma siller."(Loud laughter). Now,continued Mr.Ctosfleld there was a good deal of gas manufactured in Bir- mingham. (hear, hear). and Mr. Chamberlain was very fond of odministering it to the public, and whenhe had them under its influence he sometimes played the very mischief. (Laughter.) There were many issues before the Liberal party in the future, but the one great platform on which they all met at present was the platform of Free Trade. (Applause). The first thing they had to do,—to which, he believed, four fifths of the people of this town would be agreed—the first thing they had to do with the FALSE IMAGE OF PROTECTION, with monopoly at its elbow, and corruption dogging its heels, was to cast that false image to the ground and trample it in the dust.(Lond applause).flut friends of his had said" It is not enough to go to the country on a</p>
        <p>negative policy •like that." He did not know nh) ever thought of going to the country on what he called a mere negative policy. They had got a positive policy and progressive work before them that certainly would keep the advanced party in: the:nation actively and industriously engaged for another generation. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Gladstone said quite truly in 1895, what Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman had repeated the other day, that on was almost oppressed when one considered the amount of work that lay before the Liberal party—or the progressive party if they liked to call it so—and the only difficulty was to make a wise selection, and to see that "give and take" was properly exercised in the party, so that they did not try, in John Bright's words. to ran a dozen 'buses abreast through Temple Bar. He would like to read them a paragraph from the very able speech which</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS CHURCH. Annual Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS CHURCH. Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The Assembly Room at the Conservative Club pre- sented a pleasins scene on Wednesday afternoon, when the annual sale of work in connection with St. Barnabas' Church was opened by Mrs. Peter Rylands. Thanks to the efforts of numerous workers the room had been brightly decorated, and the stalls, heavily-laden with a variety of useful and fancy wares, were attractively arranged. The following radies acted as stall.holders : —Work stalls : No. 1, Mrs. Hollingworth, Mrs. Dowling, Mrs. Tomkins ; No., 2, Mrs. Iddon, Mrs. Connell, Miss Jefferson ; N 0.3, Mrs. Bracegirdle, Mrs. Billington, Mrs. Wakefield ; No. 4, Mrs. Osborne, Miss Blakelock,Miss Short; Japanesestell, Mrs. Evans, Mrs. Furnival, Miss Helsby ; toy stall, Misses E. and A. Fry, Miss A. Jones, Miss Bolton; flower stall, Miss A. Whitehouse, Mrs. Hands; refreshment stall, Mesdames Jones, 7. Proud, IV. Jones, Greenhalgh, Grounds, and Woodcock, Misses. Whitehouse, Gerrard, Wood and Grounds. The vicar (Rev. G. H. G. Hollingworth) presided at the opening ceremony, and the attendance included the Revs. L. Blakelock and H. R. Wild. In explaining ths&gt; objects of the sale the VICAR said the proceeds were, in the first place, on behalf of the Curacy Fund. They had to raise about £l3O locally to pay the stipend of an assistant curate. Then they had also to help their day schools. At the present time 1 they were rather hard-pressed, as several repairs must be carried out, and as yet they had no funds to do the work. He did not want to hand over his schools or to close them, because he was a firm believer in a definite religions teaching. If the church schools in this country were closed and no definite religion was taught in schools, he was afraid in a few years' time it would be "good-bye God" in England. At the present time, fortunately, he had working with him an able, loyal, and efficient colleague. The work in the day schools had never been better done than, it was being carried on now, and he never met a better staff of ' teachers. In introducing Mrs. Rylands, the Vicar said she was well-known and respected in Warrington and highly-honoured. (Applause.) Mrs. RYLANDS, before declaring the sale open, urged all present to do their utmost to make it a great success. She was sure the Vicar of a town parish like St. Barnabas' must have very uphill work, even if he were supported by an able and active band of workers such as Mr. Hollingworth evidently'possessed. Judging from the well-filled stalls a great many people in the parish could give work even if they could not give money. There must be a great difficulty in a parish of that kind to raise the funds necessary to provide a curate and the organizations which were so absolutely necessary for the efficient working of the parish. Therefore they were obliged to ask for outside help. She hoped the appeal they were making that day would be most generously responded to and that the sale would be a great success. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. J. Iddon (Vicar's warden), a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Rylands. The VicAn having thanked all who had helped in the errangement of the sale or who had sent gifts, the busi- ness of the day commenced.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS* RELIGIOUS EDUCATION IN DAY SCHOOLS. The sale was re-opened on Thursday by the Rev. G. 11. Oakley, Vicar of Deandey, near Rochdale, The VICAR again presided and in introducing the Rev. G. R. Oakley pointed out that the rev, gentleman had had a great deal to do with education in Church of England schools and he had also been a member of a School Board. He had therefore had experience of the working of both sides of secular education—the Godly side and that side which had very little religious in- struction in it. The Rev. G. R. 0 11I,LEY commenced his speech by referring to the necessity of an assistant priest in a parish like St. Barnabas', pointing out that it was im- possible for one man to do the work. He heartily con- gratulated the congregation upon the accession to the spiritual work of the parish which they had secured in their assistant curate (Rev. L. Blackelock). He was glad also that the parish possessed excellent schools and that they were particularly fortunate in having a capable and enthusiastic band of dayschool teachers. Before the Education Act was passed, there was a long and hard struggle to keep denominational schools going, but he believed that the managers and teachers of St. Barnabas' schools were successful in the efforts. He always felt it very important that Church schools should stand to remind the people that they were educating the whole and not two-thirds of the child, because they could not call a child fully educated if it were taught simply physically and morally and not spiritually. It seemed to him to be a very strange idea of education that would give a child physical and mental instruction end leave the most important part of its being altogether untaught. If they were Christians as they professed to be, and if they really believed in our Lord Jesus Christ, and His ressurection and their future life, they must feel that those who told them that secular education was all they should think of were absolutely opposed to the whole idea which was inherent in the Christian faith—the idea of giving the teaching of 'Jesus Christ and His religion which they knew was such an important element in the education of any life. Of course, in order to be successful, he always contended that religious educa- tion must be denominational, or, if one preferred the term, dogmatical. They were told so frequently that all that was required was undenominational instruction that one was inclined to ask whether those who used the term really knew what it meant. Instruction that was rmdenotuinational must be necessary jelly-fish in kind. They could see through the jelly-fish and it was not pleasant to go particularly close to it. Then again, if the teaching was not really undenonainational, it was bound to be unfair to some denomination. It would be quite impossible to teach children anything about other subjects in the ordinary school course, if they were not taught dogmatically. Only those persons who did not know what dogma meant ever dreamt of speak- ing of dogmatical teaching as being something in itself unpleasant. Mr. Oakley concluded by wishing the sale every success. The Rev. L. BLAKELOCK moved a vote of thanks to the Rev. G. 11. Oakley whom, he said, he was particu- larly pleased to see, because he had known him for so long. They owed a great debt of gratitude, he said, to the devoted band of workers who had endeavoured to snake the sale a success. Mr. J. IDDON seconded, and said Mr. Oakley's presence was always welcome to the parishioners of St.llarnabas'. They were all particularly desirous of retaining the day scoools, and seeing that religious education was given in them, The vote was heartily accorded and the sale was resumed. Each evening selections were rendered by Master T. Barton's juvenile band. The total proceeds of the sale amounted to about.£loo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Positive Policy of the Liberal Party.</head>
        <p>The Positive Policy of the Liberal Party.</p>
        <p>QUESTION OF HOME DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 27, 1905.</p>
        <p>SIR ENRY CAMPBRZL-BANNICRMAX</p>
        <p>made at Newcastle - last- Friday. Sir Henry had been referring to the education and temperance questions and he said "No doubt much will have to be done to repair the false steps that have been taken when we have the power to do it. We mast try to settle the educa- tion question on national and liberal lines. Then we must restore public control for the liquor traffic." Continuing Sir Henry went on to say :—" I have already mentioned one great subject, that of rating, which is urgent. In that must be included the taxation of urban site values a reform for which our great municipalities are agitating and almost clamouring. This principle has been three times affirmed in the House of Commons. Its effect, we believe, would be not only to place urban rating on a more equitable footing, but greatly to facilitate the solucion of the problems of housing and of urban expansion. No less urgent is the whole range of ques- tions connected with the depopulation of the agricul- tural districts. To say that the well-being and, indeed, almost the existence of a nation is threatened by this process of sapping is no • exaggeration. We hold that measures are required for extending the facilities for small holdings and 'for the erection of cottages, 'that tenant farmers are entitled to a larger liberty in the cultivation of their farms and greater security in the result." Continuing, Mr. Crosfield said he could read to them of many other great reforms that were necessary and many that Sir Henry Campbell Bannerman referred to in his speech. They must not let anyone deceive them by pretending that the Liberal party had got no positive policy to work for, or that they were fighting for a mere negation. There was only one other matter to which he wished to refer, and it was not a party matter, or at least, as far as possible it should not be a party matter. He thought the Government had made another mistake—but then they were always making mistakes. (Laughter.) They seemed beat on utterly discrediting themselves. He did not know whether Liberals would much regret that the Government had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>HASTENED THEIR OWN END</head>
        <p>HASTENED THEIR OWN END</p>
        <p>in that way. They --W-o-ad; -.Pe-ill-IPa, let them have their own way. And that again reminded him of another story, this time one of Edwin Waugh's best Lancashire ales. It was about an old couple who having taken many a holiday excursion on land, thought they'd try the sea for a change. When they got out on the briny the weather became what was termed " dirty " ; and perhaps some of thorn had experienced the pleasures of excursion steamers in dirty weather. (Laughter.) And perhaps they had suffered also from an ailment which made people very miserable, and yet entitled them to not the slightest sympathy from their friends. That was the fate of this couple, and t'owd mon up on deck was very bad, and t'owd lass down below was worse still, and she sent the following message up,to her lord and master." Tell him " she said," a'hm deein', and if he wants to see me again in this world ee' mann coom, an' coom quick." (Laughter.) But the old chap was much tuo far gone to pay any attention. "Na na, ' be said, " I'se na comic'. 'Oos had her own road all her life, an' an shall 'eve it now,"—(loud laughter)—" an' if 'oo is ter deein', 'OO maun dee." (Much laughter.) And so they too, continued Mr. Crosfield, must leave the Government sooner or later to their well- deserved fate. One thing the Government had done which really seemed to him a very gratuitous mistake' though it was a matter which ho certainly did not wish in any sense to treat as a party question. Row foolish it was of them to cut down the numbers, and, as it were, to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>DISCOURAGE TELE VOLUNTEER SYSTEM</head>
        <p>DISCOURAGE TELE VOLUNTEER SYSTEM</p>
        <p>which was just about the best guarantee that we had for the safety of the country! (Applause.) For his part, he absolutely associated himself with what Sir Edward Grey said on that matter—that the volunteers ought to receive practically unstinted support. (Applause.) Mey had had more kicks than half-pence. so far as he could judge, up to now, and he thought that while it was very necessary that volunteers should for a sulicient time go under canvas and become well-trained and well equipped soldiers, it was not fair to ordinary workingmen that they should be so long away from their work, and that their families should be without some modest recompense day by day while the volunteers were per- forming what he regarded as a thoroughly patriotic duty to their country. ,(Applause.) He considered the Government might go further and, with all propriety, provide the shooting ranges, the drill sheds, and the accoutrements that were necessary, in order that the I volunteers might be maintained in a state of efficiency I and in numbers that were sufricient to repel any raid that might be made, whether by 70,00 men —as Lord Roberts had suggestel--or even by a larger number that might possibly find a landing upon our shores. He advocated that very strongly, and for another reason—that it u-as recognized by the I nations of the world that "with out great commercial argosies and with all the interests which we as islanders had at stake in maintaining a free pissage for our fleets in both hemispheres, England must he the predominant navll power, and keep up what was called the two-power standard. She also, as a natural consequence,absolutely required well-fortified and protected coaling stations, and a small but highly efficient regular army to police the Empire. But he would like to point out that if Mr. Balfour took the line that, having the navy,we need not fear the possibility of a raid upon our shores and that therefore auxiliary forces might be more or less super- fluous,it meant that we must build more and more ships because we must have them,as the phrase went,"cribbed, cabined and confined" around our shores, to protect us from the smallest invasion from outside. He (Air. ) Crosfield) submitted that that was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>A. MOST MISTAKEN POLIC</head>
        <p>A. MOST MISTAKEN POLIC</p>
        <p>when the extra amount of naval power which would be necessary under those circumstances would only be an encouragement to foreign nations to continue what might be described as the "beggar my neighbour" game in that building of fleet against fleet which was impoverishing Europe to a most serious extent. There- fore from that point of view, it seemed to him that on the question of imperial defence tne Government had taken a short-sighted and unwise line. He mentioned that because people sometimes said that since the Liberals would not endorse and support every filibustering expedition that was undertaken, they were indifferent to the defence of the empire. There was absolutely no truth in that. They all knew that we must defend and keep what we had got and he was not prepared to say that under no circumstances would it be advisable for us to take another square yard of land. All he would submit was that a very strong case must be made out before we sought, with that earth-hunger that had affected so many people of late years,forther territorial aggrandize- ment. In the meantime they were thoroughly deter- mined to defend what we had got in this empire of ours and as Liberals they would do more—they would defend what was best in our own country and institutions. (Applause). They were no more careless about the institutions of our country than they were about the empire itself. He had often heard his old friend Mr. Thomas Birchen who, they all hoped,would at an ea:ly date recover from his temocrary indisposition, say that their object as reformers was not to pull down the edifice but to repair and strengthen it, to substitute for had workmanship a bit of good solid masonry. (Applause.) That was their object; or to vary the metaphor, and if he might refer to England in this vernal month of May as oee great garden, then their object and their duty was to root out evil growths so . that healthy plants might flourish and make England Imore beautiful than ever, and yet more the envy of the other nations or the world. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR CLEGG AND HOWLEY WARD. Councillor CLEGG briefly addressed the meAing. He had, he said, been particularly impressed with the fact that the Warrington Liberal Association had been very happy in their choice, and that they had in Mr. Arthur Crosfield one of the finest prospective candidates that any constituency could hope to have. (Applause.) As a commercial man, he believed Mr. Crostield had been fortunate in planting the name of Warrington in almost every country in the world. The concern of which Mr. Crosfield was the head employed a whole army of workers ; and the work was conducted in a way that I would never have been dreamt of a few years ago. In Mr. Crosfleld they had a man who had followed very ! closely the teachings of Gladstone,and whose family had been closely associated with Bright and Cobden. He was a friend of Mr. John Morley and a friend of Mr. Henry Broadhurst, and he had studied politics as well as busi- ness. Therefore he was a fit and proper person to represent an important industrial constituency like Warrington. (Applause.) Continuing, Councillor Clegg said he was proud they had in Howley Ward a. number or workingmen who were pleased to do what they could for the success of the Liberal cause. They bad recently had an election in Howley Ward—one of the papers ventured to call it a miniature Parliamentary election. They had actually the membcir for the Borough speaking at a public meet- ing and endeavouring, to bolster on the Conservative nominee. The result of that election proved that Howley ward could now be called Liberal and he believed the workers would be willing to do their utmost to bring about the return of Mr. Crostield when the general election took place. (Applause.) _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SPEECH BY MR. HOWES.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. HOWES.</p>
        <p>Mr, .TosE-Pit HOWES said they were in the midst of the political struggle, and he had been struck with the splendid manner in which the men bad rallied round them during the time they had been work- ing, up the contest. To secure Mr. Crosfleld's return they must have the sympathy and united of all the men and women helpers they could get. There never was an election that touched the home life more than the next election would Workingmen should hear in mind that the Government had already reduced the purchasing power of every sovereign they earned by</p>
        <p>3s. Continuing Mr. Howes said they were Free Traders ' because they found fault altogether with the way in which the Government's policy would collect the revenue. They did not want the Government to get the revenue from the food of the people. It was a crime for a Government to tax the people's food, and the Liberals were going to fight the election chiefly on that. (Applause.) The country was sick of the manoeuvres of the Government. The evil day would come upon the Government soon enough and what the Liberals had to do was to make the best use of the time at their disposal. Mr. Balfour said the Tory party must exist for the purpose of defending the throne, religion, and the empire. The throne was as safe in the keeping of the Liberal party as of the Tory party; the Tory party had not the monopoly of religion •, and as for the empire, the Liberals made it, and could therefore be entrusted to preserve it. The nation bad turned its back on Mr. Chamberlain, and his once bright and brilliant career I had collapsed. The Liberals would go on with their ! good work, marching from victory to victory, till the great chariot should have passed even through Warring- toe, and picked up their candidate and carried him onto I St. Stephen's. (Applause.) VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>MR. CROSPIELD AND TETE LABOUR PARTY.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSPIELD AND TETE LABOUR PARTY.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. JACKSON HOLMES moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Crosfield for his presence and exception- ally interesting and able address, and also to the ; artistes for their services. He was sure Mr. Crosfleld felt he 4vas already thanked by the attendance of the two excellent committees,which was most encouraging to those who had been continuing thefight on behalf of the Liberal party and had been entrusted in some measure with the direction of affairs. Mr. Holmes also paid a tribute to the excellent educational work done ; by Mr. Howts. In conclusion he urged his hearers to ; do their utmost to keep the Liberal machinery in order ; and they would then be in a state of preparedness for I the election. In that event he believed they would have ; no difficulty whatever this time in landing Mr. Cros- field at the head of the poll. (Applause). Mr. S. WaioirT seconded. As one who had taken some interest in the Labour movement, and having heard Mr. Crosfleld speak on Labour questions, he ; felt perfectly convinced that they could not have a better candidate than Mr. Crosfield. They were told that Labour could not be represented by a man of wealth. In the House of Commons to-day, however, they had men like Sir Charles Duke and Mr. John Burns who were called Liberals but who represented the true interests of the working classes. Ti Labour could lay claim to better men than those he would like to see them. Socialistic ideals were all very well, but he believed in gsing in for that which he could obtain. In Kr. Crostleld they had a candidate who would represent them as well as any of the Socialistio'candidates who could be brought forward. He (Mr. Wright) felt it was his duty as a Trade Unionist to recommend Mr. Crosaeld to the working- people of Warrington. (Applause.) The vote was enthusiastically accorded. In responding Mr. CIIOSFtELD remarked that they might go a long way before they would hear more exquisite music than the quartette party had rendered that night. He valued the vote of thanks very highly, and perhaps all the more because it had been seconded by Mr.Wri eht.He liked to have the support on the platform of fine representatives of Labour and trade unionism like Mr. Wright. There was no finer testimony to trade unionism in England than the type of men they sent to represent them in Parliament and he had heard that opinion expressed by plenty of non- Labour Liberal representatives in the House. If by their favourhe should have the great honour and at the same time the great responsibility placed upon him of representing the town for which his family had done what they could in pubil.ie affairs for many years—(ap- Dlause)—he could assure them that, although it was' not alone to the Labour members that he should go for instructions—he should seek them from all quarters—it was as much from the Labour members and perhaps more from them than from any other division in the House that he should look for guidance and sound advice. The work that lay before the Pro- gressive party got more difficult and complicated as years went on, and more and more as the years passed Was one reminded of what their old great chief said towards the close of his days—that he had had to fight through the great battle of emancipation and although the work was not wholly completed, still in a great measure it had been brought towards completion and we were now entering on the period of construction. It was that constructive legislation which was so corn- Plicated and difficult that it was not safe for any mere ; theorists to7undertake it unless they had been brought into active contact with those who had to do after all the hard work of the world. It was for that reason ; that he had valued so mach the intimacy, the long- established friendships he had enjoyed with Labour members of the House of Commons, and with many of the honoured and esteemed Labour loaders in our dear old town. They might depend upon it that whether the Government took the plunge now or whether they waited a little longer—and they would find it all the colder when they did take it—whenever the issue was decided he would not flinch from the contest, because he felt from the bottom of his heart that they had, in all the great causes they were fighting for,as magnificent a pro- gramme as ever inspired the Progressive party in this country. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WINWICK ASYLUM.</head>
        <p>WINWICK ASYLUM.</p>
        <p>Acute and Dangerous Types of Lunacy.</p>
        <p>BUILDING TO BE COMPLETED.</p>
        <p>A recommendation that a further sum not exceeding £30,000, as may be necessary for extras and to complete the asylum buildings at Winwick Asylum' be granted out of the Asylums Fund was contained in the report of the Finance., &amp;c., Committee, and laid before the Lancashire Asylums Board at their meeting at Preston Ore Thursday. Mr. Roberts (Liverpool), said there were certain things left out in connection with the building opera- tions which really had to be done to make the building satisfactory to the committee. A letter was read frrom the Lunacy Commissioners with reference to allowing winwick Asylum to be used for acute cases. It stated :—" That, having regard to the very large number of troublesome, turbulent, and dangerous cases that were found in the asylum at the annual visit in February last, the Commissioners felt unable to withstand any longer thp wishes of the Board that the asylum should not any longer be reserved for chronic. cases." in connection with this matter, the Chairman said it had been agreed that it would be desirable to build an .asylum on the villa system, and on a cheaper scale, something like a workhouse, where chronic pmients,who were unable to take care of themselves, and who were unable to work, but did not require the same skilled medical care as acute cases which went to the asylums, should be lodged. It was for the board to consider whether they could not devise a scheme for building a cheaper class of asylum, where snob patients could be maintained at a very much less cost to the ratepayers. (1-leal, hear). Eventually, a resolution was passed to the effect "that, as the restriction as to the using of Winwick Asylum for chronic oases only has now been removed, it will be desirable to consider the advisability of utilising the proposed site at Whalley for an asylum of cheap construction to accommodate cases other than of the acute and dangerous types." It was also decided to empower the Whalley committee to communicate with the various boards of guardians on the subject. A proposal to increase the salary of Dr. A. Simpson, medical superintendent at Winwick Asylum, from £BOO to £l,OOO, met with considerable opposition, but was ultimately passed by a large majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LATE REV. F. SLATER. _ Funeral at Hisley.</head>
        <p>THE LATE REV. F. SLATER. _ Funeral at Hisley.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Rev. Francis Slater,for twenty years vicar of St. James' Latchford (whose death at Oxford is reported on page six of this issue) took place at Oisley, a picturesque village near his native place of New Mills, Derbyshire, on Wednesday. The coffin was conveyed to Disley by a train leaving Oxford at 8.30 in the morning and on arrival there was met by six bearers who carried it to the grave side. The burial service was conducted by the Rev. William Scott, of Oxford, in whose parish Mr. Slater had often given assistance. The chief mourners were Messrs. Cuthbert. and Gilbert Slater (sons), Miss Marjory Slater (daughter), the Rev. A. V. C. Hordern, chaplain to the forces at Fulsvood Barraoks, Prestqp (son-in-law) and other members of the family. There were also presenethe Rev. W. Hollowell, a former master at the Warrington Grammar School and now headmaster of Calday -Grange Grammer School, West Kirby, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, vicar of Holy Trinity, Warrington, Mr. C. S. Burgess and Mr. W. H. Stansaeld (represent- ing St. James', Latchford.) Numerous floral tributes were forwarded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Well-known Full-Back Charged.</head>
        <p>Well-known Full-Back Charged.</p>
        <p>On Thursday afternoon, the Eg,rton Arms Runcorn, kept by 'Simnel Houghton the famous Cheshire county and international full back, was raided by the p dice. who searched the premises. Houghton and four customers, named Ernest Warburton, Elles- mere-street ; William Leyland, Portland-street ; Thomas Robinson, Cartwright-street ; and Alfred Lord, Parker-street, were marched to the police station and searched. Betting slips and money were found upon all except one. Subsequently they wero broutilit before Mr. Imison, and Houghton was charged with keeping a bolts° for betting purposes, and the others charged with frequenting.--The prisoners were remanded to June 5, and liberated on bail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>FRENZIED FINANCE. A QUESTION IN ETHICS. Shall We Accept iliotten Gains?</head>
        <p>FRENZIED FINANCE. A QUESTION IN ETHICS. Shall We Accept iliotten Gains?</p>
        <p>[Br Ala. H. Wana.tx]. There can be no doubt that a huge wave of thought is now running through all ranks and classes of the United States touching the growth and doings of the half-dozen Trusts that are now and have been for years having such a malevolent influence over the Eves and well-being of the people. The reviews, magazines, daily and weekly journals are, at a great expense, pouring out information on the question, and it will be strange if, as a result of this, we do not have some surprising developments in the industrial and monetary world. Still, we must not be too sanguine. It takes a deal to move the whole of the people to action.</p>
        <p>Within the past few years the truth has been forced upon the people that the officialdom of the town- • ships, villages, cities, countries and states of the nation is just maggoty-rotten with corruption ; • that our local, state and national legislators are openly controlled by mercenary private interests; that the scandals concerning our Judiciary can no longer be smothered or concealed; that our citizens are powerless to pass laws demanded by the majority, I that the burdens of taxation are spurned by those who I have amassed wealth by means of nnfair and oft-times I- I purchased legislation, and that the domination of ; corporations and vested interests is so complete as to be apparent to the dullest of the plundered. This grave charge against the wealthy is not made because of the possessions of such wealth, but because of the I methods by which such wealth is obtained. I The press gives Us day by day not merely scandals in connection with Corporations, Trusts or Companies, but charges of positive criale are made. In this city of San Francisco there is an inquiry going on now as to the doings of the Elqctrio Lighting Company. This charge is made by an employee and fully sworn to by the employees generally: "When I was working for the company as a meter inspector about three years and a half ago," he said, "I received instructions from Foreman Finley to make the meters resister about seven per cent too • much, 'and a little more won't do any harm,' he told me. He said that the orders had come to him from Superintendent SAIU Neplithaly and that all the meter inspectors were expected to do the same thing." It may be that the word of an employee will be doubted. In New York we have the greatest authority obtainable 'giving evidence that the consumers of electricity have their bills increased from 50 ti 103 per cent. The following is a press cutting : " Nevt, York, April 18th.—On the sworn testimony of Professor William Hallock, who occupies the chair of physics at Columbia University, and who is recognized as one of the greatest authorities in the world, the Consolidated Gas Company to-day was convicted of false representation, deceit, viola- tion of the city's demands as to the quality of the gas, and increasing the gas bills of its consumers by means of abnormal pressure through its mains. ' These things have frequently been charged against the trust. To-day they were proved. 'While the legislative investigation has been in progress thel professor of physics has been testing the gas many times daily at the jets in use at the college. His tests showed that the gas furnished by the company rarely came up to the twenty-candlepower standard demanded by the city. More than this, it showed that as the candle power was diminished the pres- sure was increased, with the result that bills must have been increased from 50 to 100 per cent." - Good will surely come out of all this, inasmuch as the people are turning their attention to the necessity of owning all these " public utilities." Chicago has just voted a man into power who, as Mayor, is pledged to such ownership. In San Francisco we are travelling on the 5a,...e lines.</p>
        <p>-11;e—sa-id—n-o-t-hing in the " Examiner " as to the American educational system. Why? Because I could say little in its favour, so I let it alone. I have I hid five children partially educated in the schools here and I think I should know as much about it as those who come here for a day and go ',away thinking they know all about it. Only increase the opportunities to the fullest possible extent, and see that the children of the poor are not forced to carry empty stomachs to school, and you have not much toleam from the United States on the question of education. Several friendly correspondents urge that I seem to find little but fault with the ways and doings of the American people, and question me respecting direct taxation and payment of members. If I can find time I will in the near future deal with these two questions awl with the very important one of Education. There has been, for a long time, a feeling among the Socilliets of the States that the object of their aims seemed to be almost within their grasp. Governor Hoch of Kansas, who is anything but a Socialist, has just said, "In my judgment, Rockfeller —and I men- tion him merely in representative capacity—is doing more to multiply Socialists and make Socialism popular than all the professional propagandists of that fascinat- ing fallacy in the United States are doing." This is undoubtedly true, and the Socialists are quietly accept- ing its truth. The American people have awakened up Ito the 'fact that they must now liberate themselves from the dermas of a too impassioned commercial absorption. They see now that the principles of democracy ere on a trial. It is a burning question. and is agitating pulpit, press, and platform—if our Republican form of Government is at all safe. There are forces at work that seem to be aiming at rooting out the whole foundation, forces that in their utter abondonment to the lust of self care not if the whole structure totters over. Goverment has taken up the question, and its agents are now making searching inquiry into the working of the halt-dozen Trusts that are causing the trouble, and now the pulpit is drawn in and bas through several leading Ministers spoken pretty plainly Rockefeller has given :£20,000 to the missions of the Congregational Church, and the gift involves the question in ethics.: Should a Christian church accept such a gift? It involves something more than a mere , question in ethics. Not a few of America's working- men look upon some half-dozen of the leaders in the manipulation of these Trusts as murderers and thieves, and I will in my next letter when again deal- ing with the Beef Trust, go over its horrible trail of blood. The gift of this money has roused the church. Not that there is anything wrong in taking the money of wealthy men, but the old saying of the receiver being as bad as the thief stand; awkwardly in the way and will not down. Dr. Washington Gladden is general moderator of the Congregational Church in this copntry and he has spoken out in no uncertain voice. He says : "The evidence is damning. The business methods of the Standard Oil are often utterly dishonourable. It has played continually with stacked cards and loaded dice. On the football field or in the prize ring such unsportsmanlike behaviour would be ex crated: This man (Rockfellerl and his company have done more to create these evil social conditions than any score of other agencies." Speaking of the Standard Oil Company and its doings in its national aspect, the Dr. says "It is undeniable that among I the powers which have led the nation into the peril of a conflict between corporate greed and industrial freedom none has been more potent or more ruthless that that which it is now proposed to take into partner- ship in missionary work.' These words bite deep and will no doubt have a wide-reaching effect on the friends of missionary societies.</p>
        <p>I wish to keep my personal feelings entirely clear of this question, merely stating facts. I look at the whole question simply from a wage-earner's point of view. I 'could from this very day's paper quote a lorg article dealing faith the vast nurffber of millions of dollars that Rockefeller has within the past few days gathered into his sack. Where does hall come from? All wealth comes from the land, and is the result of toil, but the worker gets precious little of it. Another minister says :—" I suppose no clear-headed, unbiased reader Of a half-dozen histories, favourable or unfavour- able, describing the rise of the Standard Oil Company, would doubt that the process .of crushing rival con- cerns by which it came to affluence was unspeakably, villainous. For myself I would rather go before God the Father to-night in the parson of the maeslaYer who hae'deliberately killed his one man than in the person of the head Of that company." Rocke- feller has from the pulpit been called "A liar, a cheat, a fraud and a rascal." it is stated that his money is Judas money, and conscience money, and the cartoons depict him with an oil can in hand pouring oil on the road to heaven. He has his defenders ; for has not his money been accepted because, forsooth, "it will enable the missionary board to rescue a great many souls which it would otherwise be unable to reach."</p>
        <p>...... Now let us look at the man himself. Heis a religious man, but he knows how to separate religion from business. For forty yedrs he has been insthe public eye. For at least twenty years he has been a target for all the hot shot the public could let go at him, and yet until now he has never opened his mouth in his defence. This comes through the Rev. Mr. Hutchins who says —" I believe Mr. Rockefeller is an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>eminently Christian man. His private life is ideal." Mr. Hutchins was asked : "If Mr. Rockefeller is the type of Christian that you declare him to be, how is it that he can reconcile his conscience with his business methods as exemplified in the conduct of the Standard Oil Co ?" " I put it to him squarely one day while we were tramping over the mountains," replied the clergyman, "I said to him, Mr. Rookielfer, how is it that you, who have such an ideal home life and who lead a private life that seems above reproach, can be satisfied in conscience with the things the Standard Oil Company does, and can sanction the principles that are represented by it in the business world? Do you find that they are compatible with your moral conceptions and the teachings of your Church?" His reply was this: "I am fully aware that I am adversely criticised for the things ,that the company does. The criticism at times is most severe and it is generally undesthwed. The Standard Oil is not a philanthropy. It is/ not conducted along philanthropic lines. It is a business, as much a busi- ness as that of selling 'harness. Ile company is con- ducted along the lines that are laid down in the business world as being those of to-day. These. lines all tend towards the .centralizaticm idea. By that I mean that businesses of common kinds are in the future to be conducted in a greater way, and on a bigger and broader scale than they have been in the past." _ _</p>
        <p>wife, who, he was informed, had devoted herself during the past few days to the task of making the proceedings of the evening a success. (Applause.Y On that occasion they had to rejoice over the success of the principal team, as well as over the success of the "A "team—those who had succeeded at Leeds and those who had won the,Lancashire Combination championship. The Warrington team at Leeds did remarkably well' 'When he read the accounts of the match, or some account of it, his old-fashioned mind was somewhat startled with the terms he read, and he could not help being reminded of the puzzling terms which used'to be used in connection with the prize fights, when persons "tapped each other's clarets" and "copped it on the bread basket." (Laughter). The first team was a team composed of excellent strong men, fit to do battle not only in football but in other respects. Amongst the team they had a Fish that never went stale; might he , never go stale at all. (Laughter and applause). And I amongst other names which lent themselves to remark he thought they had a Swift and a Naylor. Might he say to all that he reckoned that they were all swift and he knew they were all of them" nailers." They had tried to gain the prize twice before, but that happened which must happen to nearly all aspirants for a great thing—they had failed twice, but had not been dis- couraged. They tried the third time and they succeeded. (Applause.) Force of circumstances had detained him in Warrington instead of being in the House of Commons. Had he been there last night perhaps he might have regretted that the time of his football days had been so long gone by, and he might have thought th had he been younger he should . .</p>
        <p>Thus speaks the great Oil magnate as to the I have been more likely to have joined la the possible way we are likely to get out of the competitive system. ; scrimmage there. (Liughter.) Happily things did not g He offers a small dealer, whether it be in pumping oro so far. Powerful good sense overcame bodily force, retailing the oil, a price. If the party wishes to hold I and he hoped things would go right now, and that for ;some time there would be no more scrimmages in the on to the business he immediately undersells,or makes' 11311 SO. Should there be it might be advisable for some- it irdpossible for the producer to sell, and this is the , one to be brought in with the power, strength and outcome of the saying " business is business." As an alertness possessed by the Warrington football team, te ideal father, let us look at the result of his home assert the power of decency and order. He trusted that, teaching. The Rek Dwight Hillis of Brooklyn ad- what had already been achieved by the club would dressing the American Board of Foreign Missions said: encourage them to go on and achieve further victories. The saddest words that have been spoken in this coupled [with the toast the names.of il generation were those given by,a young man before 're and Mr. He Ashton. (Applause.) he toast haying been suitably honoured Brown University. The young man is to inherit one ; Mr. NEIL in reply, thanked Sir Gilbert 'for his kind'. of the greatest fortunes in this country, ' young Mr. hospitality, which, he was sure, every member of the Rockefeller. Listen to them, "The American Beauty team also very highly appreciated. He thanked Mr.. rose can be produced in all its splendor only by saori- Pierpoint for his kind remarks about the club and its ficing the early buds that grew up around it. The success; the details of the work of the teams be would rose had 1,030 buds, and in order to produce the leave t;le Ir. Ashton. He (the speaker) was a sort of a Tzi re. rt o y - d had been for four ears Cries of American Beauty the gardener goes around it with a o inner working of the club. (was looked knife and-snips 990 in order that all the strength and after by the committee. (Applause.)He had taken a beauty may be forced into one bloam." One wonders great deal of interest in the doings of the club daring if this is the legitimate outcomes of the Darwinian- the past past four years, and it had been a great honour Sp encerian'aheory of the "Survival of the Fittest." indeed to him to have been president. The club seemed In this economic argument this young man brutally ;to have had a spell of good luck. They had for two years been successful in winning the South-West Lancashire tells the working classes that 999 small business men cup, and three times their men had appeared in the final must be snuffed out of existence in order that his for the trophy which then stood in front of him. It was American beauty, the Trust, may be produced, and a great pleasure to bins to be able to say that the club yet Jesus Christ said, "Let the strong bear the burdens , had won the cup during his presidency. It was an of the weak." , excited Warrington when the cup was brought home. as Mr. Carnegie has just given £2,000.000 to be used well as the other evening when the Mayor hommired in providing old-age pensions for college professors in them even more so by specially selecting an evening on the United Seates, Canada, and Newfoundland. Now, which to entertain them. That honour had been further to by their worthy host and hostess that evening it is a well-known fact that pretty well all the colleges added applause)—who lad invited them to partake of their and universities are between:the finger and thumbs of hospitality. No doubt the team thought something of, rich men. It is rare that a. Professor at the Universi- themselves that day. (Laughter). He hoped that the- ties dares to speak on economical questions from a people of Warrington would stick by them next year. Radical or Social standpoint. Nor does the pulpit (Applause.) speak out to a millionaire congregation. The Press Mr. ASHTON also responded. They had not only tried' now states and gives instances where the rich are con- to deserve success, but had achieved it, which was- better still. It was gratifying when foemen worthy of trolling the pulpit supply. Not only is this so, but the their steel,after a very severestruggle as was done after dead band of the rich men is making itself felt. Is the contest at Leeds, admitted that they had been beaten- it wise that educational establishments should accept by the better team of the two. (Applause.) It was the rich man's meney ? How Rockefeller made his also gratifying to find that this season they hadachieved money by almost wading through blood may be learned a dual success by winning not only the Northern Union- by reading " Wealth versus Commonwealth" now in Cup, but the Lancashire,CoMbinaticm. He thanked Sir the Free Library, and I may say that I forwarded Gilbert and hoped he would enjoy health and happinese, some months age to your Free Library "The History for many years to come. (Applause.) of the Carnegie Steel Works." I sent it through a HONOURING SIR GILBERT AND LADY friend, but have not yet heard of its arrival. GREENALL. My next letter will be on the Beef Trust. I shall Mr. A. H. CROSINELD said he had the privilege and most likely be in the very centre of the slaftghtering the very pleasing duty of proposing thehealth of Lady :eards of the Trust, some two thousand miles away Greene'', who had taken an active interest in the arranging of those beautiful surroundings, and of Sir from my home. - - I Gilbert Greenall. (Applause.) He thought it was a very happy idea on the part of Sir Gilbert to entertain the players after their great victory at Leeds, and 'he was sure they all heartily thanked their host and hostess for their bountiful hospitality. (Applause.) Sir Gilbert had referred to H.M.the King as one of the best sports- , men in the land. We in England valued our sportsmen' iand if he might say so it was not theleast because hews a charactenatic sportsman that they felt such a warm , affection for Sir Gilbert arsenal'. (Applause). In the I old days he used to see more of ice:Abell but he was solve' : to say, some few years ago he was attacked by that ve''Y prevalent and dangerous epidemic—golf. Referring to- the final at Leeds Mr. Crosfleld said he never saw a man iwho was ad able to keep Ma head and show his judg- ment, courage and dexterity in a more striking manner than the hero of the match whose name figured on the menu cards that evening. (Applause.) The turning point in the game was when Brooks—who he believed was called the "baby" of the team and who was indeed a very promising baby -so negotiated 'matters, that backea up by Fish the second try of the match' was scored. A finer piece of work than that one, never wisbed to see. (Applause.) While he believed ; Brooks was el years of age, and fl.eured as the baby of the team, a cousin of his (Mr. Crosfleld) who- wars pretty useful at other things—Sir Gilbert knew he was a fairly good shot in the field—Ernest Crostield when 17 years' of age was asked to play for all England, so that it would be seen that in the family they had taken some' little share in the gate they were gathered together !that evening to celebrate. (Applause.) as The tot was received with musical honours hearty cheers being given for Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall. Sir GILBERT Gm:F.:cum, in reply, sai I he thanked sir present for the very kind manner in which the company j had drunk the health of his wife and himself. It had I given them the very greatesE pleasure to be able to• entertain them that evening. There was nothing that appealed to people in the North of England so much as football, and they could look back with the utmost satisfaction upon a most dticeessful season in which their ewn players had upheld the name of Warrington 10 the most popularof all sport—football, in going into sport of any sort a -man had to be fit and ready, and that could it et be accomplished without a great deal of patience, self-denial, and exercise, and he was sure they must all be congratulated in. that neighbourhood in having been so successful. The Duke of Wellingtee used to Gab that the battle of Waterloo WM won on the playing fields of Eton, and that saY ing was as trips to- day as ever. (Applause), In conclusion, he said they all thoroughly appreciated what Warrington could do. (Applause).</p>
        <p>H. WHITHAM, 3.1 v 6th, 1905. 775-14th street, San Francisco.</p>
        <p>THE TWO CAPTAINS. Alderman PIERPOINT submitted the toast of the "Captains of the First and 'A' teams." He believed that both possessed the essential qualificatio-,s wbicl? made good captains. (Applause.) He did not know why he had been asked to submit the toast, except it• was that he was the oldest ex-president of the club. How many years had elapsed since he was in office he did not know, but this he did know that during his term of office the football team played exceedingly well, but although the men tried so hard they never won the cup. Whether that was the fault of the Presidentr the committee or the players, or jointly, he was totally unable to say. (Laughter.) He hoped that when neat they went into the final they would be able to secure in the smartest manner a similar victory to that which was' being celebrated that evening in order that Sir Gilbert Greenall might have an opportunity of inviting them all to be present again to partake of his hospitalitY- (Hear, hear, laughter and applause.) Mr. Ha.Li, 01 briefly returned thanks, remarking that he would rather face 20,000 spectators than make n speech. Mr. BROOKS also bi idly replied. Mr. T. PEMBERTON, in proposing the health of the Mayor of Warrington (Alderman Bolton) remarked that he (the speaker) was the oldest treasurer in Rugby foot- ball. He was sure they` were all proud of the way in, which the Mayor fulfilled the duties of his office. au" were proud to acknowledge the part which he fulfilled in entertaining them. They would like to see him on the football ground occasionally, and he was pleased to take that opportunity to inform the people of Warring' ton that although the Mayor did not watch the foot- ball matches he took a great interest in the team- (Applause.) I The' MAYOR in replying referred to the viatorieS achieve.' by Warrington in the various branches of sport. Sir Gilbert Greenall was never happier than when winning the highest prizes offered for horses. He (the Mazer) had won the highest prize for the production of orchids: Warrington had also cothe to the front in football, gala altogether the town did not take a back sof"- (Applause.) Ho congratulated the club on having done' somethiog for the name of the town. OTHER TOASTS. Mr. A. C. NEIL in proposing " Sports " urged the necessity of physical as well as mental development,: In fact he thought the two were inseparable and that mental development could not be fully achieved without physical development. He urged upon the players,,,the,r desirability of making football a scientific• game mu"- , than a display of strength. Mr. FORSTIAW supported the toast which was heartili" drunk.</p>
        <p>..... Cheers for Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall brought a most enjoyable evening •to a close. ffly During the evening the speechmaking was pleasin an excellent programme bein ?" • interspersed by contributed to by Messrs. L. Aukland, J. Rabbits, I song. Aukland, E. Whitehouse, J. Fish, G. Thomas. and a gle° party consisting of itiessrs. L. Aukland, E. Whiteholleh," C. W. Davenport and F. Scattergood. Mr. F. acted as accompanist.</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and pnblshed by JOTIN WALKICR &amp; CO., at The Examiner General Printint Work 13 gr ewsey-street, and published by them at t e sera; place, and also at The Examiner Oakes, Victoria-roaci WIDNEs : 32, GeOrge-street, ST. HELMS: 3, M'° Street, FIARLESToWN; Church-street, R[7=012'4,14° 27th. 190%</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FOOTBALL FETE AT WALTON.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL FETE AT WALTON.</p>
        <p>VICTORIOUS TEAMS ENJOY SIR</p>
        <p>GILBERT GREENALL'S HOSPITALITY. The Warrington Football Club's recent succeses in the Northern Union Cup Competition and the Lancashire Combination were further celebrated on Tuesday evening, when the victorious teams, with officials and guests, to the total number of 70 were entertained to dinner by Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall at Walton Park. The invitation was issued before the cup-final at Leeds, and " win or lose" the War- rington men were assured of Sir Gilbert's hospitality. As we all know they won the cup in handsome style, and could therefore attend at Sir Gilberts' hospit- able board with minds and consciences at ease. In their, own sphere the junior players had been equally successful, and they were heartily entitled to accompany the cup-holders to Walton. The host and hostess of the evening had gone to considerable trouble iikorder to ensure the comfort and happiness of their guests, and we may say here that the unqualified satisfaction of all present testifies to their success in this direction. A portion of the spacious and airy Riding School had been partitioned off,floored, illumin- ated and decorated for the occasion, and very bright and attractive it looked. Everywhere the club coloufs,primrose and blue,met the eye, being embodied in the drapery which graced the walls, while topical phrases such as "We are not down-hearted," Combination brings success," " Well played Hallam," " Here's better luck to you Preston," "We are proud of you, Fish," and Bravo, Brooks," coupled with the names of many other players, were conspicuously displayed round the room in blue and gold. Rugs, carpets, flowers and furniture contributed their share to a scene of comfort and cheerfulness. The teams and officials arrived in good time per omnibus, and after a brief I inspection of Sir Gilbert's a circus" for breaking in young horses, and other interesting appliances, all adjourned to the dining hall. Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall 'personally received the guests, and it was , 'amusing to note that even the baronet knew Fish by name and gave hm a special greeting. Lady Greenail was unfortunately unable to stay throughout the pro- ceedings but she was in evidence for some , time, looking very striking in a rich brown velvet costume. Sir Gilbert Greenall, of course, presided over the principal table, conspicuous in the centre of which was the massive Northern Union Challenge Cup. Messrs. Ashton and Pemberton were at the head of the other tables, and the guests included the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, the Mayor of Warrington, Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P., Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, Mr. John White, Mr. W. Bainbridge, Mr. W. R. Neil (president of the club), Mr. Alexander Neil, Mr. John Neil, Alderman William Pierpoint, Coancillor Forshaw, Mr. Thomas Pemberton (hon. treasurer),Mr. Henry Ashton (hon. secretary), Messrs. T. Wallington and W. Webster (hon. auditors), com- mittee, lifessr4. T. Davies, T. Bowers, T. Locker, W. Hewitt, W. Nevins, and T. Barnes, sen. All the players were present with the exception of F. Stingers, D. Davies and J. Davies, who had returned to their homes in South Wales. • The menu cards wertioost ingenious, and by the I majority of those present they. will be retained as mementos of an interesting}occasion,Each card had been hand-painted in colours by Miss Mutter, of Aswarby, Grantham,and had for a back-ground a football, which was surrounded by a primrose and blue scroll, the whole being surmounted by a life-like fish in honour of Warrington's crack threequarter. The menu itself was written on the brown surface of the football, and was as follows : MENU. Soup: Thick of-tail. Fish: Dee salmon, lobster sauce. Entrees:. Fricassee of chicken and mushrooms. Joints: Roast beef and roast lamb. Vegetables: Peas and new potatoes. Sweets: Strawberry,- and lemon cream, wine jelly. Cheese and watercress, dessert.</p>
        <p>After dinner, and before the atmosphere had become"chargecl with the fumes of cigars, Mr. J. Birtles took a couple of flash-light photographs of the assembly. Then, until after eleven o'clock, the even- ing was spent in conviviality. The customary loyal toasts were honoured, on the proposition of the CHAIRMAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TOAST OF THE EVENING.</head>
        <p>THE TOAST OF THE EVENING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Friii-porsT, M.P., sn-bin-itt—eri 71;e—r-t:(Ta-st of the evening "The Warrington Football Club." Although the ideas of football playing had long since vanished from him, he still felt a sympathy for a game which encouraged the young men of England to display their hardihood, self-sacrifice and courage. He congratu- lated all present on being at the hospitable board of Sir Gilbert Greenall under the auspices of his excellent</p>
      </div>
