        <p>Amid all the ten,c excitement of the scene in Parlia- Smart Fine for Obscene Language. tnent on Monday cveniag there was more than one At Newton-le-Willows, William Watson was on laughable incident. To an Irishman, however, the Thursday limed 40.4. and costs for using obscene credit naturally goes for the best "hit." When the 1„.,4.f1age. . 'uproar was at the highest one of the Nationalist Death of Mrs Henry Broadhurst. , members called out in stentorian tones: "order in the " compound! "—and the ironical laughter which followed Widespread regret was aroused in Liecester on was tremendous. Wednesday on receipt of the news of the death of Mrs. * * * Broadhurst, wife of Mr. Henry Braadhurst M.P., the The completion ot the tramway service to Victoria senior member for the Borough. She was married to Square, Stockton Heath, will re-awaken the old Mr. Broadhurst in 1860. Iquestion of the fare to be paid by those making the i "PREMIER" CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade, complete journey. Many of the Heathens, we hear, : unsurpassed.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., ; 'threaten to boycott the cars rather than pay lid., and it is easy to picture portly and perspiring businessmen ~,' making their way to town on foot, at the same time , casting envious glances at less conscientious ' individuals comfortably seated in the cars. Man Warrington ** * Warrington.—Advt. . Betting Prosecution. ) to , , y people appeared to be puzzled know why flap were flying from buildings and , •,. principal works on Wednesday. The reason was that , ~..., , Empire Day" was being celebrated—in a very At N -1 W*ll Thursday, Frank Dal Newton-le-Willows, e- i ows, one, a well-known bookmaker, was fined £5 and costs in two 1 cases for street betting, and Henry Wilson, a local footballer, and John H. Parkinson were fined 20s. and costs for aiding and abetting, on the 9th and 10thinst. 1 Asw for Cumertgowitm's Hroa-CLA.ss MINERAL i vr ATMS. Advt. modest way it is true. It is well that while glorying , Boxing Contest at Sheffied. in our Empire we should also realise its responsi- An important boxing contest was decided at Sheffield bities, and if Empire Day encoarges this phase of on Monday night between " Cockney " Cohen, of imperial thought it will . serve a truly worthy Leeds, and Tommy Burns of Widnes, for £l7O. The Purpose . . , lads met at Bst. 81b. give and take 21b and after going i the full 15 rounds Cohen wat awarded the verdict on po . i inta. , . • . Death of Mr. Peter Chrimes. i We regret to announce that Mr. Peter Chrimes, licenoee of the Roebuck Hotel, Bridge-street, died on Thursday. The deceased gentleman, who had been jailing for a longtime, was the son of the 'late Mr. lJoseph Chrimes who was Mayor of the Borough in 1885-6-7. He had for many years been treasurer of the Old Folks' Treat Committee. Child Severely Burnt. • Arthur Berry, aged 16 months, 21, Hale-street, was admitted to the Warrington Infirmary on Tuesday suffering from severe burns about the legs and arms. His injuries were dressed and be is progressing favourably. It is surmised that he fell into a lime pit in the neighbourhood of his parents house. UNDER THE CLOCK fO/ Good Boors.—Advt. The George Hotel Licence. Notice is given in our advertising columns that all persons claiming to be interestedin the licence of the "George and Dragon " hotel (other than the liceensee and registered owners of the premises) are required to 'send to the Compensation Authority notice of their claims before 'Tune 19th, for the purpose of enabling the Authority to ascertain', in manner provided by the Licensing Rules, 1904, the persons entitled to com- pensation under the Act .of 1904. , ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLAM, AIMERAL WATERS.—AdYt. • Music in the Park. In the 'presence of a large concourse of tewnapeople Mr. T. J. Down's military band rendered selections in tlut - tark •OH •-Thrirsday evening. The admirable rendering of an' agreeably varied programme, under Mr. Arthur Down's ' able cqnductorship, was hignly appreciated. The' pimes included the hymn " Even-tide " (Mottle) arranged for military , bands by the late Mr. T. J. Down. ,By the kind permission of Lieut. Colonel Edeleten , and officers the Volunt4r Band will play the follow- ing programme on Thursday next at 7.30 p.m: in the park. Polka march, " Viens penpoule," C. Kiefert ; selection, "The Mikado," Sullivan; romance. " Japonaise," M. Feline; selection, "Irish Melodies," Hume ; duet, "Ida and Dot," (polka) Losey; selection, "A Hot Time," T. Neat; " God save the King." Bandmaver J. T. Richardson, , PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. The New Dbputy Town Clerk. A Meeting of the General Purposes Committee of the Corporation was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening, the Mayor in the 'chair, to anpoint,a Deputy Town Clerk to succeed Mr. Stanley Wilson, who has been selected as Town Clerk of Leigh. There was a good attendance of members. The seven candidates selected out of a large number of applicants appeared before the committee, and each, in turn, was personally interviewed by the committee. The final selection rested between Mr. A. T. Hallaway, who has been seven years in the town clerk's office at Carlisle, and Mr. Roger.Pierpoint, 808 of Alderman Pierpoint, who is in private practice in Warrington. The' voting was in favour of Mr. Hallsway,- who received 14 votes against 12 recorded for Mr. Pierpoint. The salary will commence at £l7s,,and.rise by annual increments of £25 to a maximum of £250. The new deptity will commence his duties in about a month. . PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK. FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. ' , I , Conservative -Association Annual Meeting. ' The annual meeting of the Conservative Association was held at the Conservative Club on Monday evening, Sir Gilbert °Toenail, Bart., presiding over a fair, attendance.—Before the ordinary business, the Chair- man proposed :—" That the members of the Warring- ton Conservative Association desire to place on record their deep sense of the irreparable loss they have sus- tained by the death of Alderman T. H. Sutton, and express their appreciation of the very valuable services rendered by him as honorary secretary of this Associa- tion for many years." (Hear, hear.) It was hardly necessary for him to say anything about the deceased gentleman, as those present had known him much longer than he (the Chairman) had done. They knew how deeply interested he was in the cause of Conser- vatism in Warrington, and that he was always willing to de all he could for it. He felt that in Alderman Sutton's death they had lost a very sincere and valued friend. (Applause.) The motion was seconded by Mr. John White, sup)aorted by Mr. R. Pierpoint, M.P. and carried. The officers of the Association were heartily thanked for their past ser- vices. The following officers were appointed t— president,Sir Gilbert Greenall ; vice presidents,Messrs J. C. Parr and R.Pierpoint, M.P.; hon. sec. Councillor Fulton Smith; hon. treasurer,' Alderman 'Bennett; delegates to the National Union, Aldermen Francomb and Bennett. Mr. R. Piet-point addressed the meeting at length, mainly on the fiscal question, and at the close was heartily thanked for his presence, as was also the chairman for his services. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE Toxic Cures Nervousness. —Advt.-</p>
