        <p>'LOCAL NEWS. Coming Events. Sir Gilbart Greenall, Bart., is announced to preside Over the anntild meeting of the Warrington and District Branch of the N.S.P.C,C., which will be held at the Town Hall on Thursday next. The preacher at Wycliffe Church to-morrow will ha' Ihe Rev. J. chater, Southport. • To-morrow the Rev. W. Dannico will 'preach at the Baptist Church, Golbtrne-street. In the morning his subject will be "Is there a God: If so can we know Mm?' and in the evening" The scepticism of Pilate: 'What is truth?" "PREMIER" CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge 'at., Warrington.— Advt. • '1 'Passive Resistance Sale. , The sale of goods seized under the distress warrant • 'issued some tirde ago against the Rev. J. S. Hughes, Rev. Herbert Dunnico, and Mr. Lloyd Jones will take ,Place at Stockton Heath next week. Tostly Revenge. At the Warrington Police Court yesterday (Friday), 7 W. Jackson, 13, Crosley-street, was fined 10s. and 'Costs and in addition ordered to pay 128. for ("wag 'wilful damage to a tent the property of Mr. Haig: Haig said he was holding a show on the Winwick- '• road fairground. Defendant demanded to be admitted • free and on being refused ripped the tent in seven or : eight places. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE.CLOCK.BRIDGR-STREET.—Advt. bisastrous Fire at Stockton Heath. . A disastrous stable fire occurred at Stockton Heath in the early hours of Wednesday morning. Shortly after one o'clock information was conveyed to the War- t /ington Fire Brigade that a fire had broken out in the Premises occupied by Mr. John Taylor, greengrocer, ''n Walton-road. The brigade were quickly on the scene under the command of Sergeant H. 'Cook. ' slid the fire was extinguished by means of hydrants. -The stables were however gutted and the large stock vhich they contained destroyed. A particularly sad feature of the outbreak was that three horses, a pony, *two donkeys and a dog were burned to death. The ; • less; which is considerable, is only partly covered by insurance. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC Creates It fine Appetite.—Advt. Progressive Labour League and the Scenes in Parliament. A largely-attended meeting of the Warrington Pro- ,: gressive Labour League was held at the Liberal Club Tuesday evening. Mr. J. Howes presided. On the . motion of the Rev. H. Dunnico, seconded by Mr. J. • Morton, the following resolution was passed "That • itrhie meeting of the Warrington Progressive Labour "e, ague views with regret the unfortunate proceedings n r • • lat took place in the House of Commons on Monday. , It considers the evasive, dishonest tactica of Mr. Bal- f"nr responsible for such proceedings. It is of opinion `that such conduct ill becomes the Premier of this nation that it is a direct violiation of the honourable ; traditions of British public and Parliamentary life. It • :tenders to Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman its sincere , thanks for his prompt action in defence of the sanctity "If public pledges given to the nation by leading states- ' It was decided to hold a series of opon-air Ineetings at an early date. "PoEuton." CYCLES at IT guineas with change ilDeed gear &amp;Al, unequalled.—J. S. GARLAND, 31 to Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. , Warrington Albions A.F.C. The annual meeting of the above club was held a -the Coach and Horses on Tuesday evening. The chair ' l'fas occupied by the President (Mr. George Bowman). -T, ne secretary's report, a very gratifying one, was Passed as read. During the past season the first • learn had been very successful, having won the First diviaion of the Warrington and District League out- -ight, and also becoming joint holders' of the • ~ ~bserver " Cup after a replayed tie and extra time. e Treasurer's report, wjlich showed a balance in : -"•,elld of over -..C20 was passed. Votes of thanks to the • tVesident, Vice-presidents, and officials were given for jeir past services. The following are the officials '..;;•ected for next season