e right to act !upto my convictions." ' "Very well," I said, retaining her hand. that you may disagree if you choose." "What is she like personally?" "The woman I love is—you have no idea how nice, and never will know her. Her figare has all the gracefulness of young womanh Aid.. Nothing mars the beauty of har face, whose reposefulness is easily awakened into =linty by human interest. But, myself, I like to watch for the tenderness that comes into her eves when looking into some other face." " You think all that." Her eyes looked into mine, then fell before my steady glance. "Of course," she said, "if you are in love, that must be the case. You cannot help it." " Do not condole with me," I said," for my mood is not fitting. Tell me, rather, what she ought to say to me." "But who is she ; what is her name ; and where does she live ? " " Her name," I said, " is Phyllis, and she lives in my heart. I want to marry her, so that she can live in my house, too ; and if she is the delightful woman I take her to be (it is impossible for me to be wrong), why, she will say yes or no—either meaning yes—and marry:me Iron." " I suppose," she said, " I could not well reform your mind now if I wished ?" "You could no-.," I said ; "you mast regard that as a hopeless task and give way." "I seem to have no choice," Phyllii said. " You haven't," I assured her • " nor I either. 'Pis Love's choice, which is most arbitrary." . Phyllis's head was so perilously close at the moment the fact made her an ac,e.essory, so that she could not blame me for what happened. , It was in that way she consented to marry me. I certainly never heard her " Yes" at the altar, but so far she has not denied that she said it. The other day she came into my den. If the soft rustle of her skirts had not disturbed my thoughts, her sitting down on the arm of my chair would. The odour of violets mingled with that of tobacco. "Put your pipe down," she commanded; then put her arm round my neck and deliberately kissed me on the lips. "I often wished to do that before," she calmly remarked—" to kiss you myself"; then mile° tively, "and tobacco adds a piquancy to it." Phyllis is still the most delightful of her sex. '(These articles originatly appeared in the "Londoa Rcho. ")</p>
